export const MODULE_DOCS: Record<string, { title: string; html: string }> = {"1.1":{"title":"Software Lifecycle","html":"\n<h1>Module 1.1: Software Lifecycle</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 1 · Week 1 · Environment: browser DevTools console</strong></p>\n\n<h2>SLO Focus</h2>\n<p>- <strong>Introduces SLO-1</strong> (software life-cycle): primary artifact A1.1.</p>\n<p>- Reinforces <strong>SLO-4</strong> (process as algorithm).</p>\n\n<h2>Module Goal</h2>\n<p>Students understand what programming is and where it sits in a software</p>\n<p>project's lifecycle: the four framework activities, the umbrella activities</p>\n<p>that run alongside them, task sets, SDLC methodologies and the four Ps, and</p>\n<p>how to judge whether a prescriptive process model fits a given project.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>None. First module of the course.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Can name the four framework activities and classify a task into the correct</p>\n<p>one; can name umbrella activities; can write a task set; can explain SDLC</p>\n<p>methodologies and the four Ps; can judge whether a prescriptive process</p>\n<p>model fits a given project; can open the browser console and run</p>\n<p><code>console.log</code>.</p>\n\n<h2>Note</h2>\n<p>Book §1.1 Software Lifecycle. Ships as a 23-lesson sequence. Full Numbered</p>\n<p>Lesson List has not yet been spec'd out per <code>sub-module-spec-conventions.md</code>.</p>\n\n<h3>⚠ Folder numbers do NOT match title numbers in this module</h3>\n\n<p>Resequenced 2026-08-16 <strong>by title only.</strong> Lesson order comes from the number</p>\n<p>in <code>lesson.json.title</code> (<code>parseNumberedIdFromTitle</code>, <code>lib/curriculum.ts</code>);</p>\n<p>student progress hangs off the folder name via <code>lesson_state.lesson_id</code>. So</p>\n<p>the titles were renumbered and **every folder name was left exactly as it</p>\n<p>was**, which is what kept 16 existing progress rows intact: 15 of them on</p>\n<p><code>1-1-4-sdlc-overview</code>.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Do not \"tidy\" the folder names to match the titles.</strong> Renaming a folder</p>\n<p>orphans real student work. <code>BOOK-TO-MODULE.md</code> §7.3 is explicit on this:</p>\n<p>renaming a title is safe, renaming a folder is not.</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Order</th><th>Folder</th><th>Was</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1.1.3</td><td>`1-1-3-first-statement`</td><td>1.1.6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.1.4</td><td>`1-1-4-sdlc-overview`</td><td>1.1.4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.1.6</td><td>`1-1-6-reading-four-phases`</td><td>1.1.8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.1.7</td><td>`1-1-7-classify-the-task`</td><td>1.1.9</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.1.8</td><td>`1-1-8-example-greeting-phases`</td><td>1.1.10</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.1.9</td><td>`1-1-3-a1-1-sdlc-writeup`</td><td>1.1.3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.1.10–1.1.22</td><td>`1-1-11-…` … `1-1-23-…`</td><td>each shifted down one</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.1.23</td><td>`1-1-23-a3-3-unit-quiz`</td><td>1.1.7</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h3>What the resequence fixed</h3>\n\n<p>1. <strong>The unit quiz sat at position 7 of 23</strong>, assessing material taught in</p>\n<p>   lessons 8–21. It is now last.</p>\n<p>2. <strong>A1.1.1 (the SDLC writeup) sat at position 3</strong>, before the Four Phases</p>\n<p>   reading that teaches what it asks for. It now follows that reading and</p>\n<p>   its worked example.</p>\n<p>3. <strong>The two console labs were inverted.</strong> <code>1-1-3-first-statement</code> (run five</p>\n<p>   ready-made statements, change two) is gentler than <code>1-1-4-sdlc-overview</code></p>\n<p>   (write six <code>console.log</code>s from scratch), and came second. Gentle first now.</p>\n\n<p>Nine cross-references of the form `<code>Read before attempting </code>1.1.N Title<code> </code>`</p>\n<p>were also repaired. They had been stale since before this pass: the numbers</p>\n<p>disagreed with the titles printed beside them, e.g. \"`1.1.8 Name That</p>\n<p>Umbrella`\" when 1.1.8 was the Four Phases reading. They are now resolved by</p>\n<p>title, which is the half that stayed reliable.</p>\n\n<h3>JS basics here are shCode's own: the book has none</h3>\n\n<p><code>1-1-2-reading-console-log</code> and <code>1-1-4-sdlc-overview</code> teach <code>console.log</code>,</p>\n<p>strings, booleans and sequential execution. **Book §1.1 contains none of</p>\n<p>this**: it is purely the software lifecycle (zero occurrences of boolean,</p>\n<p>integer, string or sequential; <code>console.log</code> appears only inside two worked</p>\n<p>examples). This material is deliberate week-one scaffolding so day one has</p>\n<p>something runnable, per <code>curriculum-plan.md</code> §1.1's in-class activities.</p>\n\n<p>Two consequences. <strong>A resync must not delete these as \"not in the book\".</strong></p>\n<p>And the §1.1 deck cannot cover them, so a teacher presenting from slides</p>\n<p>alone will reach the console labs having introduced no JavaScript: logged</p>\n<p>as a book-side gap in <code>BOOK-TO-MODULE.md</code> §8.</p>\n\n\n<p>Points audit (2026-08-13): only <code>1.1.4</code> (<code>sdlc-overview</code>, a console/regex lab)</p>\n<p>was a genuine no-points violation: zeroed. <code>1.1.16</code>, <code>1.1.19</code>, <code>1.1.22</code>, and</p>\n<p><code>1.1.23</code> are AI-graded written assignments correctly carrying nonzero</p>\n<p><code>aiGrader.rubric[].points</code> per <code>written-assignment-conventions.md</code>'s</p>\n<p>points-weighted rubric shape, not a bug. Note that written assignments</p>\n<p>(unlike this module's 3 console labs) are <strong>not</strong> gated at 100%:</p>\n<p><code>components/WrittenGrader.tsx</code> allows partial credit (≥70% of rubric points</p>\n<p>for the <code>1</code>-per-item shape used here; <code>1-1-3-a1-1-sdlc-writeup</code> and</p>\n<p><code>1-1-23-a3-3-unit-quiz</code> use the <code>0</code>-per-item shape instead, gated at a bare</p>\n<p>majority of criteria). See <code>written-assignment-conventions.md</code> §4.</p>\n"},"1.2":{"title":"Variables and Data Types","html":"\n<h1>Module 1.2: Variables and Data Types</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 1 · Week 2 · Environment: browser DevTools console</strong></p>\n\n<h2>SLO Focus</h2>\n<p>- Reinforces <strong>SLO-1</strong>; introduces <strong>SLO-2</strong> (structured, conventional code).</p>\n\n<h2>Module Goal</h2>\n<p>Students declare variables with <code>let</code>/<code>const</code>, name the eight data types and</p>\n<p>give an example of each, explain what \"dynamically typed\" means, use <code>typeof</code></p>\n<p>to check what a value actually is, and tell <code>null</code> from <code>undefined</code>.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Module 1.1 (Foundations) complete.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Students can declare variables, name and recognise every type in the</p>\n<p>language, check a value's type at runtime, and write a description sentence</p>\n<p>with a template literal.</p>\n\n<h2>Note</h2>\n<p>Book §1.2 Variables and Data Types (568 lines). Ships as a 30-lesson</p>\n<p>sequence, matching module 1.1's density (23 lessons over 253 book lines).</p>\n\n<h3>Ordered to follow the book's sections</h3>\n\n<p>Resequenced 2026-08-16 so the module walks book §1.2's own subsections in</p>\n<p>order: types intro, Number, BigInt, String, Boolean, <code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code>,</p>\n<p>Objects and Symbols, <code>typeof</code>, Summary, with the plan-derived lessons placed</p>\n<p>beside the book section they belong to rather than bolted on the front:</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Plan lesson</th><th>Sits with</th></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-2-2-reading-let-const`</td><td>first, at 1.2.2: the book assumes `let` already exists</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-2-7-reading-operators-coercion`</td><td>book §1.2.1 Number</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-2-14-reading-string-methods`</td><td>book §1.2.3 String</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-2-16-lab-operators-check`</td><td>after arithmetic and comparison are taught</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-2-26-lab-variables-types-check`</td><td>after §1.2.8 `typeof`</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p><strong>This fixed a real prerequisite violation.</strong></p>\n<p><code>1-2-26-lab-variables-types-check</code> has <code>typeof</code> in a graded requirement and</p>\n<p>used to sit at position 5: seventeen lessons before the <code>typeof</code> reading.</p>\n<p><code>1-2-5-lab-change-the-type</code> had the same problem at position 9.</p>\n\n<p><code>1-2-5</code> now follows the <code>typeof</code> reading, at 1.2.26. <code>1-2-9</code> stays beside the</p>\n<p>dynamic-typing reading it demonstrates, at 1.2.5, and **hands the student the</p>\n<p>single line of <code>typeof</code> it needs** in its own step instructions: the way</p>\n<p><code>1-1-4-sdlc-overview</code> teaches <code>console.log</code>. That mirrors the book, which uses</p>\n<p><code>typeof</code> in its §1.2.5 and §1.2.6 Try It Nows and only defines it at §1.2.8.</p>\n\n<h3>⚠ Folder numbers do NOT match title numbers in this module</h3>\n\n<p>Reordered <strong>by title only.</strong> Order comes from the number in</p>\n<p><code>lesson.json.title</code>; student progress hangs off the folder name via</p>\n<p><code>lesson_state.lesson_id</code>. <code>1-2-1-slides</code>, <code>1-2-26-lab-variables-types-check</code></p>\n<p>and <code>1-2-16-lab-operators-check</code> carry 19 progress rows between them, so every</p>\n<p>folder name was left exactly as it was.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Do not \"tidy\" the folder names to match the titles</strong>: renaming a folder</p>\n<p>orphans real student work. See <code>BOOK-TO-MODULE.md</code> §7.3.</p>\n\n<h3>Lesson order</h3>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>#</th><th>Lesson</th><th>Book</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.1</td><td>`1-2-1-slides`</td><td>:</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.2</td><td>`1-2-2-reading-let-const`</td><td>plan</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.3</td><td>`1-2-3-reading-every-value-has-a-type`</td><td>§1.2 intro</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.4</td><td>`1-2-4-reading-dynamically-typed`</td><td>§1.2 intro Context Pause</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.5</td><td>`1-2-5-lab-change-the-type`</td><td>§1.2 intro: teaches `typeof` inline</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.6</td><td>`1-2-6-reading-number`</td><td>§1.2.1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.7</td><td>`1-2-7-reading-operators-coercion`</td><td>plan</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.8</td><td>`1-2-8-reading-special-numeric-values`</td><td>§1.2.1 **Def 1.2.1**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.9</td><td>`1-2-9-example-nan-is-sticky`</td><td>§1.2.1 **Example 1.2.1**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.10</td><td>`1-2-10-lab-predict-the-number`</td><td>§1.2.1 Try It Now 1.2.1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.11</td><td>`1-2-11-reading-bigint`</td><td>§1.2.2 **Def 1.2.2**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.12</td><td>`1-2-12-reading-strings-and-quotes`</td><td>§1.2.3 **Def 1.2.3**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.13</td><td>`1-2-13-reading-template-literals`</td><td>§1.2.3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.14</td><td>`1-2-14-reading-string-methods`</td><td>plan</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.15</td><td>`1-2-15-lab-greeting-with-backticks`</td><td>§1.2.3 Try It Now 1.2.3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.16</td><td>`1-2-16-lab-operators-check`</td><td>plan</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.17</td><td>`1-2-17-reading-boolean`</td><td>§1.2.4 **Def 1.2.4**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.18</td><td>`1-2-18-example-comparisons-make-booleans`</td><td>§1.2.4 Try It Now 1.2.4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.19</td><td>`1-2-19-reading-null`</td><td>§1.2.5 **Def 1.2.5**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.20</td><td>`1-2-20-reading-undefined`</td><td>§1.2.6 **Def 1.2.6**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.21</td><td>`1-2-21-null-vs-undefined`</td><td>PS 1.2.3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.22</td><td>`1-2-22-reading-primitive-vs-object`</td><td>§1.2.7 **Def 1.2.7**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.23</td><td>`1-2-23-reading-typeof`</td><td>§1.2.8 **Def 1.2.8**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.24</td><td>`1-2-24-example-typeof-surprises`</td><td>§1.2.8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.25</td><td>`1-2-25-lab-typeof-round-up`</td><td>§1.2.8 Try It Now 1.2.8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.26</td><td>`1-2-26-lab-variables-types-check`</td><td>plan: consolidates `let`/`const` + `typeof`</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.27</td><td>`1-2-27-reading-the-seven-primitives`</td><td>§1.2 Summary</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.28</td><td>`1-2-28-a1-2-1-fix-ten-declarations`</td><td>**A1.2.1**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.29</td><td>`1-2-29-a1-2-2-describe-an-object`</td><td>**A1.2.2**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2.30</td><td>`1-2-31-unit-quiz`</td><td>PS 1.2.1–1.2.10</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Authoring notes</h3>\n\n<p>- <strong><code>symbol</code> is named but not taught.</strong> The book introduces it in §1.2.7 and</p>\n<p>  defers it to the objects chapter; 1.2.22 does the same.</p>\n<p>- <strong><code>typeof null</code> is taught as a bug, twice</strong>, once in passing at 1.2.19 and</p>\n<p>  once properly at 1.2.24, because it is the single most common way a</p>\n<p>  beginner's type check goes wrong.</p>\n<p>- <strong>Nothing here teaches conditionals.</strong> 1.2.18's comparisons produce</p>\n<p>  booleans and stop there; <code>if</code> belongs to §2.1.</p>\n<p>- <strong>1.2.5 is the one lesson that runs ahead of its material</strong>, and does so</p>\n<p>  deliberately. It needs <code>typeof</code>, which the book keeps for its last</p>\n<p>  subsection, so the lab teaches the single line it needs in its own step</p>\n<p>  instructions and points forward to 1.2.23. The alternative: moving it to</p>\n<p>  position 26: was tried and reverted: it landed as the third <code>typeof</code> drill</p>\n<p>  in a row and stopped demonstrating dynamic typing at all.</p>\n<p>- The AP CSP binary-numbers discussion in <code>curriculum-plan.md</code> §1.2 has a</p>\n<p>  worksheet at <code>curriculum/resources/1.1.2_binary-worksheet.md</code> and no lesson</p>\n<p>  slot. It is a teacher-led in-class activity, deliberately not in-app.</p>\n<p>- <strong>1.2.28's r5 accepts two spellings on purpose.</strong> The course dropped</p>\n<p>  <code>UPPER_SNAKE_CASE</code> on 2026-09-19 — every value is camelCase and <code>const</code> alone</p>\n<p>  says <em>this never moves</em> (1.3.2 teaches it; <code>class-style-guide.md</code> records it).</p>\n<p>  This module ran in week 4 under the old rule, so r5 reads</p>\n<p>  <code>const\\s+(?:maxStudents|MAX_STUDENTS)\\s<em>=\\s</em>[\\d.]</code> and a paper already handed</p>\n<p>  in still scores 10/10, verified through <code>lib/grader.ts</code>. Do not collapse the</p>\n<p>  alternation: it is a grandfather for submitted work. The starter's own</p>\n<p>  declaration is <code>let maxStudents = 30;</code>, so the lesson still grades something.</p>\n"},"1.3":{"title":"Documentation and Coding Conventions","html":"\n<h1>Module 1.3: Documentation and Coding Conventions</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 1 · Week 3 · Environment: browser DevTools console</strong></p>\n\n<h2>SLO Focus</h2>\n<p>- Reinforces <strong>SLO-2</strong>: structured, conventional, readable code.</p>\n\n<h2>Module Goal</h2>\n<p>Students choose clear descriptive names, explain why reusing a variable for</p>\n<p>different purposes makes code harder to debug, follow the class style guide</p>\n<p>for naming and layout, comment what is not obvious, and write a README.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Module 1.2 (Variables and Data Types) complete.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Students write readable, consistently-styled, documented code. **The class</p>\n<p>style guide (<code>curriculum/resources/class-style-guide.md</code>) is handed out this</p>\n<p>week and is the grading rubric for every code assignment after it.**</p>\n\n<h2>Note</h2>\n<p>Book §1.3 Documentation and Coding Conventions (229 lines). Ships as a</p>\n<p>20-lesson sequence, matching module 1.1's density.</p>\n\n<h3>The book covers less than this module does</h3>\n\n<p>Book §1.3 has only <strong>two</strong> subsections: 1.3.1 Naming Variables Well and</p>\n<p>1.3.2 Reuse or Create? Comments, indentation, spacing, braces and READMEs are</p>\n<p><strong>not in the book at all</strong>; they come from <code>curriculum-plan.md</code> §1.3 and the</p>\n<p>class style guide. So this module is deliberately wider than its book</p>\n<p>section, and that is not drift: it is the plan's own scope.</p>\n\n<p>Numbering runs in two blocks for the same reason as module 1.2: <code>1.3.1</code>–<code>1.3.5</code></p>\n<p>were built earlier (slides, camelCase, layout, comments, and A1.3.2), and</p>\n<p><code>1.3.6</code>–<code>1.3.20</code> were appended 2026-08-16 rather than interleaved. No 1.3</p>\n<p>lesson currently carries <code>lesson_state</code> rows, but appending keeps folder ids</p>\n<p>stable for free, and the order still reads: conventions first, then the</p>\n<p>book's depth on naming, then reuse, then the README and A1.3.1.</p>\n\n<h3>Lesson list</h3>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Lesson</th><th>Type</th><th>Source</th></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-3-1-slides`</td><td>slides</td><td>placeholder; no deck built yet</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-3-2-reading-camelcase`</td><td>reading</td><td>style guide: camelCase</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-3-3-reading-code-layout`</td><td>reading</td><td>style guide: indentation, spacing</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-3-4-reading-comments`</td><td>reading</td><td>style guide: `//` and `/* */`</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-3-5-why-documentation`</td><td>reading</td><td>why documentation matters (A1.3.2 was planned here and never built)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-3-6-reading-human-readable-names`</td><td>reading</td><td>§1.3.1: **Definition 1.3.1** rule 1 + Context Pause</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-3-7-reading-avoid-abbreviations`</td><td>reading</td><td>§1.3.1: rule 2, plus the loop-counter exception</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-3-8-reading-descriptive-but-concise`</td><td>reading</td><td>§1.3.1: rule 3, both ditches</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-3-9-reading-agree-on-terms`</td><td>reading</td><td>§1.3.1: rule 4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-3-10-example-good-vs-bad-names`</td><td>example</td><td>§1.3.1 **Example 1.3.1**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-3-11-lab-rename-the-mystery-variables`</td><td>console lab</td><td>PS 1.3.4 + PS 1.3.7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-3-12-rate-these-names`</td><td>assignment</td><td>Try It Now 1.3.1 + PS 1.3.6 + PS 1.3.9</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-3-13-reading-reuse-or-create`</td><td>reading</td><td>§1.3.2: the labelled-box analogy</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-3-14-reading-why-reuse-costs-you`</td><td>reading</td><td>§1.3.2 Insight Note + PS 1.3.5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-3-15-example-one-variable-three-meanings`</td><td>example</td><td>Try It Now 1.3.2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-3-16-lab-split-the-reused-variable`</td><td>console lab</td><td>Try It Now 1.3.2, extended to four purposes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-3-17-team-vocabulary`</td><td>assignment</td><td>PS 1.3.3 + PS 1.3.8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-3-18-reading-what-a-readme-is`</td><td>reading</td><td>style guide: README requirement</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-3-19-a1-3-1-document-a-messy-program`</td><td>console lab</td><td>**A1.3.1**: renames, spacing, comments, README</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-3-20-video-names-are-for-readers`</td><td>video</td><td>names are for readers</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-3-21-a1-3-3-unit-quiz`</td><td>assignment</td><td>**A1.3.3**</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>Two of the plan's three assignments exist: <strong>A1.3.1</strong> at 1.3.19 and</p>\n<p><strong>A1.3.3</strong> at 1.3.21. <strong>A1.3.2 was never built.</strong> The plan plots it at 1.3.5,</p>\n<p>but that lesson is a reading -- <code>type: lesson</code>, <code>preview: reading</code>, no</p>\n<p><code>requirements</code>, no <code>aiGrader</code>, no <code>assignmentCode</code>. Either build it as an</p>\n<p>AI-graded writeup modelled on 1.3.12 / 1.3.17, or drop it from the plan; do</p>\n<p>not let this table claim it again. Note <code>scripts/check-assignment-codes.mjs</code></p>\n<p>validates the codes that exist and cannot detect a missing one, so nothing</p>\n<p>catches this automatically.</p>\n\n<h3>Two things deliberately not built</h3>\n\n<p>- <strong>JSDoc.</strong> <code>curriculum-plan.md</code> §1.3 lists \"JSDoc-style function</p>\n<p>  documentation (introduce now, reinforce all year)\". Students have not met</p>\n<p>  functions yet: those arrive in §3.1, so a JSDoc lesson here would be</p>\n<p>  teaching <code>@param</code> for a construct that does not exist to them. The style</p>\n<p>  guide already marks JSDoc as <em>enforced from Week 6</em>. Build it against §3.1.</p>\n<p>- <strong>The AP CSP open-source/licensing discussion</strong> in the plan is a</p>\n<p>  teacher-led in-class activity with no in-app slot, same as §1.2's binary</p>\n<p>  worksheet.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Known inconsistency, not fixed here:</strong> <code>class-style-guide.md</code>'s deductions</p>\n<p>table is written in points (\"-1 pt per instance\"), which predates the</p>\n<p>move to mastery grading. Every lesson in this module carries <code>points: 0</code>.</p>\n<p>The style guide needs a pass to match; that is a curriculum-author call, not</p>\n<p>a build one.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Grandfathered graders, 2026-09-19.</strong> The course dropped <code>UPPER_SNAKE_CASE</code>:</p>\n<p>every value is camelCase now and <code>const</code> alone says <em>this never moves</em>. 1.3.2 is</p>\n<p>the reading that teaches it and was rewritten for it; <code>class-style-guide.md</code>'s</p>\n<p>Constants row moved with it. Because this module ran in week 4 under the old</p>\n<p>rule, <code>1.3.19</code>'s r1 and r5 accept <strong>either</strong> spelling — <code>(?:taxRate|TAX_RATE)</code> —</p>\n<p>so a paper already handed in still scores 10/10 (verified through</p>\n<p><code>lib/grader.ts</code>). Do not collapse those alternations: they are a grandfather for</p>\n<p>submitted work. Chapters 2 and 3 are not grandfathered.</p>\n"},"1.4":{"title":"Programming Paradigms and Languages","html":"\n<h1>Module 1.4: Programming Paradigms and Languages</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 1 · Week 4 · Environment: browser DevTools console</strong></p>\n\n<h2>SLO Focus</h2>\n<p>- Reinforces <strong>SLO-2</strong>: structured programming, placed against the other</p>\n<p>  paradigms so students can see what discipline it actually imposes.</p>\n\n<h2>Module Goal</h2>\n<p>Students can say why so many languages exist, tell a low-level language from</p>\n<p>a high-level one, say what a paradigm is, name the three structures every</p>\n<p>program is built from, tell procedural, object-oriented and functional code</p>\n<p>apart on sight, and explain what it means to call JavaScript multi-paradigm.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Module 1.3 (Documentation and Coding Conventions) complete. Students have</p>\n<p>written sequential console code with variables, comments and conventions :</p>\n<p>and nothing else. No conditionals, no loops, no functions yet.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Students can name three paradigms and at least one language suited to each,</p>\n<p>defend a language choice in terms of the problem being solved, and identify</p>\n<p>which structures a described program needs. No new syntax is required of</p>\n<p>them.</p>\n\n<h2>Note</h2>\n<p>Book §1.4 Programming Paradigms and Languages (368 lines). Built 2026-08-16</p>\n<p>as a 23-lesson sequence (<code>1-4-1-slides</code> … <code>1-4-23-a1-4-1-two-languages</code>),</p>\n<p>matching module 1.1's density (23 lessons over 253 book lines).</p>\n\n<p>This is a <strong>zoom-out week: no new syntax.</strong> The readings show code in other</p>\n<p>paradigms to look at and talk about, never to write. That is deliberate: it</p>\n<p>means classes in Q2 moSHion and the array methods in §3.7 land as \"oh, that's</p>\n<p>the other paradigm from §1.4\" rather than as unexplained new vocabulary.</p>\n<p>Students have only seen procedural code, so do not grade them on writing</p>\n<p>OOP or functional code here.</p>\n\n<h3>Lesson list: 1:1 against the book's seven subsections</h3>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Lesson</th><th>Type</th><th>Book</th></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-4-1-slides`</td><td>slides</td><td>: (placeholder; no deck built yet)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-4-2-reading-why-so-many-languages`</td><td>reading</td><td>§1.4.1: purpose driven, \"best for what?\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-4-3-match-the-language`</td><td>console lab</td><td>§1.4.1: five jobs, five languages</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-4-4-reading-levels-of-abstraction`</td><td>reading</td><td>§1.4.2: level of abstraction</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-4-5-reading-high-and-low-level`</td><td>reading</td><td>§1.4.2: **Definition 1.4.1** and the trade-off</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-4-6-which-level-fits`</td><td>assignment</td><td>§1.4.2 Concepts in Practice + PS 1.4.2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-4-7-reading-what-is-a-paradigm`</td><td>reading</td><td>§1.4.3: **Definition 1.4.2**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-4-8-reading-procedural-programming`</td><td>reading</td><td>§1.4.4: data and instructions kept separate</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-4-9-reading-structured-programming`</td><td>reading</td><td>§1.4.4: **Definition 1.4.3**, the strong claim</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-4-10-reading-three-structures`</td><td>reading</td><td>§1.4.4: sequence, selection, repetition</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-4-11-example-which-structures`</td><td>example</td><td>§1.4.4 Try It Now 1.4.1, extended to three programs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-4-12-name-the-structure`</td><td>console lab</td><td>§1.4.4 Concepts in Practice + PS 1.4.3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-4-13-reading-why-goto-went-away`</td><td>reading</td><td>§1.4.4 Context Pause + PS 1.4.9</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-4-14-three-structures-three-examples`</td><td>assignment</td><td>PS 1.4.8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-4-15-reading-object-oriented`</td><td>reading</td><td>§1.4.5: **Definition 1.4.4** + Table 1.4.1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-4-16-example-procedural-vs-oo`</td><td>example</td><td>§1.4.7 Try It Now 1.4.2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-4-17-where-does-the-balance-live`</td><td>assignment</td><td>§1.4.5 Concepts in Practice + PS 1.4.4, 1.4.10</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-4-18-reading-functional-programming`</td><td>reading</td><td>§1.4.6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-4-19-reading-multi-paradigm`</td><td>reading</td><td>§1.4.7: the whole subsection</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-4-20-sort-the-snippet`</td><td>console lab</td><td>§1.4.7 Concepts in Practice</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-4-21-video-why-so-many-languages`</td><td>video</td><td>§1.4.1 (URL empty: teacher curates)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-4-22-unit-quiz`</td><td>assignment</td><td>PS 1.4.1, 1.4.7, 1.4.5, 1.4.8, 1.4.10, 1.4.11</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-4-23-a1-4-1-two-languages`</td><td>assignment</td><td>A1.4.1 (shCode's own, per BOOK-TO-MODULE §1)</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Two authoring notes</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Vocabulary follows the book, not the plan.</strong> <code>curriculum-plan.md</code> frames</p>\n<p>this section as imperative-versus-declarative; the book never uses the word</p>\n<p>\"declarative\" in §1.4 and organises the whole section around</p>\n<p>procedural / object-oriented / functional instead. The book is upstream</p>\n<p>(BOOK-TO-MODULE §1), so the lessons use the book's three names. \"Iteration\"</p>\n<p>is likewise demoted to a glossary alias for the book's \"repetition\".</p>\n\n<p><strong>The functional reading uses <code>.filter</code> in its live block</strong>, an idiom</p>\n<p><code>reading-conventions.md</code> §4 normally keeps out of beginner readings. That is</p>\n<p>the sanctioned exception: the idiom <em>is</em> the concept under discussion, and</p>\n<p>the student reads and runs it rather than reproducing it. The object-oriented</p>\n<p>reading deliberately does <strong>not</strong> show a <code>class</code>: the book defers methods to</p>\n<p>Chapter 5 and stops at the object literal, so this module does too.</p>\n"},"1.5":{"title":"Program Design Tools and Environments","html":"\n<h1>Module 1.5: Program Design Tools and Environments</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 1 · Weeks 5–6 · Environment: browser DevTools console</strong></p>\n\n<h2>SLO Focus</h2>\n<p>- <strong>Introduces SLO-3</strong> (program design tools and environments): this is that</p>\n<p>  topic's primary coverage in Butte's official outline.</p>\n\n<h2>Module Goal</h2>\n<p>Students plan before they type: name the four techniques of computational</p>\n<p>thinking, pseudocode a task in structured English, read and draw a flowchart</p>\n<p>that passes the class convention, run JavaScript in the console and an</p>\n<p>editor, read an error message, and find a silent bug with <code>console.log</code>.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Module 1.4 (Programming Paradigms and Languages) complete.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Students can produce a flowchart that passes all structural checks, turn it</p>\n<p>into working JavaScript, and debug a program that produces a wrong answer</p>\n<p>without erroring. From here on <strong>every</strong> graded build artifact opens with a</p>\n<p>flowchart, and the coding half stays locked until the chart is green.</p>\n\n<h2>Note</h2>\n<p>Book §1.5 Program Design Tools and Environments (983 lines: the largest</p>\n<p>section in Chapter 1, scheduled at <strong>two meetings</strong>). Ships as a 44-lesson</p>\n<p>sequence, 1:1 against the book's nine subsections.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The class flowchart convention (Appendix D) is handed out here</strong>, exactly</p>\n<p>as the style guide is handed out in §1.3, and is enforced on every graded</p>\n<p>build artifact from this point forward. Only four shapes exist this week :</p>\n<p>the book's oval, rectangle and diamond, plus the parallelogram, which is</p>\n<p>ours. The editor hides four more behind <strong>+ more shapes</strong>: the loop hexagon</p>\n<p>arrives at §2.2, the function-call shape at §3.1, connectors and notes at</p>\n<p>§4.1. Tell students they exist and to leave them alone: a student who finds</p>\n<p>them early will use a hexagon as decoration.</p>\n\n<h3>This module was renumbered, unlike 1.2 and 1.3</h3>\n\n<p>Modules 1.2 and 1.3 had their new lessons <strong>appended</strong> because existing</p>\n<p>folders carried live <code>lesson_state</code> rows. Module 1.5 had <strong>none</strong> (verified</p>\n<p>against D1 on 2026-08-16), and appending would have left A1.5.2 sitting at</p>\n<p>1.5.5, before the four lessons that teach computational thinking. So the</p>\n<p>five pre-existing lessons were renumbered into book order instead:</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Was</th><th>Is</th><th>Why it moved</th></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-5-1-slides`</td><td>`1.5.1`</td><td>unchanged</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-5-5-a1-5-2-computational-thinking`</td><td>`1.5.17`</td><td>now follows §1.5.1–1.5.3, which teach it</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-5-2-reading-pseudocode-and-flowcharts`</td><td>`1.5.19`</td><td>sits with the rest of §1.5.4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-5-3-reading-the-flowchart-convention`</td><td>`1.5.29`</td><td>after the book's three shapes are taught</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-5-4-a1-5-1-flowchart-gate`</td><td>`1.5.30`</td><td>immediately before its own JS half</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p><strong>A1.5.2's narrower wording is restored</strong> as a consequence. It now asks for</p>\n<p>four examples drawn from a program the student wrote this quarter, with an</p>\n<p>everyday task as fallback: the change <code>curriculum-plan.md</code> asked for \"once</p>\n<p>§1.5.1–1.5.3 are built\".</p>\n\n<h3>Lesson list: 1:1 against the book's nine subsections</h3>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Lessons</th><th>Book</th><th>Covers</th></tr>\n<tr><td>`1.5.1`</td><td>:</td><td>slides placeholder; no deck built yet</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1.5.2`–`1.5.7`</td><td>§1.5.1</td><td>two difficulties; **Def 1.5.1**; the four cornerstones; the three As; the jam-sandwich robot (Try It Now 1.1)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1.5.8`–`1.5.12`</td><td>§1.5.2</td><td>**Def 1.5.2**; pattern recognition and Table 1.5.1; induction vs deduction; Try It Now 1.2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1.5.13`–`1.5.16`</td><td>§1.5.3</td><td>**Def 1.5.3**; layers of abstraction; chess; Try It Now 1.3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1.5.17`</td><td>:</td><td>**A1.5.2**, AI-graded</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1.5.18`–`1.5.24`</td><td>§1.5.4</td><td>**Def 1.5.4**, **Def 1.5.5**; the two rules; Example 1.1; butter-or-no-butter keywords; Try It Now 1.4 + PS 1.5.10</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1.5.25`–`1.5.31`</td><td>§1.5.5</td><td>Table 1.5.2, **Def 1.5.6**; the diamond; when to use which; Try It Now 1.5; the class convention; **A1.5.1** chart **and its JS half**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1.5.32`–`1.5.34`</td><td>§1.5.6</td><td>sequential/parallel/concurrent; **Def 1.5.7** recursion; Try It Now 1.6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1.5.35`–`1.5.37`</td><td>§1.5.7</td><td>**Def 1.5.8** console; code editors; Try It Now 1.7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1.5.38`–`1.5.39`</td><td>§1.5.8</td><td>the three parts of an error message; Try It Now 1.8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1.5.40`–`1.5.44`</td><td>§1.5.9</td><td>test cases; **Def 1.5.9** debugging; Try It Now 1.9; a three-bug debug lab; module quiz</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p><strong>A1.5.1 is now complete.</strong> <code>1.5.30</code> is the chart half (unchanged) and</p>\n<p><code>1.5.31</code> is the JavaScript half the plan listed and this module never had :</p>\n<p>the student writes their chart out as pseudocode, implements it, and confirms</p>\n<p>the code matches the plan.</p>\n\n<h3>Authoring notes</h3>\n\n<p>- <strong>Recursion (1.5.33–1.5.34) is a preview, not a requirement.</strong> The book</p>\n<p>  teaches it here with a JS function; functions arrive in §3.1. Students read</p>\n<p>  and run it; nothing asks them to write one.</p>\n<p>- <strong>1.5.34 uses <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code></strong> solely so the stack-overflow message prints</p>\n<p>  tidily instead of halting the page. It is flagged in the lesson as</p>\n<p>  something to ignore for now.</p>\n<p>- <strong>1.5.31 has no \"write the pseudocode first\" grader check.</strong> A regex cannot</p>\n<p>  distinguish a student's plan comments from the starter's instructions, and</p>\n<p>  a check that passes on an untouched file is worse than no check. The</p>\n<p>  pseudocode requirement is graded properly at 1.5.24 instead.</p>\n"},"1.6":{"title":"Chapter 1 Group Performance Assessment","html":"\n<h1>Module 1.6: Chapter 1 Group Performance Assessment</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 1 · Week 4 · One meeting · Paired · Environment: browser DevTools console</strong></p>\n\n<p>This is the chapter's performance assessment, and it is the first one of the year.</p>\n<p>It runs in a single block, in pairs, in three thirds: <strong>design, build, demo.</strong> The</p>\n<p>design third is a flowchart and it comes first — neither partner opens an editor</p>\n<p>until the chart is green (Appendix D).</p>\n\n<h2>What it assesses</h2>\n\n<p>Everything in Chapter 1 at once, which is the point. No section is assessed twice</p>\n<p>and none is left out:</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Section</th><th>Where it shows up</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1.1 Software Lifecycle</td><td>The demo names which lifecycle phase each third of the period was</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2 Variables and Data Types</td><td>Three distinct primitive types in the program, reported with `typeof`</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.3 Documentation and Conventions</td><td>Header comment, pseudocode comments, inline comments, camelCase names</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.4 Paradigms and Languages</td><td>The demo names the paradigm the program is written in, and why</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.5 Program Design Tools</td><td>The flowchart, the pseudocode, the test-case table, the `console.log` trace</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Weights</h2>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Third</th><th>Weight</th><th>Lesson</th></tr>\n<tr><td>**Design** — the flowchart, drawn together, all checks green</td><td>25%</td><td>1.6.1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**Build** — the program, documented to the style guide</td><td>50%</td><td>1.6.2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**Demo** — run it, test it, explain it</td><td>25%</td><td>1.6.3</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>The in-app lessons are green-to-advance like every other lesson in the course.</p>\n<p>The 25/50/25 split is how the three artifacts are weighted in the gradebook, not</p>\n<p>something the checker computes.</p>\n\n<h2>Timing for one 1.75-hour block</h2>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Minutes</th><th>What</th></tr>\n<tr><td>0–10</td><td>Pairs form, pick one problem from the menu of four, read the brief</td></tr>\n<tr><td>10–35</td><td>**Design.** Pseudocode on paper, then the chart in 1.6.1. Editors stay closed.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>35–75</td><td>**Build.** 1.6.2. One screen, partners swap the keyboard at least once.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>75–95</td><td>**Demo.** 1.6.3 writeup, then run the program for the teacher.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>95–105</td><td>Slack. Some pair will spend 20 minutes arguing about the chart, which is the pairing working.</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Why the flowchart is 25% and comes first</h2>\n\n<p>Two students who disagree about what happens next have to settle it on the diagram</p>\n<p>before either of them can type. That argument is the assessment. A pair that skips</p>\n<p>it produces a chart drawn after the code, which reads exactly like one and is</p>\n<p>graded as one (Appendix D §D.7).</p>\n\n<h2>What this deliberately does not ask for</h2>\n\n<p><strong>No <code>if</code> statement.</strong> Conditionals are Chapter 2. The chart has a diamond because</p>\n<p>1.5 teaches the diamond, and the program answers that diamond by *computing a</p>\n<p>boolean* with a comparison — which 1.2 does teach — and printing it. The two</p>\n<p>branches are written out as pseudocode comments. Chapter 2's Group PA is where they</p>\n<p>become code.</p>\n\n<p><strong>No functions, no loops, no arrays.</strong> The program is a straight line. That is not a</p>\n<p>simplification, it is the honest ceiling of what Chapter 1 taught, and it is the same</p>\n<p>reason the Chapter 1 Test's Part D is a chart and pseudocode rather than JavaScript.</p>\n\n<h2>The menu</h2>\n\n<p>Four problems, matched in difficulty, all the same shape: take some numbers in,</p>\n<p>compute one value, compare that value against a fixed threshold. Full statements</p>\n<p>are in 1.6.1.</p>\n\n<p>1. <strong>Slice Economics</strong> — cost per slice of a pizza, against a good-deal limit</p>\n<p>2. <strong>Split the Check</strong> — cost per person after tip, against what everyone agreed to</p>\n<p>3. <strong>Grade Forecast</strong> — a weighted course average, against the passing mark</p>\n<p>4. <strong>Fuel Stop</strong> — how far a tank goes, against the length of the trip</p>\n\n<p>Pairs at adjacent tables should not pick the same one.</p>\n\n<h2>Teacher notes</h2>\n\n<p>- <strong>Circulate during the design third, not the build third.</strong> The build is where it</p>\n<p>  looks like the work is happening and the design is where the work actually is.</p>\n<p>  A pair whose chart is green in four minutes has drawn a straight line with no</p>\n<p>  diamond; send them back.</p>\n<p>- <strong>The one failure mode is a silent partner.</strong> The demo writeup asks who drove</p>\n<p>  which third by name. Read that answer before you read the program.</p>\n<p>- <strong>A pair that finishes early has not finished.</strong> Ask them for a fourth test case</p>\n<p>  that breaks their program. Every one of these four problems has one — a zero, a</p>\n<p>  negative, a string where a number belongs.</p>\n\n<h2>Grading note: r3 accepts two spellings on purpose</h2>\n\n<p>The course dropped <code>UPPER_SNAKE_CASE</code> on 2026-09-19. One naming rule now covers</p>\n<p>every value — camelCase — and <code>const</code> alone carries <em>this never moves</em>; 1.3.2</p>\n<p>teaches it that way and <code>class-style-guide.md</code> records it.</p>\n\n<p>This module ran in <strong>week 4, under the old rule</strong>, so <code>1.6.2</code>'s r3 pattern reads</p>\n<p><code>const\\s+(?:[a-z][A-Za-z0-9]<em>\\s</em>=\\s<em>\\d|[A-Z][A-Z0-9_]{2,}\\s</em>=)</code>. The second</p>\n<p>branch is the week-4 pattern <strong>verbatim</strong>, so a paper already handed in scores</p>\n<p>exactly what it scored then; the first carries the current rule. Verified through</p>\n<p><code>lib/grader.ts</code>: both spellings score 10/10.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Do not collapse the alternation.</strong> It is a grandfather for submitted work, not</p>\n<p>redundancy. Modules 2.6 and 3.9 are deliberately <em>not</em> grandfathered — they open</p>\n<p>Sep 28 and later, have no submissions behind them, and their r3 rejects the old</p>\n<p>spelling so the single convention is what students meet.</p>\n"},"1.7":{"title":"Chapter 1 Individual Performance Assessment","html":"\n<h1>Module 1.7: Chapter 1 Individual Performance Assessment</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 1 · Week 4 · Thu Sep 03, 2026 · One meeting · Individual · ~50 minutes</strong></p>\n\n<p>The individual half of the chapter's assessment, and the sibling of the Group</p>\n<p>PA two days before it: same chapter, same week, same in-app shape, sat alone.</p>\n<p>Five lessons, 16 items, 100 points, and no working program written at any</p>\n<p>point — because Chapter 1 has not taught a conditional, and an assessment that</p>\n<p>outruns the book is testing shCode rather than the course.</p>\n\n<h2>Why this is not the Group PA again, alone</h2>\n\n<p>The PA (1.6) already proves a pair can produce. What a paired build cannot tell</p>\n<p>you is which of the two produced it.</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th></th><th>1.6 Group PA (Sep 1)</th><th>1.7 Test (Sep 3)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Who</td><td>pairs</td><td>individual</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Length</td><td>~85 min over three lessons</td><td>~49 min over five</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The verb</td><td>**build** one thing end to end</td><td>**read · diagnose · design**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Code written</td><td>a whole documented program</td><td>none</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Flowchart</td><td>25 min, drawn together, gates the build</td><td>8 min, drawn alone, gates the pseudocode</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Coverage</td><td>all five modules through one artifact</td><td>all five modules as independent items</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The failure it catches</td><td>cannot turn a plan into a program</td><td>cannot read code, cannot name a bug, **was carried**</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>Tracing and diagnosis are the two verbs the PA structurally cannot isolate, and</p>\n<p>they are the ones that generalise: a student who can only write and never read</p>\n<p>produces code they cannot debug.</p>\n\n<h2>Coverage and weights</h2>\n\n<p>16 items. Every module of the chapter appears in at least two places.</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Part</th><th>Lesson</th><th>Items</th><th>Points</th><th>Minutes</th></tr>\n<tr><td>**A** Concepts (multiple choice)</td><td>1.7.1</td><td>3</td><td>15</td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**B** Trace the code</td><td>1.7.1</td><td>5</td><td>25</td><td>16</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**A** Concepts (short answer)</td><td>1.7.2</td><td>3</td><td>10</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**C** Find and fix, name the type</td><td>1.7.3</td><td>4</td><td>20</td><td>11</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**D1** Chart it</td><td>1.7.4</td><td>1</td><td>10</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**D2** Write the steps</td><td>1.7.5</td><td>1</td><td>20</td><td>8</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td></td><td>**16**</td><td>**100**</td><td>**49**</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>The four-part A/B/C/D weighting the curriculum plan fixes for every chapter test</p>\n<p>— 25 / 25 / 20 / 30 — lands exactly: the eight equally weighted questions in</p>\n<p>1.7.1 come to 15 + 25, and Part A's remaining 10 are the short answers in 1.7.2.</p>\n\n<h3>Where each module is assessed</h3>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Module</th><th>Points</th><th>Where</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1.1 Software Lifecycle</td><td>9</td><td>one concept MCQ, one short answer</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.2 Variables and Data Types</td><td>30</td><td>all five traces, one of the four bugs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.3 Documentation and Conventions</td><td>9</td><td>one short answer on a supplied snippet, one bug</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.4 Paradigms and Languages</td><td>8</td><td>one concept MCQ, one short answer</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.5 Program Design Tools</td><td>44</td><td>one concept MCQ, two bugs, the whole of Part D</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p><strong>The skew toward 1.2 and 1.5 is structural, not an oversight.</strong> Tracing only</p>\n<p>works on code Chapter 1 permits you to run, which is all §1.2, and Part D is</p>\n<p>design, which is all §1.5 — so those two soak up 74% before any item is written.</p>\n<p>§1.4 at 8% is the accepted cost of that; the alternative is padding Part B with</p>\n<p>recall questions dressed as traces. Reviewed and accepted, 2026-09-02.</p>\n\n<h2>The ceiling this test may not exceed</h2>\n\n<p><strong>No conditionals, no loops, no functions, no arrays, no objects.</strong> Every snippet</p>\n<p>on this test is a straight sequence. Part D is a chart plus pseudocode, never</p>\n<p>JavaScript, for the same reason the PA's program has a diamond that computes a</p>\n<p>boolean instead of an <code>if</code>.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Three flowchart shapes — oval, rectangle, diamond</strong> (Appendix D §D.2). Book</p>\n<p>exercise 1.5.11 asks for exactly three; the parallelogram is a shCode addition.</p>\n<p>The editor offers it and a student who uses one is not penalised, but nothing on</p>\n<p>this test may <em>require</em> it.</p>\n\n<h2>Three forms, assigned automatically</h2>\n\n<p>Same room, same minute, different papers — with no cards to hand out and no</p>\n<p>roster for a teacher to keep.</p>\n\n<pre><code>\n1.7.1 quiz ── variants: [\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"] ── hash(lessonId + student email)\n                                              │\n        ┌─────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────┐\n        ▼                                     ▼                     ▼\n   3 common concepts               3 common concepts       3 common concepts\n   + 5 traces (form a)             + 5 traces (form b)     + 5 traces (form c)\n        └──────── questions and options shuffled per student ───────┘\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Eighteen questions are authored; each student is asked eight. The five traces</p>\n<p>are the same five <em>shapes</em> on every form — a <code>const</code> reassignment, a coercion, a</p>\n<p><code>typeof</code> surprise, a sticky <code>NaN</code>, a template literal — with different names and</p>\n<p>numbers, so the forms are genuinely equivalent and a neighbour's screen is no</p>\n<p>help. <code>lib/quiz-variant.ts</code> does the assignment; <code>scripts/test-quiz.mjs</code> refuses</p>\n<p>to build a quiz whose forms come out different lengths.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The other three lessons are single-form, deliberately.</strong> Part C's answer is</p>\n<p>typed into a file rather than picked from a list, so copying a neighbour means</p>\n<p>retyping their code more slowly than fixing your own. Parts D1 and D2 offer a</p>\n<p>menu of three problems and the student picks one, the same way the PA does.</p>\n\n<h2>Test mode</h2>\n\n<p>Both <code>QuizConfig</code> and <code>AiGraderConfig</code> take <code>summative: true</code>, added for this</p>\n<p>test and inherited by the other seven chapter tests.</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th></th><th>A module quiz</th><th>A chapter test (`summative`)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Attempts</td><td>unlimited</td><td>one; the paper locks on submit</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Marking shown</td><td>per-question, right or wrong</td><td>none</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Explanations shown</td><td>yes, on every question</td><td>none</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Score shown</td><td>yes</td><td>no — the teacher hands it back</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Completion</td><td>on passing</td><td>**on submitting**</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>Completing on submission rather than on score is the load-bearing one. The</p>\n<p>course is green-to-advance, so a score gate on Part A would lock a student out</p>\n<p>of Parts B through D of their own exam over an answer nobody has marked yet.</p>\n\n<p>Without this mode a quiz is a study tool wearing a test's clothes: it marks each</p>\n<p>answer, explains why, and invites another go.</p>\n\n<h2>What each part reveals while it is being sat</h2>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Part</th><th>Attempts</th><th>What the app tells the student</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1 Concepts and Traces</td><td>one</td><td>nothing — no marking, no explanations, no score</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2 In Your Own Words</td><td>one</td><td>nothing</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5 Write the Steps</td><td>one</td><td>nothing</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4 Chart It</td><td>unlimited</td><td>whether it is a *legal* flowchart, never whether it answers the problem</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3 Find and Fix</td><td>unlimited</td><td>which symptom is still showing</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>Parts 3 and 4 stay open on purpose: repairing and redrawing ARE the skills, and</p>\n<p>a student who cannot check their work has been set a guessing game. What Part 3</p>\n<p>gives back was narrowed on 2026-09-02 — the checklist used to name the repair</p>\n<p>(\"Shipping is a number, not text\"), which walked a student who had found nothing</p>\n<p>to full marks. It now names the symptom they could see for themselves by running</p>\n<p>it (\"the Before tax line prints the wrong thing\"), and the hints say where to</p>\n<p>look rather than what to type. The diagnosis, which is the thing being assessed,</p>\n<p>stays theirs.</p>\n\n<p>Console lessons have no <code>summative</code> concept, so Part 3 could not be locked to one</p>\n<p>submission without new behaviour in <code>LessonWorkspace</code>. Narrowing the checklist was</p>\n<p>chosen over building that on the eve of the assessment.</p>\n\n<h2>Grading and the gradebook</h2>\n\n<p>shCode records the work; it does not compute the 100. <code>points</code> is 0 on all five</p>\n<p>lessons and <code>passingScore</code> is 0, as everywhere else in the course. The teacher</p>\n<p>reads the submissions from the teacher view and enters one score per student in</p>\n<p>Aeries, against the 15% Individual Chapter Tests category.</p>\n\n<p>The AI grader marks 1.7.2 and 1.7.5 against their rubrics and stores the result;</p>\n<p>under <code>summative</code> the student never sees it. Treat the model's marks as a first</p>\n<p>pass to review, not as the grade.</p>\n\n<h2>No retake</h2>\n\n<p><strong>Decided 2026-09-02.</strong> The score a student earns on the day is the score, and</p>\n<p>the curriculum plan's common-format rule was changed to match. There is no Form</p>\n<p>R, no alternate paper and no second sitting.</p>\n\n<p>That puts the whole weight of fairness on the three same-room forms and on the</p>\n<p>open-date lock, because a student who sees the paper early has no second chance</p>\n<p>to be assessed honestly and neither does anyone else. Keep the module locked</p>\n<p>until the period starts.</p>\n\n<p>An absence is the case this leaves open. A student who is not in the room on the</p>\n<p>day cannot sit a paper that does not reopen, so that is a teacher judgement each</p>\n<p>time rather than a policy the app enforces.</p>\n\n<h2>Teacher notes</h2>\n\n<p>- <strong>Hand the PA checklist back as the study guide on Sep 1.</strong> Every item type on</p>\n<p>  this test has an analogue in the PA the student just built. That is deliberate</p>\n<p>  and it is not a secret.</p>\n<p>- <strong>Proctor Part B.</strong> In-app means a console is a tab away, and a \"what does this</p>\n<p>  print\" question is checkable in about twenty seconds by anyone who opens one.</p>\n<p>  Shuffling and three forms defeat the over-the-shoulder glance; they do not</p>\n<p>  defeat a second tab. Circulate.</p>\n<p>- <strong>The open-date lock is client-side only</strong> (<code>components/LessonAccessGate.tsx</code>).</p>\n<p>  It stops the lesson rendering early; it does not stop a determined student</p>\n<p>  reaching the lesson's data through the API. Good enough for a lock that is</p>\n<p>  really a courtesy, and worth knowing before you rely on it.</p>\n<p>- <strong>Read Part D1 and D2 together.</strong> A correct chart with botched pseudocode is a</p>\n<p>  different failure from no chart at all, and the 10/20 split is what makes that</p>\n<p>  visible. D2 is graded against the problem, not against D1 — a student who</p>\n<p>  charts one approach and writes a better one loses nothing. Only a blank D1</p>\n<p>  costs the 10.</p>\n\n<h2>Still open</h2>\n\n<p>- <strong>The flowchart-check count was contradictory and is now fixed.</strong></p>\n<p>  <code>1-5-29-reading-the-flowchart-convention</code> headed its table \"The nine checks\",</p>\n<p>  listed nine correctly, then said \"All eight can be green on a chart that is</p>\n<p>  wrong.\" Corrected to nine, 2026-09-02. Appendix D §D.3 still lists eight</p>\n<p>  because it omits <code>no-self-loop</code>; 1.7.4 declares ten, which is those nine plus</p>\n<p>  a <code>min-process</code> floor the convention explicitly allows an assignment to add.</p>\n<p>- <strong><code>AiGraderConfig</code> is declared twice</strong> — <code>lib/types.ts</code> and</p>\n<p>  <code>components/WrittenGrader.tsx</code> — and <code>summative</code> had to be added to both. Not</p>\n<p>  introduced here, but it will bite the next field somebody adds.</p>\n"},"2.1":{"title":"Conditionals","html":"\n<h1>Module 2.1: Conditionals</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 1 · Week 5 · Environment: browser DevTools console</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Meets Tue Sep 08 + Thu Sep 10, 2026 (2 meetings, book §2.1 Conditionals, 1329 lines: largest section in the book).</strong></p>\n\n<h2>SLO Focus</h2>\n<p>- Reinforces <strong>SLO 3</strong>: students implement their first structured programs with branching.</p>\n\n<h2>Module Goal</h2>\n<p>Students can make decisions in code: <code>if</code> / <code>else if</code> / <code>else</code>, truthy/falsy conversion, the conditional (ternary) operator <code>?</code>, comparison operators (<code>===</code> vs <code>==</code>), and logical operators (<code>&amp;&amp; || !</code>).</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Unit 1 complete: students can declare variables, use the four primitive types, and write expressions.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Students can branch with <code>if</code>/<code>else if</code>/<code>else</code>, pick values with <code>?</code>, and combine conditions with logical operators. Ready for §2.2 Algorithms and Loops.</p>\n\n<h2>Lesson count</h2>\n<p><strong>43 lessons</strong> (<code>2.1.1</code>–<code>2.1.43</code>), up from 14. The 2026-08-16 buildout below records</p>\n<p>40; three more have since been inserted with letter-suffixed folder slugs so that no</p>\n<p>built folder was renamed — <code>2-1-9a</code>/<code>2-1-9b</code> (titled <code>2.1.18</code>/<code>2.1.19</code>, book Example</p>\n<p>2.1.2 One Equals Sign, or Three?) and <code>2-1-34a</code> (titled <code>2.1.38</code>, book Example 2.1.12</p>\n<p>A Condition That Is Always True). Titles are contiguous integers <code>2.1.1</code>–<code>2.1.43</code> with</p>\n<p>no gaps or duplicates.</p>\n\n<h2>Assignments</h2>\n<p>- <strong>A2.1.1</strong> (<code>2.1.42</code>): Lab: grade recommendation advisor using <code>score</code>/<code>attendance</code>/<code>lateAssignments</code>, an <code>else if</code> chain, and at least one <code>&amp;&amp;</code>/<code>||</code>.</p>\n<p>- <strong>A2.1.2</strong> (<code>2.1.43</code>, last lesson in the section): Quiz: <code>===</code> vs <code>==</code>, operator precedence, and tracing three conditional snippets. AI-graded written assignment.</p>\n\n<h2>Book coverage (built §2.1, checked 2026-09-05)</h2>\n\n<p>Every subsection of the built book section has at least one lesson. Lesson numbers</p>\n<p>are <code>title</code> numbers (<code>2.1.N</code>), not folder slugs.</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Book §2.1 subsection</th><th>Lessons</th></tr>\n<tr><td>2.1.1 The \"if\" Statement</td><td>2.1.2, 2.1.3, 2.1.5, 2.1.6, 2.1.18, 2.1.19</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.1.2 Boolean Conversion</td><td>2.1.12, 2.1.13, 2.1.14</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.1.3 The \"else\" Clause</td><td>2.1.3, 2.1.5, 2.1.7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.1.4 Several Conditions: \"else if\"</td><td>2.1.3, 2.1.4, 2.1.8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.1.5 Conditional Operator '?'</td><td>2.1.20, 2.1.21, 2.1.22, 2.1.36</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.1.6 Multiple '?'</td><td>2.1.23, 2.1.24, 2.1.25</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.1.7 Non-traditional Use of '?'</td><td>2.1.26, 2.1.27, 2.1.28</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.1.8 Combining Conditions</td><td>2.1.29, 2.1.30, 2.1.31, 2.1.32, 2.1.33, 2.1.34, 2.1.35, 2.1.37, 2.1.38</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>shCode-designed, anchored to no single subsection: <code>2.1.1</code> (deck), <code>2.1.9</code>–<code>2.1.11</code></p>\n<p>(nested conditionals, composed from §2.1.1 + §2.1.3), <code>2.1.15</code>–<code>2.1.17</code> (comparison</p>\n<p>operators and <code>===</code> vs <code>==</code>: the book uses them throughout §2.1 but gives them no</p>\n<p>subsection), <code>2.1.39</code> (Problem Set 2.1.7 intent), <code>2.1.40</code> (curriculum-plan's print</p>\n<p>settings advisor), <code>2.1.41</code> (challenges), <code>2.1.42</code> (<strong>A2.1.1</strong>), <code>2.1.43</code> (<strong>A2.1.2</strong>).</p>\n\n<h2>Note</h2>\n<p>Split out of the former combined \"1.2 Control Flow\" unit spec during the</p>\n<p>2026-08-12 book-native renumber (book §2.1 Conditionals).</p>\n\n<p><strong>2026-08-16 buildout:</strong> expanded from 14 lessons to 40 against a full read of</p>\n<p>book §2.1 (Pass A: <code>if</code>/truthy-falsy/<code>else</code>/<code>else if</code>/ternary/chained</p>\n<p>ternary/ternary-vs-if/<code>&amp;&amp;</code>/<code>||</code>/<code>!</code>, all eight book subsections plus both</p>\n<p>Definitions) and the curriculum plan's named in-class activities (Pass B:</p>\n<p>nested conditionals, the \"print settings advisor\" scenario). <code>week</code> corrected</p>\n<p>from the stale <code>4</code> to <code>5</code> (curriculum-plan schedules §2.1 at calendar week 5,</p>\n<p>Tue Sep 08 + Thu Sep 10, 2026) across every lesson in the module, including</p>\n<p>the 14 pre-existing ones. Fixed a real defect found in the pre-existing</p>\n<p><code>2-1-4a-chart-the-if-else</code>: its title carried a retired letter suffix</p>\n<p>(<code>2.1.5a</code>), which the strict <code>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+</code> title parser silently drops from</p>\n<p><code>/module/2.1</code>: renumbered to the integer-only <code>2.1.5</code>. Every lesson,</p>\n<p>including the 14 carried over, now has a <code>sourceRef</code> tagging it Pass A</p>\n<p>(book-sourced) or Pass B (designed scaffolding). Book Problem Set items were</p>\n<p>read for intent and folded into designed lab/quiz scenarios rather than</p>\n<p>transcribed 1:1, per <code>BOOK-TO-MODULE.md</code> §5 (\"shCode invents its own graded</p>\n<p>work... do not port the book's Problem Sets into lessons as assignments\").</p>\n"},"2.2":{"title":"Algorithms and Loops","html":"\n<h1>Module 2.2: Algorithms and Loops</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 1 · Week 6 · Meets Mon Sep 14, 2026 · Environment: browser DevTools console</strong></p>\n\n<h2>SLO Focus</h2>\n<p>- <strong>Introduces SLO 4</strong> (algorithms): primary artifact <strong>A2.2.1</strong> (Written + Lab), gated by the <strong>A2.2.0</strong> flowchart.</p>\n<p>- Reinforces SLO 3 (<code>for</code>, <code>while</code>). <code>do…while</code> belongs to module 2.4, per book §2.4.2.</p>\n\n<h2>Module Goal</h2>\n<p>Students define what an algorithm is, see that a program is an algorithm written</p>\n<p>in a language a computer can run, and repeat work with loops: <code>for</code> and</p>\n<p><code>while</code>. They trace loops to predict output, avoid infinite loops, use</p>\n<p>a step other than 1, and meet the idea of a canonical algorithm (linear search)</p>\n<p>without yet having arrays or <code>break</code> to write the full version.</p>\n\n<p>22 lessons: 16 shipped in the 2026-08-15 diagram-native pilot, 4 added in the</p>\n<p>2026-08-16 coverage pass to close book-coverage gaps (§Note below), and 2 more</p>\n<p>(<code>2.2.19</code>/<code>2.2.20</code>, Example 2.2.6 Walking Through a Word) added afterwards.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Module 2.1 (Conditionals) complete.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Students can trace and write <code>for</code> and <code>while</code> loops, state precisely</p>\n<p>what an algorithm is and how a program differs from one, use a <code>for</code> loop with</p>\n<p>a step other than 1, and describe what a canonical algorithm is.</p>\n\n<h2>Book coverage (built §2.2, checked 2026-09-05)</h2>\n\n<p>Every subsection of the built book section has at least one lesson.</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Book §2.2 subsection</th><th>Lessons</th></tr>\n<tr><td>2.2.1 What Is an Algorithm?</td><td>2.2.2, 2.2.5, 2.2.8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.2.2 Programs Implement Algorithms</td><td>2.2.3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.2.3 Common Ideas Across Languages</td><td>2.2.15</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.2.4 Introduction to Loops</td><td>2.2.9, 2.2.10, 2.2.13, 2.2.14, 2.2.19, 2.2.20</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.2.5 Canonical Algorithms</td><td>2.2.21</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>shCode-designed, anchored to no single subsection: <code>2.2.1</code> (deck), <code>2.2.4</code>, <code>2.2.6</code>,</p>\n<p><code>2.2.11</code>, <code>2.2.18</code> (the Appendix D diagram thread), <code>2.2.7</code> (<strong>A2.2.0</strong> gate),</p>\n<p><code>2.2.12</code>, <code>2.2.16</code> (Problem Set practice), <code>2.2.17</code> (<strong>A2.2.1</strong> Part B), <code>2.2.22</code></p>\n<p>(challenges).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Resolved 2026-09-05 — <code>do...while</code> removed from this module.</strong> Book §2.2 contains</p>\n<p>zero occurrences of <code>do...while</code> (measured across the section <em>and</em> its solutions</p>\n<p>file); the book introduces it in §2.4.2, which module 2.4 covers at <code>2.4.5</code>-<code>2.4.8</code>.</p>\n<p>Lessons <code>2.2.13</code> and <code>2.2.14</code> taught it two modules early, and 2.4 then re-taught it.</p>\n<p>The <code>do...while</code> half of both lessons is now gone: <code>2.2.13</code> is retitled</p>\n<p>\"Reading: The while Loop\" and <code>2.2.14</code> drops its Step 2, so this module teaches <code>for</code></p>\n<p>and <code>while</code> only, exactly as the book does.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Directive: do not re-add <code>do...while</code> to module 2.2.</strong> <code>2.4.5</code> opens with \"Both</p>\n<p>loops you've met so far: <code>for</code> and <code>while</code>\" -- a sentence that was false while the</p>\n<p>preview existed and is true now. Anything reintroduced here breaks it silently, and</p>\n<p>nothing in <code>npm test</code> reads book prose to catch that.</p>\n\n<p>Neither folder was renamed and neither lesson number moved, so student progress on</p>\n<p><code>2-2-8-reading-while-loop</code> and <code>2-2-9-example-while-vs-for</code> is untouched.</p>\n\n<h2>Note</h2>\n<p>Split out of the former combined \"1.2 Control Flow\" unit spec during the</p>\n<p>2026-08-12 book-native renumber (book §2.2 Algorithms and Loops).</p>\n\n<p><strong>2026-08-16 coverage pass</strong> (this build): verified all 16 existing lessons</p>\n<p>against the book, renumbered the A2.2.0 gate chart (<code>2-2-12-a5-2-flowchart-decision</code>)</p>\n<p>from 2.2.15 to 2.2.7 so it actually precedes the artifact it gates, added</p>\n<p>\"A2.2.0\"/\"A2.2.1 (Part A)\" codes to the two existing assignment titles, and</p>\n<p>added 4 lessons to close gaps the coverage table found with no lesson at all:</p>\n<p>2.2.3 Reading: Programs Implement Algorithms (book §2.2.2, previously</p>\n<p>uncovered), 2.2.15 Worked Example: Same Algorithm, Three Spellings (book</p>\n<p>§2.2.3, previously uncovered), 2.2.17 A2.2.1 (Part B), For + While Loop</p>\n<p>Program (curriculum-plan.md's A2.2.1 had no lab half built), and 2.2.19</p>\n<p>Reading: Canonical Algorithms &amp; Linear Search (book §2.2.5, previously</p>\n<p>uncovered: searches a number range instead of an array, since arrays/<code>break</code></p>\n<p>aren't taught until 3.3/2.4). Also fixed: an existing challenge step that</p>\n<p>required arrays before they're taught, a stale numbering leftover from the</p>\n<p>pre-renumber scheme in two labs' scaffold files, and a missing <code>for</code>-loop</p>\n<p>step-size example (book Example 2.4 / Try It Now 2.4).</p>\n\n<p><strong>2026-08-16, later same day:</strong> the first pass of this note used letter-suffixed</p>\n<p>title numbers (<code>2.2.2a</code>, <code>2.2.5b</code>, etc.) to insert lessons without renumbering</p>\n<p>their neighbors. That convention is broken in this codebase: <code>displayTitle</code></p>\n<p>(<code>lib/curriculum.ts</code> line 136) strips only the leading <code>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+</code>, so the</p>\n<p>suffix letter leaked into the rendered title, and <code>numberedId</code> also drops the</p>\n<p>suffix, so two lessons could parse to the same id and sort by directory-read</p>\n<p>order instead of the authored order: silently undoing the gate-before-artifact</p>\n<p>fix. All lessons are now renumbered to plain contiguous integers, title-only; no</p>\n<p>folder was renamed. The module currently runs 2.2.1–2.2.22. Full render order and the corrected</p>\n<p>verification output are in the build report.</p>\n"},"2.3":{"title":"The Switch Statement","html":"\n<h1>Module 2.3: The Switch Statement</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 1 · Week 6 (Wed Sep 16, 2026) · Environment: browser DevTools console</strong></p>\n\n<h2>SLO Focus</h2>\n<p>- Reinforces <strong>SLO 3</strong>.</p>\n\n<h2>Module Goal</h2>\n<p>Students can write a <code>switch</code> statement with <code>case</code>/<code>default</code>/<code>break</code>, explain</p>\n<p>and avoid the fall-through and strict-equality traps, group cases that share</p>\n<p>an outcome, and choose correctly between <code>switch</code> and an <code>if...else</code> chain.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Module 2.1 (Conditionals) and Module 2.2 (Algorithms and Loops) complete.</p>\n<p><code>break</code> is taught here as a <code>switch</code>-exit keyword only: <strong>not</strong> as loop</p>\n<p>control, which is Module 2.4's job. No try/catch, no functions, no arrays.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Students can convert between if-chains and switch statements, predict and fix</p>\n<p>fall-through bugs, and know why <code>switch</code> never matches a value of the wrong</p>\n<p>type. Module 2.4 (Loop Control and Nested Loops) reuses <code>break</code>, this time as</p>\n<p>a loop-exit: flag the difference explicitly when it's re-taught there.</p>\n\n<h2>Lesson count</h2>\n<p><strong>31 lessons</strong> (<code>2-3-1-slides</code> … <code>2-3-30-challenges</code>, titled <code>2.3.1</code>–<code>2.3.31</code>).</p>\n<p>5 pre-existing, 26 added in this pass: full mapping and rationale in the</p>\n<p>2026-08-16 build report (see git history for this file's companion commit).</p>\n\n<h2>What this build added</h2>\n<p>Rebuilt the module from 5 lessons (a thin slice of book §2.3's six</p>\n<p>subsections and 1,024 lines) to 31, using the two-pass method: Pass A mapped</p>\n<p>every book element in §2.3.1–§2.3.6 to at least one lesson; Pass B added the</p>\n<p>worked examples, predict-then-run labs, and bug-fix drills the book doesn't</p>\n<p>supply but the lesson/lab/challenge cycle needs. Highlights:</p>\n\n<p>- A dedicated reading + worked example + two labs for fall-through: the</p>\n<p>  book's own #1 flagged bug: instead of one paragraph.</p>\n<p>- The two book-given fixes for the strict-equality dead-case bug (match the</p>\n<p>  case type vs. convert with <code>Number()</code>) split into their own worked</p>\n<p>  examples, since they're genuinely different techniques.</p>\n<p>- Grouping cases gets a reading, two worked examples (weekend/weekday, then</p>\n<p>  the denser days-in-month), and a lab, rather than one combined lesson.</p>\n<p>- <code>2-3-29-a2-3-1-print-settings-advisor</code> is the real <strong>A2.3.1</strong> artifact</p>\n<p>  from <code>curriculum-plan.md</code> §2.3 (filament-type switch, 4 cases + default,</p>\n<p>  temperature + speed per case, fall-through comment). The pre-existing</p>\n<p>  <code>2-3-4-lab-convert-to-switch</code> was NOT A2.3.1: it's kept as ungraded</p>\n<p>  practice ahead of the real assignment, renumbered to <code>2.3.29</code>.</p>\n<p>- <code>2-3-30-challenges</code> follows the console-track challenge pattern</p>\n<p>  (<code>type: \"assignment\"</code>, <code>difficulty: \"beginner\"</code>, <code>preview: \"console\"</code>) per</p>\n<p>  the established inconsistency documented in</p>\n<p>  <code>lab-assignment-conventions.md</code> §7.7, not the moshion-challenge pattern.</p>\n\n\n<h2>Book coverage (built §2.3, checked 2026-09-05)</h2>\n\n<p>Every subsection of the built book section has at least one lesson. Lesson numbers</p>\n<p>are <code>title</code> numbers (<code>2.3.N</code>), not folder slugs.</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Book §2.3 subsection</th><th>Lessons</th></tr>\n<tr><td>2.3.1 One Value, Many Answers</td><td>2.3.2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.3.2 The `switch` Statement</td><td>2.3.3, 2.3.4, 2.3.5, 2.3.6, 2.3.7, 2.3.8, 2.3.10</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.3.3 `break` and Fall-Through</td><td>2.3.9, 2.3.11, 2.3.12, 2.3.13</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.3.4 Grouping Cases</td><td>2.3.14, 2.3.15, 2.3.16, 2.3.17</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.3.5 `switch` Compares Strictly</td><td>2.3.18, 2.3.19, 2.3.20, 2.3.21, 2.3.22</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.3.6 Choosing Between `if...else` and `switch`</td><td>2.3.23, 2.3.24, 2.3.25, 2.3.26, 2.3.27</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>shCode-designed, anchored to no single subsection: <code>2.3.1</code> (deck), <code>2.3.28</code>, <code>2.3.29</code></p>\n<p>(practice ahead of the artifact), <code>2.3.30</code> (<strong>A2.3.1</strong>), <code>2.3.31</code> (challenges).</p>\n\n<h2>Note</h2>\n<p>Split out of the former combined \"1.2 Control Flow\" unit spec during the</p>\n<p>2026-08-12 book-native renumber (book §2.3 The Switch Statement). In the</p>\n<p>built lesson sequence, switch is taught directly after conditionals (module</p>\n<p>2.1) rather than after algorithms/loops (module 2.2): content order was kept</p>\n<p>as originally taught per an explicit decision; only the module <em>number</em></p>\n<p>follows the book's own section order.</p>\n"},"2.4":{"title":"Loop Control and Nested Loops","html":"\n<h1>Module 2.4: Loop Control and Nested Loops</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 1 · Weeks 6–7 · Environment: browser DevTools console</strong></p>\n\n<h2>SLO Focus</h2>\n<p>- Reinforces <strong>SLO 3</strong>.</p>\n\n<h2>Module Goal</h2>\n<p>Students can choose between <code>for</code>/<code>while</code>/<code>do...while</code>, control a loop's flow with <code>break</code> and <code>continue</code>, diagnose an infinite loop, and write and trace a nested loop.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Module 2.1 (Conditionals), 2.2 (Algorithms and Loops), 2.3 (The switch Statement) complete. No functions, arrays, or try/catch: those are §3.1, §3.3, and §2.5 respectively.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Students can write any of the three loop forms, control their flow with <code>break</code>/<code>continue</code>, and reason about nested-loop cost and scope: the last console-track prerequisite before try/catch (§2.5) and functions (§3.1).</p>\n\n\n<h2>Book coverage (built §2.4, checked 2026-09-05)</h2>\n\n<p>Every subsection of the built book section has at least one lesson. Lesson numbers</p>\n<p>are <code>title</code> numbers (<code>2.4.N</code>), not folder slugs.</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Book §2.4 subsection</th><th>Lessons</th></tr>\n<tr><td>2.4.1 Choosing a Loop: `for` vs `while`</td><td>2.4.2, 2.4.3, 2.4.4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.4.2 The `do...while` Loop</td><td>2.4.5, 2.4.6, 2.4.7, 2.4.8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.4.3 Leaving Early with `break`</td><td>2.4.9, 2.4.10, 2.4.11, 2.4.12</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.4.4 Skipping a Round with `continue`</td><td>2.4.13, 2.4.14, 2.4.15, 2.4.16, 2.4.17, 2.4.18, 2.4.19</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.4.5 Infinite Loops</td><td>2.4.20, 2.4.21, 2.4.22</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.4.6 Nested Loops</td><td>2.4.24, 2.4.25, 2.4.26, 2.4.27, 2.4.28, 2.4.29, 2.4.30, 2.4.31</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>shCode-designed, anchored to no single subsection: <code>2.4.1</code> (deck), <code>2.4.23</code></p>\n<p>(<strong>A2.4.1</strong>), <code>2.4.32</code> (<strong>A2.4.2</strong>), <code>2.4.33</code> (challenges).</p>\n\n<p>This table regenerates from the lessons: 29 <code>sourceRef</code> strings were retagged on</p>\n<p>2026-09-05 from <code>book 2.4: ...</code> to the subsection form <code>book 2.4.N: ...</code> already used</p>\n<p>by modules 2.1, 2.3 and 2.5, and the regenerated table matches the rows above exactly.</p>\n<p>Read the first <code>2.4.N</code> in a <code>sourceRef</code> that is not preceded by Example / Try It Now /</p>\n<p>Definition / Problem Set: that is the lesson's book anchor.</p>\n\n<h2>Build note (2026-08-16)</h2>\n<p>33 lessons, <code>2-4-1-slides</code> through <code>2-4-33-challenges</code>, across two class meetings</p>\n<p>(week 6: for-vs-while through infinite loops, ending at <strong>A2.4.1</strong>; week 7: nested</p>\n<p>loops, ending at <strong>A2.4.2</strong>, then Challenges). Built fresh: no prior lesson folders</p>\n<p>in this section.</p>\n\n<p>Book §2.4's Example 2.3 (\"Filtering while you loop\") uses an array, which is</p>\n<p>book §3.3 and outside this section's prerequisite ceiling. <code>2-4-14-example-filtering-while-loop</code></p>\n<p>adapts the same filtering idea onto a numeric range instead: see its <code>sourceRef</code>.</p>\n<p>No other book element required a forward reference.</p>\n\n<p>Five lessons are <code>preview: \"diagram\"</code>: do...while, break, continue, an</p>\n<p>infinite-loop repair, and nested loops each get a chart, continuing the</p>\n<p>diagram-native pattern module 2.2 established. <code>break</code>'s relationship to the</p>\n<p><code>switch</code>-context <code>break</code> taught in module 2.3 is called out explicitly in</p>\n<p><code>2-4-9-reading-break</code>.</p>\n"},"2.5":{"title":"Handling Errors with try/catch","html":"\n<h1>Module 2.5: Handling Errors with try/catch</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 1 · Week 7 · Environment: browser DevTools console</strong></p>\n\n<h2>SLO Focus</h2>\n<p>- Reinforces <strong>SLO 3</strong> (design/implement/test robust programs): error handling is</p>\n<p>  the \"test against bad input\" half of that SLO, deepened again in §9.3.</p>\n\n<h2>Module Goal</h2>\n<p>Students can explain what a runtime error does to a running program, tell it apart</p>\n<p>from a syntax error, wrap risky code in <code>try...catch</code>, read <code>err.name</code>/<code>err.message</code>,</p>\n<p>raise their own errors with <code>throw</code> when a value breaks the program's rules, and use</p>\n<p><code>finally</code> for cleanup that must run either way. This is intro-level only: custom</p>\n<p>error types, input-validation design, and the DevTools debugger workflow are §9.3's</p>\n<p>job.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Module 2.4 (Loop Control and Nested Loops) complete. Cumulative toolkit entering this</p>\n<p>module: variables, types, strings, operators, <code>if</code>/<code>else</code>, <code>for</code>/<code>while</code>/<code>do...while</code>,</p>\n<p><code>switch</code>, <code>break</code>/<code>continue</code>, nested loops. No functions, no arrays (chapter 3).</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Students can wrap risky code in <code>try...catch</code>, report <code>err.message</code> instead of</p>\n<p>swallowing an error, throw their own errors when input breaks the program's rules</p>\n<p>(not the language's), and use <code>finally</code> for cleanup. §9.3 deepens this with custom</p>\n<p>error types and input-validation design; §9.3 explicitly does not re-teach basic</p>\n<p><code>try/catch</code> syntax.</p>\n\n<h2>What this module added</h2>\n<p>Built from book §2.5 Handling Errors with try/catch (single meeting, Thu Sep 24,</p>\n<p>2026, week 7: last teaching day of chapter 2 before the Ch 2 Group PA and Ch 2</p>\n<p>Test). 27 lessons: 1 slides, 8 readings, 3 worked examples, 2 diagrams, 11 console</p>\n<p>labs, 1 graded lab (<strong>A2.5.1</strong>, <code>2.5.26</code>), 1 challenges bundle (<code>2.5.27</code>). Lessons</p>\n<p><code>2.5.10</code>/<code>2.5.11</code> exist specifically because the team lead flagged empty-catch-block</p>\n<p>swallowing as a bad habit that must be explicitly taught, not implied: this reframes</p>\n<p>\"red console error\" from \"you broke it\" (chapter 2's running theme) to \"something you</p>\n<p>can plan for.\"</p>\n\n\n<h2>Book coverage (built §2.5, checked 2026-09-05)</h2>\n\n<p>Every subsection of the built book section has at least one lesson. Lesson numbers</p>\n<p>are <code>title</code> numbers (<code>2.5.N</code>), not folder slugs.</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Book §2.5 subsection</th><th>Lessons</th></tr>\n<tr><td>2.5.1 What an Error Actually Does</td><td>2.5.2, 2.5.3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.5.2 Catching an Error with `try...catch`</td><td>2.5.4, 2.5.5, 2.5.8, 2.5.9, 2.5.10, 2.5.11</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.5.3 Reading the Error Object</td><td>2.5.12, 2.5.13, 2.5.14</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.5.4 Throwing Your Own Errors</td><td>2.5.15, 2.5.16, 2.5.17, 2.5.18, 2.5.19, 2.5.20</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.5.5 Cleaning Up with `finally`</td><td>2.5.21, 2.5.22</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.5.6 What `try...catch` Cannot Do</td><td>2.5.23, 2.5.24, 2.5.25</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>shCode-designed, anchored to no single subsection: <code>2.5.1</code> (deck), <code>2.5.6</code> and <code>2.5.7</code></p>\n<p>(practice for §2.5.2's Definition 2.5.2 and Problem Set), <code>2.5.26</code> (<strong>A2.5.1</strong>),</p>\n<p><code>2.5.27</code> (challenges).</p>\n\n<h2>Note</h2>\n<p>First module authored directly against the book-native scheme with no legacy</p>\n<p><code>Q.U.S</code>-era spec to migrate. <code>sourceRef</code> tags on every lesson distinguish Pass A</p>\n<p>(book-sourced) from Pass B (shCode-designed scaffolding) content: see</p>\n<p><code>curriculum/resources/sub-module-spec-conventions.md</code> §3.</p>\n"},"2.6":{"title":"Chapter 2 Group Performance Assessment","html":"\n<h1>Module 2.6: Chapter 2 Group Performance Assessment</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 1 · Week 8 · Mon Sep 28, 2026 · One meeting · Paired · Environment: browser DevTools console</strong></p>\n\n<p>The second Group PA of the year, and the one that collects on a promise the first</p>\n<p>one made. Same shape as 1.6 — one block, in pairs, three thirds: **design, build,</p>\n<p>demo**, with the design third first and neither partner opening an editor until the</p>\n<p>chart is green (Appendix D).</p>\n\n<h2>What Chapter 1's PA promised and this one delivers</h2>\n\n<p>1.6 charted a diamond that the program answered by <em>computing a boolean</em> and printing</p>\n<p>it, because Chapter 1 had not taught a conditional. Its two branches were pseudocode</p>\n<p>comments. Its own text said Chapter 2's Group PA is where they become code.</p>\n\n<p>They become code here. Three things change, and nothing else about the shape does:</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th></th><th>1.6 (Sep 1)</th><th>2.6 (Sep 28)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>The diamond</td><td>computes a boolean, prints it; branches are comments</td><td>a real `if` / `else if` / `else`</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The program's shape</td><td>a straight line</td><td>a loop with a decision inside it</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chart shapes in play</td><td>oval, rectangle, diamond</td><td>+ the **loop-setup hexagon** (§2.2)</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>What it assesses</h2>\n\n<p>Everything in Chapter 2 at once. No section is assessed twice and none is left out:</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Section</th><th>Where it shows up</th></tr>\n<tr><td>2.1 Conditionals</td><td>The decision inside the loop — a real `if`/`else if`/`else`, with one `&&` or `\\</td><td>\\</td><td>`</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.2 Algorithms and Loops</td><td>The loop that walks the input; the hexagon that sets it up on the chart</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.3 The switch Statement</td><td>The classification step — grouped cases, `break` on every one</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.4 Loop Control and Nested Loops</td><td>The `break` or `continue` the brief forces, named in the demo — one each in Locker Sweep, Word Audit and Coin Drawer; Savings Run forces both</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.5 Handling Errors with try/catch</td><td>A guard around the one input that can be bad, reporting `err.message`</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>Chapter 1's five sections met the program in five different registers — one was a</p>\n<p>naming convention, one was a paradigm the demo had to <em>name</em>. Chapter 2's five all</p>\n<p>turn into executable lines. That is why the build is bigger than 1.6's at the same</p>\n<p>25/50/25, and it is the pressure the timing below has to absorb.</p>\n\n<h2>Weights</h2>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Third</th><th>Weight</th><th>Lesson</th></tr>\n<tr><td>**Design** — the flowchart, drawn together, all checks green</td><td>25%</td><td>2.6.1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**Build** — the program, documented to the style guide</td><td>50%</td><td>2.6.2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**Demo** — run it, test it, explain it</td><td>25%</td><td>2.6.3</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>Unchanged from 1.6 on purpose. The in-app lessons are green-to-advance like every</p>\n<p>other lesson in the course; the 25/50/25 is how the three artifacts are weighted in</p>\n<p>the gradebook, not something the checker computes.</p>\n\n<blockquote>**Do not mark these lessons `summative`.** The chapter *test* (2.7) sets the flag on</blockquote>\n<blockquote>all five of its parts; the Group PA deliberately does not, and 1.6's three lessons</blockquote>\n<blockquote>set it nowhere. Green-to-advance is the whole mechanism here — 2.6.1's chart is</blockquote>\n<blockquote>supposed to gate 2.6.2, and `summative` exists precisely to switch that gating off.</blockquote>\n<blockquote>The gate now agrees: `scripts/check-summative-parts.mjs` keys test units on the unit</blockquote>\n<blockquote>name matching `N.7 Chapter N Individual Performance Assessment`, so a stray flag on</blockquote>\n<blockquote>one 2.6 lesson would not cascade today — but the pedagogy has not changed, and</blockquote>\n<blockquote>neither will the next person's temptation to copy-paste from 2.7. Leave all three</blockquote>\n<blockquote>flagless.</blockquote>\n\n<h2>Timing for one 1.75-hour block</h2>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Minutes</th><th>What</th></tr>\n<tr><td>0–8</td><td>Pairs form, pick one problem from the menu of four, read the brief</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8–33</td><td>**Design.** Pseudocode on paper, then the chart in 2.6.1. Editors stay closed.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>33–75</td><td>**Build.** 2.6.2. One screen, partners swap the keyboard at least once.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>75–95</td><td>**Demo — Gallery Walk.** Two waves (8 hosts / 7 visitors). Each visitor sees 3 stations × ~3 min; teacher circulates with rubric and samples 4–5 live.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>95–105</td><td>Slack.</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>**The design third stays at 25 minutes and the build takes its two extra from the</p>\n<p>pick-a-problem window**, which can afford them — the menu format is familiar by now</p>\n<p>and the pairs are not new.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The demo is a gallery walk, not a serial parade.</strong> Two waves (8 hosts / 7 visitors) fit in 20 minutes with ~80 s per station; the teacher circulates with the rubric and samples 4–5 live demos. This is the first PA where programs are complex enough that peer review is valuable — 1.6's straight-line programs were too simple to benefit.</p>\n\n<p>The design third does not get shortened, and the reason is worth stating because the</p>\n<p>opposite looks true. §2.2 nominally gated a loop chart: the plan specifies <strong>A2.2.0</strong></p>\n<p>as \"chart the counting-or-accumulation problem… using the loop-setup hexagon… all</p>\n<p>eight structural checks,\" and it is the module's graded flowchart gate. **As shipped</p>\n<p>it charts a decision, not a loop.** <code>2-2-12-a5-2-flowchart-decision</code> is titled</p>\n<p><code>2.2.7 Flowchart a Decision</code>, its rules are <code>min-decisions</code> with no loop floor, and</p>\n<p>its own <code>sourceRef</code> records the divergence: *\"plan text describes charting the</p>\n<p>loop/hexagon counting problem… but this lesson charts a decision instead: mismatch</p>\n<p>flagged in build report, kept as shipped.\"*</p>\n\n<p>So the only place a student has drawn a loop on a chart is</p>\n<p><code>2-2-6a-chart-the-for-loop</code> (<code>2.2.11 Chart the Code: A for-loop That Accumulates</code>),</p>\n<p>a Tier-1 chart-the-code exercise, not a graded gate. **2.6.1 is therefore the first</p>\n<p>time a loop chart is graded at all**, which is an argument for the full 25 minutes</p>\n<p>and for reading these charts closely, not an argument that the shape is old news.</p>\n\n<h2>Why the flowchart is 25% and still comes first</h2>\n\n<p>Same reason as 1.6, one turn harder. Two students who disagree about what happens</p>\n<p>next have to settle it on the diagram before either can type — and in Chapter 2 the</p>\n<p>disagreement is no longer only <em>what</em> happens next but <em>how many times</em>. A pair that</p>\n<p>cannot say where the loop ends on paper will not discover it in code; they will</p>\n<p>discover an infinite loop at minute 60.</p>\n\n<p>A chart drawn after the code reads exactly like one and is graded as one</p>\n<p>(Appendix D §D.7).</p>\n\n<h2>What this deliberately does not ask for</h2>\n\n<p><strong>No functions, no arrays, no objects.</strong> Those are Chapter 3. The program is one</p>\n<p>console script, top to bottom, with a loop in the middle.</p>\n\n<p><strong>No <code>[[ ]]</code> double-rail on the chart.</strong> The subroutine shape is released at §3.1</p>\n<p>(Appendix D §D.2). A pair that finds the chart outgrowing a page has over-sized a</p>\n<p><code>process</code> rectangle, not discovered a need for decomposition — say so rather than</p>\n<p>releasing the shape early.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Iteration without arrays is the real constraint here</strong>, and all four problems below</p>\n<p>respect it. Chapter 2 can walk exactly two things: numbers generated by the loop</p>\n<p>counter, and the characters of a string via <code>word[i]</code> and <code>word.length</code></p>\n<p>(<code>2-2-17a-example-walk-a-word</code>, with <code>.length</code> from 1.2.14). Any brief that needs a</p>\n<p>list of values has to get them from a counter or a string. There is no third option</p>\n<p>until §3.3.</p>\n\n<h2>The checks on 2.6.1, and why they are not 1.6.1's</h2>\n\n<p>**2.6.1 must not inherit 1.6.1's rule block unchanged, and the course has already</p>\n<p>worked out what to use instead.**</p>\n\n<p><code>min-process</code> counts <code>shape === 'process'</code> and nothing else</p>\n<p>(<code>lib/diagram-check.ts:394</code>). A hexagon is <code>preparation</code>, a parallelogram is <code>io</code>,</p>\n<p>and neither is a rectangle. 1.6.1 and 1.7.4 both set <code>min-process: 3</code>, which is a</p>\n<p>floor a Chapter 1 chart could not avoid clearing — oval, rectangle and diamond were</p>\n<p>the entire vocabulary, so every step that was not Start, End or the comparison <em>had</em></p>\n<p>to be a rectangle.</p>\n\n<p>A Chapter 2 chart spends shapes elsewhere. Loop setup goes in the hexagon this module</p>\n<p>introduces; a pair that also reaches for <code>io</code> on \"get the number\" and \"report the</p>\n<p>total\" is left with the accumulate step and possibly one other. **The better chart</p>\n<p>fails the floor** — the 1.6 audit's parallelogram trap (<code>d48ef998</code>: \"offered and then</p>\n<p>silently punished\") pointed at the one shape this PA exists to teach.</p>\n\n<p>This is not a judgement call. Surveying every diagram lesson's rule block:</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Where</th><th>`min-process`</th><th>`min-decisions`</th><th>`min-nodes`</th></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-6-1` Ch 1 Group PA chart</td><td>**3**</td><td>1</td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`1-7-4` Ch 1 Test chart</td><td>**3**</td><td>1</td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`2-2-6a` chart the `for` loop</td><td>2</td><td>—</td><td>**6**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`2-4-7` chart `do…while`</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`2-4-15` chart `continue`</td><td>—</td><td>1</td><td>**5**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`2-4-26` chart nested loops</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>**5**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`3-4-20` chart forms comparison</td><td>**3**</td><td>2</td><td>**6**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`4-1-3a` Print Shop design chart</td><td>—</td><td>1</td><td>**10**</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p><code>min-process: 3</code> exists in exactly <strong>three</strong> lessons in the course (<code>1-6-1</code>, <code>1-7-4</code>, and <code>3-4-20-chart-forms-comparison</code> — add this row to the table above). <strong>From §2.2 onward the floor is <code>min-nodes</code></strong>, which counts</p>\n<p>every flow shape regardless of kind (<code>isFlowShape</code> excludes only <code>comment</code> —</p>\n<p><code>lib/diagram-types.ts:62</code>), so Start and End count and no shape choice can dodge it.</p>\n<p>That is the whole reason it replaced <code>min-process</code>: it measures whether the chart is</p>\n<p>bigger than a straight line without dictating which shapes make it so.</p>\n\n<p><strong>What 2.6.1 should carry</strong>, sitting between §2.2's loop chart and §4.1's</p>\n<p>project design chart because that is what it is:</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Rule</th><th>Value</th><th>Why</th></tr>\n<tr><td>`min-nodes`</td><td>**8**</td><td>§2.2's loop chart is 6, §4.1's design chart is 10. A PA chart is input + loop setup + a decision + the body + a classification + a report + two terminals</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`min-process`</td><td>**2**</td><td>§2.2's and §2.4's value. Keeps the rectangle from vanishing entirely without punishing the hexagon</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`min-decisions`</td><td>1</td><td>Unchanged from 1.6.1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`no-self-loop`</td><td>keep</td><td>See **Still open** — untested against a tight `while`</td></tr>\n<tr><td>the other six</td><td>keep</td><td>Unchanged</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>Ten rules either way; <code>min-nodes</code> takes the weight <code>min-process</code> used to carry.</p>\n\n<p><strong>And say so in the lesson text</strong>, the way 1.6.1 was made to after the audit — its</p>\n<p>own wording is *\"the checker counts rectangles — a parallelogram is not one… never in</p>\n<p>place of a rectangle.\"* The Chapter 2 version has to add the hexagon to that</p>\n<p>sentence. A rule a student cannot see is a trap whether or not it was meant as one.</p>\n\n<h2>The menu</h2>\n\n<p>Four problems, matched in difficulty, all the same shape: **take one or two inputs,</p>\n<p>walk something with a loop, decide inside the loop, classify one result with a</p>\n<p><code>switch</code>, guard the input that can be bad, report a running total and a verdict.**</p>\n<p>Full statements are in 2.6.1.</p>\n\n<p>1. <strong>Locker Sweep</strong> — walk locker numbers <code>1..n</code>, flip each on a rule, <code>continue</code></p>\n<p>   past the ones the rule skips, classify one locker's final state — open, closed, or</p>\n<p>   never touched — with <code>switch</code>, guard a bad <code>n</code></p>\n<p>2. <strong>Word Audit</strong> — walk a phrase character by character, <code>continue</code> past the spaces,</p>\n<p>   sort each remaining character into vowel / digit / other with <code>switch</code>, count all</p>\n<p>   three, <code>throw</code> on an empty string</p>\n<p>3. <strong>Savings Run</strong> — loop weeks, add a deposit, skip the weeks a rule excludes with</p>\n<p>   <code>continue</code>, <code>break</code> the moment the target is hit, classify the outcome — hit early,</p>\n<p>   hit on the last week, or missed — with <code>switch</code>, guard a negative target</p>\n<p>4. <strong>Coin Drawer</strong> — a <code>while</code> loop making change from an amount, <code>switch</code> naming</p>\n<p>   each denomination, <code>break</code> when the drawer runs dry, guard a negative amount</p>\n\n<p>Pairs at adjacent tables should not pick the same one.</p>\n\n<p>Every one of the four has a <code>break</code> or a <code>continue</code> in it that the brief forces</p>\n<p>rather than suggests — §2.4 is the section a PA most easily assesses by accident,</p>\n<p>and \"there is a loop in it\" is not evidence a pair understands loop <em>control</em>.</p>\n\n<h2>The checklist's honest limits (measured 2026-09-18)</h2>\n\n<p>Three student lenses worked the built block. The advanced lens built a 15-line</p>\n<p>program that satisfied all ten checks while solving nothing — a single `continue;</p>\n<p>break;` pair and a two-case switch. Two fixes landed: r8 now demands <strong>both</strong> a</p>\n<p><code>break</code> and a <code>continue</code> in the loop, and r9 demands **three cases with breaks plus</p>\n<p>a default** (matching the brief's own three-outcome wording). A token-matching</p>\n<p>checklist verifies the presence of syntax, never the program's shape — a pair that</p>\n<p>games it will produce a program that runs and says nothing true. The demo writeup</p>\n<p>is the defense: Part 2's three test cases and Part 5's section-mapping are where a</p>\n<p>paper program shows what it actually does. Read those before you read the code.</p>\n\n<h2>Teacher notes</h2>\n\n<p>- <strong>Circulate during the design third, not the build third.</strong> Unchanged from 1.6 and</p>\n<p>  still the note that matters most.</p>\n<p>- <strong>A chart with no hexagon is the failure to look for, and no check will catch it.</strong></p>\n<p>  The structural checks judge legality, and Appendix D §D.3 lets an assignment add</p>\n<p>  only <code>min-decisions</code>, <code>min-process</code>, <code>min-nodes</code> and <code>no-self-loop</code> — there is no</p>\n<p>  floor that can require a loop. A pair can go ten-for-ten green on a straight-line</p>\n<p>  chart that ignores the whole chapter. Read the shapes yourself. See <strong>Still open</strong>.</p>\n<p>- <strong>The one failure mode is still a silent partner.</strong> The demo writeup asks who drove</p>\n<p>  which third by name. Read that answer before you read the program.</p>\n<p>- **A pair that finishes early has not finished — but ask the right question for</p>\n<p>  their problem.** The zero-iteration input is the one to chase, because a loop that</p>\n<p>  never enters is invisible where Chapter 1's straight line always ran. It is not the</p>\n<p>  same question on all four:</p>\n<p>  - <strong>Savings Run</strong> (<code>weeks = 0</code>) and <strong>Coin Drawer</strong> (<code>amount = 0</code>) have a genuine</p>\n<p>    zero-iteration input their guard lets through — the guard rejects a <em>negative</em></p>\n<p>    target or amount, so zero is legal and the loop simply never runs. Ask for it.</p>\n<p>  - <strong>Locker Sweep</strong> (<code>n = 0</code>) and <strong>Word Audit</strong> (the empty phrase) do <strong>not</strong>. Their</p>\n<p>    zero-iteration input is precisely the input their guard throws on, so a pair that</p>\n<p>    built the guard has already answered it. Ask them the boundary question instead:</p>\n<p>    what happens at <code>n = 1</code>, or at a phrase that is nothing but spaces — which walks</p>\n<p>    the loop and <code>continue</code>s past every character, reaching the report with all three</p>\n<p>    counts at zero and no error thrown.</p>\n<p>- <strong>Hand this back as the study guide on Sep 28.</strong> Every item type on the Ch 2 Test</p>\n<p>  two days later has an analogue in the program the pair just built. Same policy as</p>\n<p>  1.6 → 1.7, and it is not a secret.</p>\n\n<p>- <strong>Absent-partner policy:</strong> the present member proceeds through all three thirds;</p>\n<p>  the pair artifact and observed contribution are graded. The absent member completes</p>\n<p>  an adjusted-scope solo make-up at a scheduled time; the present partner's grade is</p>\n<p>  never affected by the make-up.</p>\n\n<p>- <strong>Odd-class trio policy:</strong> one trio is permitted; roles (driver/navigator/recorder)</p>\n<p>  rotate every 20 minutes; they produce one chart, one program, and the demo names</p>\n<p>  all three and who drove which third. The 25/50/25 weights apply to the trio's</p>\n<p>  single artifact.</p>\n\n<h2>Still open</h2>\n\n<p>- **<code>switch (true)</code> is not taught, which is why every classification names three</p>\n<p>  outcomes.*<em> Verified: the string appears nowhere in <code>lessons/2-</em></code>. §2.3 teaches</p>\n<p>  <code>switch</code> on a value matched by strict equality, so a range test — \"is the balance</p>\n<p>  past the target\" — cannot be a <code>switch</code> case at all; it has to be resolved into a</p>\n<p>  value first, by <code>if</code>/<code>else if</code> or a ternary, and the <code>switch</code> then classifies that</p>\n<p>  value. Two consequences the briefs above were rewritten to respect: a two-outcome</p>\n<p>  classification is an <code>if</code>/<code>else</code> by §2.3.23's own teaching, so a brief asking for a</p>\n<p>  <code>switch</code> over open/closed or hit/missed is asking for the thing the chapter tells</p>\n<p>  students not to write; and Coin Drawer's <code>switch</code> is genuinely denomination-shaped</p>\n<p>  (four cases plus a default) while Locker Sweep's and Savings Run's only become</p>\n<p>  switch-shaped once the third outcome is named. **If 2.6.1 restates any of the four</p>\n<p>  with two outcomes, it reintroduces this.**</p>\n<p>- <strong>§2.2's graded flowchart gate does not chart a loop.</strong> Detailed under <em>Timing</em></p>\n<p>  above. <code>curriculum-plan.md</code> §2.2 specifies A2.2.0 as a hexagon/loop chart gating</p>\n<p>  A2.2.1; the shipped <code>2-2-12-a5-2-flowchart-decision</code> charts a decision and says so</p>\n<p>  in its own <code>sourceRef</code>. Either the plan's A2.2.0 text is wrong, or the module is</p>\n<p>  missing the gate the plan promised — and if it is the latter, 2.6.1 and 2.7.4 are</p>\n<p>  both grading a shape that was never gated. Resolve before Ch 3 adds the double-rail</p>\n<p>  on top of it.</p>\n<p>- <strong>The hexagon cannot be auto-required.</strong> Flagged above. Two ways out: add a</p>\n<p>  <code>min-preparation</code> floor to the checker alongside the four in Appendix D §D.3, or</p>\n<p>  accept that the loop is graded by eye and write that into the 2.6.1 rubric. This is</p>\n<p>  the first assessment where the gap bites, and it will bite again on every chapter</p>\n<p>  from here — §3.1's double-rail has the same problem.</p>\n<p>- <strong><code>no-self-loop</code> against a tight <code>while</code>, and <code>reaches-end</code> against any cycle.</strong></p>\n<p>  2.6.1 keeps <code>no-self-loop</code> (see the rule table above), but a <code>while</code> charted</p>\n<p>  without a hexagon — condition diamond, body, arrow straight back to the diamond — is legal flowcharting</p>\n<p>  and is not a self-loop, while a single-rectangle body that arrows to itself is.</p>\n<p>  <code>2-2-6a-chart-the-for-loop</code> carries a back-edge under ten rules — the same set as</p>\n<p>  1.6.1 but with <code>min-nodes</code> where 1.6.1 has <code>min-decisions</code> — and</p>\n<p>  <code>lib/diagram-mermaid.ts</code>'s <code>layout()</code></p>\n<p>  is documented as giving back edges no vote on rank or column, so cycles are handled</p>\n<p>  — but **no lesson has yet run a loop chart under <code>min-decisions</code> plus</p>\n<p>  <code>no-self-loop</code> together**, which is what 2.6.1 needs. Draw both the hexagon form and</p>\n<p>  the tight <code>while</code> form into the 2.6.1 solution and run them through the checker</p>\n<p>  before the day.</p>\n"},"2.7":{"title":"Chapter 2 Individual Performance Assessment","html":"\n<h1>Module 2.7: Chapter 2 Individual Performance Assessment</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 1 · Week 8 · Wed Sep 30, 2026 · One meeting · Individual · ~53 minutes</strong></p>\n\n<p>The individual half of the chapter's assessment, and the sibling of the Group PA two</p>\n<p>days before it: same chapter, same week, same in-app shape, sat alone. Five lessons,</p>\n<p>17 items, 100 points — the identical skeleton to 1.7, with one thing genuinely new.</p>\n\n<h2>The one thing that is new: Part D is JavaScript</h2>\n\n<p>1.7's Part D was a chart and pseudocode, and the reason was stated plainly there —</p>\n<p>Chapter 1 had not taught a conditional, so a test that asked for working code would</p>\n<p>have been testing shCode rather than the course.</p>\n\n<p>Chapter 2 has taught conditionals, three loop forms, <code>switch</code>, <code>break</code>/<code>continue</code> and</p>\n<p><code>try</code>/<code>catch</code>. <strong>D2 is real, runnable JavaScript from here on</strong>, on this test and on</p>\n<p>every chapter test after it. That is the whole difference between the two papers; the</p>\n<p>parts, the weights, the forms mechanism, the test mode and the no-retake rule are all</p>\n<p>carried over unchanged.</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th></th><th>1.7 (Sep 3)</th><th>2.7 (Sep 30)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>D1</td><td>chart, three shapes</td><td>chart, **hexagon legal** (§2.2)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D2</td><td>pseudocode for the charted problem</td><td>**JavaScript**, including one nested loop</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Code written on the paper</td><td>none</td><td>one working snippet</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ceiling</td><td>no conditionals</td><td>no functions, no arrays, no objects</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Why this is not the Group PA again, alone</h2>\n\n<p>Unchanged from 1.7, and worth restating because the answer has not moved: the PA</p>\n<p>(2.6) already proves a pair can produce. What a paired build cannot tell you is which</p>\n<p>of the two produced it.</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th></th><th>2.6 Group PA (Sep 28)</th><th>2.7 Test (Sep 30)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Who</td><td>pairs</td><td>individual</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Length</td><td>~87 min over three lessons</td><td>~53 min over five</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The verb</td><td>**build** one thing end to end</td><td>**read · diagnose · design · write**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Code written</td><td>a whole documented program</td><td>one snippet, in Part D2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Flowchart</td><td>25 min, drawn together, gates the build</td><td>7 min, drawn alone, gates the code</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Coverage</td><td>all five modules through one artifact</td><td>all five modules as independent items</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The failure it catches</td><td>cannot turn a plan into a program</td><td>cannot read a loop, cannot name a bug, **was carried**</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>Tracing and diagnosis remain the two verbs the PA structurally cannot isolate, and in</p>\n<p>Chapter 2 they are worth more than they were in Chapter 1: a student who cannot trace</p>\n<p>a loop cannot tell an infinite loop from a slow one, and that is the bug this chapter</p>\n<p>produces most.</p>\n\n<h2>Coverage and weights</h2>\n\n<p>17 items. Every module of the chapter appears in at least three places.</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Part</th><th>Lesson</th><th>Items</th><th>Points</th><th>Minutes</th></tr>\n<tr><td>**A** Concepts (multiple choice)</td><td>2.7.1</td><td>3</td><td>15</td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**B** Trace the code</td><td>2.7.1</td><td>5</td><td>25</td><td>16</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**A** Concepts (short answer)</td><td>2.7.2</td><td>3</td><td>10</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**C** Find and fix, name the type</td><td>2.7.3</td><td>4</td><td>20</td><td>11</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**D1** Chart it</td><td>2.7.4</td><td>1</td><td>10</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**D2** Write it</td><td>2.7.5</td><td>1</td><td>20</td><td>12</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td></td><td>**17**</td><td>**100**</td><td>**53**</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>The four-part A/B/C/D weighting the curriculum plan fixes for every chapter test —</p>\n<p>25 / 25 / 20 / 30 — lands exactly, the same way it does in 1.7: the eight equally</p>\n<p>weighted questions in 2.7.1 come to 15 + 25, and Part A's remaining 10 are the short</p>\n<p>answers in 2.7.2.</p>\n\n<p>**Every part keeps 1.7's minutes; D2 alone goes 8 → 12, so the paper is 53 minutes</p>\n<p>rather than 49.** Nothing was shaved to pay for it. Eight minutes was sized for</p>\n<p>pseudocode and D2 is JavaScript with a nested loop in it; taking the four from Part B</p>\n<p>or D1 would trade the two skills this test exists to isolate against the one the PA</p>\n<p>already covered. 53 is still the plan's \"~50 minutes\", and the 105-minute block</p>\n<p>absorbs it — the leftover is the next chapter's first lesson either way.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The ~52 minutes after the test are not idle.</strong> The plan's common format says</p>\n<p>\"the remaining period time is the next chapter's first lesson\", but the calendar</p>\n<p>(<code>curriculum-plan.md:2641</code>) puts §3.1 on Fri Oct 02, two days later. So on Sep 30</p>\n<p>there are ~52 unscheduled minutes. This meeting uses them for:</p>\n<p>- Exam wrapper (5–10 min): students note what they found hardest and what they'll</p>\n<p>  study differently.</p>\n<p>- Errata review (10–15 min): teacher addresses common misconceptions from the test.</p>\n<p>- §3.1 preview (10–15 min): vocabulary and mental model for \"what is a function\"</p>\n<p>  without writing code — no new syntax, just concepts.</p>\n<p>The calendar still places §3.1 on Fri Oct 02; this is a bridge, not an early start.</p>\n\n<p><strong>D1 in particular does not get shortened.</strong> The tempting argument is that students</p>\n<p>have charted loops all chapter and need less time; they have not. §2.2's graded</p>\n<p>flowchart gate charts a <em>decision</em>, not a loop — see 2.6's <em>Timing</em> note on the</p>\n<p>A2.2.0 divergence — so by Sep 30 a student has drawn a loop on a chart exactly</p>\n<p>twice: once in <code>2-2-6a-chart-the-for-loop</code>, once in the 2.6 PA two days ago.</p>\n\n<h3>Where each module is assessed</h3>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Module</th><th>Points</th><th>Where</th></tr>\n<tr><td>2.1 Conditionals</td><td>21</td><td>one trace (`==` truthiness trap), one short answer on a supplied snippet, one bug, the branch in D2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.2 Algorithms and Loops</td><td>24</td><td>one concept MCQ, one trace (`for` with a step ≠ 1), one short answer (`for` vs `while`), the whole of D1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.3 The switch Statement</td><td>15</td><td>one concept MCQ, one trace (fall-through), one bug (missing `break`)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.4 Loop Control and Nested Loops</td><td>22</td><td>one trace (nested loop), one bug (`continue` past the increment), the loop and the nesting in D2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.5 Handling Errors with try/catch</td><td>18</td><td>one concept MCQ, one trace (`try`/`catch`/`finally` order), one short answer, one bug</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>**This spread is even, and that is a contrast worth recording rather than a</p>\n<p>coincidence.** 1.7 ran 44 and 30 points into §1.2 and §1.5 — 74% of the paper in two</p>\n<p>modules, with §1.4 at 8 — and the note there explained why: tracing only worked on</p>\n<p>code Chapter 1 permitted you to run, and Part D was pure design. Neither constraint</p>\n<p>survives Chapter 2. All five of these modules produce runnable, traceable lines, so</p>\n<p>every one of them can carry a trace, a bug and a share of Part D on its own merits.</p>\n<p>The range here is 15 to 24. No module needed padding and none had to be skimped.</p>\n\n<p>The blueprint's named requirements all land: <code>if</code>/<code>else if</code>/<code>else</code> (2.1 trace, D2),</p>\n<p>the truthiness traps (2.1 trace and short answer), <code>for</code> vs <code>while</code> (2.2 short</p>\n<p>answer), <code>switch</code> with correct <code>break</code> placement <strong>and one fall-through question</strong></p>\n<p>(2.3 trace and bug), <code>break</code>/<code>continue</code> (2.4 bug, D2), **one nested-loop trace in</p>\n<p>Part B and one nested-loop write in Part D** (2.4), and a <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code> around code</p>\n<p>that throws (2.5 trace and bug).</p>\n\n<p>**Two of those items need pinning down before they are authored, or they will be</p>\n<p>written wrong.**</p>\n\n<p><strong>The 2.4 bug must be a <code>while</code>, and the word is \"update\", not \"increment\".</strong> In a</p>\n<p><code>for</code> loop <code>continue</code> still runs the third clause, so \"<code>continue</code> past the increment\"</p>\n<p>is not a bug there at all — it is only a bug when the update is a statement in the</p>\n<p>body that <code>continue</code> jumps over, which is a <code>while</code>. The course's own lesson is</p>\n<p><code>2-4-16-reading-continue-while-trap</code>, titled for the loop form precisely because the</p>\n<p>form is the bug. Authoring it as a <code>for</code> produces a snippet with nothing wrong in it.</p>\n\n<p><strong>And that bug hands its own answer to the student, through the runner.</strong> The correct</p>\n<p>snippet never terminates. That is safe — <code>LessonWorkspace</code> runs student code in a</p>\n<p>Worker with <code>RUN_TIMEOUT_MS = 3000</code> and kills it, built deliberately because \"Module</p>\n<p>2.4 teaches infinite loops\" (<code>components/LessonWorkspace.tsx:340-344</code>) — so no tab</p>\n<p>freezes. The problem is what it <em>prints</em>:</p>\n\n<blockquote>Your code was still running after 3 seconds, so it was stopped. That usually means a</blockquote>\n<blockquote>loop never reaches its stopping point — **check that the value in the condition</blockquote>\n<blockquote>actually changes inside the loop.**</blockquote>\n\n<p>That is the repair, named. It is the same giveaway the 2026-09-02 narrowing stripped</p>\n<p>out of Part C's checklist — which now names only the symptom — reappearing one layer</p>\n<p>down, from the platform rather than the lesson, on the one Part C item that can</p>\n<p>trigger it. Either this bug does not go in Part C, or the runner needs a summative</p>\n<p>message that reports the stop without diagnosing it.</p>\n\n<h2>The ceiling this test may not exceed</h2>\n\n<p><strong>No functions, no arrays, no objects.</strong> Those are Chapter 3. Every snippet on this</p>\n<p>paper is a top-level script. <strong>Assumes:</strong> Chapter 1 variables and types, and nothing</p>\n<p>else.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Iteration comes from the counter or from a string.</strong> Same constraint the PA works</p>\n<p>under — <code>word[i]</code> and <code>word.length</code> (<code>2-2-17a-example-walk-a-word</code>) are the only way</p>\n<p>to walk data before §3.3. A Part B trace that iterates a list has silently imported</p>\n<p>Chapter 3.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Five shapes, and this is the first test where a fifth one is legal.</strong> Oval,</p>\n<p>rectangle, diamond, parallelogram — plus the <strong>loop-setup hexagon</strong>, released at §2.2</p>\n<p>(Appendix D §D.2). 1.7 could not <em>require</em> the parallelogram because book exercise</p>\n<p>1.5.11 asks for exactly three; that argument does not extend here, because the rule</p>\n<p>Appendix D §D.2 actually states is about <em>release</em> — \"a shape not yet released may</p>\n<p>not appear on a test or a required deliverable\" — and §2.2 releases the hexagon.</p>\n\n<p><strong>But note what is not backing that up.</strong> The natural second argument — that §2.2's</p>\n<p>own graded gate already required a hexagon, so the test is only asking for what was</p>\n<p>gated — is false: A2.2.0 as shipped charts a decision (see 2.6's <em>Timing</em> note). D1</p>\n<p>may require the hexagon on the release rule alone, and should, because a loop charted</p>\n<p>without one is the failure this part exists to catch. Just be aware that **2.6.1 and</p>\n<p>2.7.4 are the first two places a hexagon is graded**, two days apart, with no graded</p>\n<p>rehearsal behind either.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Not released, must not appear:</strong> the <code>[[ ]]</code> double-rail (§3.1), the connector</p>\n<p>circle and the comment bracket (§4.1).</p>\n\n<p><strong>2.7.4 must not copy 1.7.4's rule block.</strong> 1.7.4 sets <code>min-process: 3</code> +</p>\n<p><code>min-decisions: 1</code> and no <code>min-nodes</code> — correct for a chart whose only shapes were</p>\n<p>oval, rectangle and diamond, and wrong the moment a hexagon is legal, because</p>\n<p><code>min-process</code> counts rectangles only (<code>lib/diagram-check.ts:394</code>) and a student who</p>\n<p>charts the loop properly can end up with two. 2.6 sets out the survey and the</p>\n<p>replacement in full; <strong>2.7.4 takes the same block as 2.6.1</strong> — <code>min-nodes: 8</code>,</p>\n<p><code>min-process: 2</code>, <code>min-decisions: 1</code>, the other seven unchanged. Identical rules on</p>\n<p>both charts is also the fairer arrangement when the PA is two days earlier and is</p>\n<p>being handed back as the study guide.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The printed syntax reference needs rebuilding for this chapter.</strong> Ch 1's sheet was</p>\n<p>declarations and the primitive types. Ch 2's is: the three loop forms, <code>break</code> and</p>\n<p><code>continue</code>, <code>switch</code>/<code>case</code>/<code>default</code>/<code>break</code>, <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code>/<code>finally</code>/<code>throw</code>, the</p>\n<p>comparison and logical operators, and the Appendix D shape table including the</p>\n<p>hexagon. Beginners are assessed on whether they can reason, not on whether they</p>\n<p>memorised where <code>default</code> goes.</p>\n\n<h2>Three forms, assigned automatically</h2>\n\n<p>Unchanged mechanism, restated so the module stands alone.</p>\n\n<pre><code>\n2.7.1 quiz ── variants: [\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"] ── hash(lessonId + student email)\n                                              │\n        ┌─────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────┐\n        ▼                                     ▼                     ▼\n   3 common concepts               3 common concepts       3 common concepts\n   + 5 traces (form a)             + 5 traces (form b)     + 5 traces (form c)\n        └──────── questions and options shuffled per student ───────┘\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Eighteen questions authored; each student is asked eight. The five traces are the</p>\n<p>same five <em>shapes</em> on every form — a <code>==</code> truthiness trap, a <code>for</code> with a step other</p>\n<p>than 1, a <code>switch</code> fall-through, a nested loop, a <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code>/<code>finally</code> print order</p>\n<p>— with different names and numbers, so the forms are genuinely equivalent.</p>\n<p><code>lib/quiz-variant.ts</code> does the assignment; <code>scripts/test-quiz.mjs</code> refuses to build a</p>\n<p>quiz whose forms come out different lengths.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Vary the numbers, never the trip.</strong> A trace whose inner loop changes from <code>i</code> to</p>\n<p><code>i + 1</code> is a different question wearing the same shape, and that is the easy mistake</p>\n<p>when authoring three forms of a loop rather than three forms of a straight line.</p>\n\n<p>**But \"vary the numbers\" does not survive contact with the nested loop, and this is</p>\n<p>the one place 1.7's mechanism does not simply carry over.** Chapter 1's five traces</p>\n<p>were straight-line snippets: change the numbers and the student still reads the same</p>\n<p>three or four lines, so workload was independent of the values. A nested loop's</p>\n<p>workload <em>is</em> the values — 3 × 2 is six lines to write out and 4 × 2 is eight. Three</p>\n<p>forms varying the bounds are the same skill at measurably different cost, on a timed</p>\n<p>paper, assigned by a hash the student did not choose.</p>\n\n<p>So for this trace **hold the product constant and vary the names and the body</p>\n<p>expression instead.** Six lines factors only two non-degenerate ways (3 × 2 and</p>\n<p>2 × 3), so the third form has to come from the body — <code>row + \"-\" + col</code> on one,</p>\n<p><code>row * col</code> on another — not from a third pair of bounds. A triangle</p>\n<p>(<code>for (let j = 1; j &lt;= i; j++)</code>) is <em>not</em> a third form: the inner bound depending on</p>\n<p>the outer is a different trip by the rule above.</p>\n\n<p><code>scripts/test-quiz.mjs</code> refuses forms of different <strong>length</strong>. It cannot see any of</p>\n<p>this, so it is an authoring discipline and nothing more.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The other four lessons are single-form, deliberately</strong>, for the reason 1.7 gives:</p>\n<p>Part C's answer is typed into a file rather than picked from a list, and D1/D2 offer</p>\n<p>a menu of three problems from which the student picks one.</p>\n\n<h2>Test mode</h2>\n\n<p>Built for 1.7 and <strong>inherited here with no new work</strong> — this is the second of the</p>\n<p>eight chapter tests to use it, which is the first evidence the mode generalises.</p>\n\n<p>**The flag lives in whichever config block the part's renderer reads, and there are</p>\n<p>four of them; the wrong one is silently inert.** All five parts must set theirs:</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Part</th><th>Lesson</th><th>Block</th><th>Renderer</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1 Concepts and Traces</td><td>2.7.1</td><td>`quiz.summative`</td><td>`components/QuizView.tsx`</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2 In Your Own Words</td><td>2.7.2</td><td>`aiGrader.summative`</td><td>`components/WrittenGrader.tsx`</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3 Find and Fix</td><td>2.7.3</td><td>`grading.summative`</td><td>`components/LessonWorkspace.tsx`</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4 Chart It</td><td>2.7.4</td><td>`diagram.summative`</td><td>`components/DiagramAssignmentView.tsx`</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5 Write It</td><td>2.7.5</td><td>`aiGrader.summative`</td><td>`components/WrittenGrader.tsx`</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p><code>node scripts/check-summative-parts.mjs</code> enforces this and should be run before the</p>\n<p>day. <strong>It exists because of this exact failure on 1.7</strong>: measured 2026-09-03, Parts</p>\n<p>1, 2 and 5 were flagged and Parts 3 and 4 were not, so a student who could not fix</p>\n<p>Part 3's syntax error could not reach Parts 4 and 5 at all and lost marks they had</p>\n<p>already earned. All five of 1.7's lessons now set it.</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th></th><th>A module quiz</th><th>A chapter test (`summative`)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Attempts</td><td>unlimited</td><td>one; the paper locks on submit</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Marking shown</td><td>per-question, right or wrong</td><td>none</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Explanations shown</td><td>yes, on every question</td><td>none</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Score shown</td><td>yes</td><td>no — the teacher hands it back</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Completion</td><td>on passing</td><td>**on submitting**</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>Completing on submission rather than on score remains the load-bearing one: the</p>\n<p>course is green-to-advance, and a score gate on Part A would lock a student out of</p>\n<p>Parts B through D of their own exam over an answer nobody has marked yet.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"Attempts: one\" describes the <code>quiz</code> and <code>aiGrader</code> halves only.</strong> What</p>\n<p><code>Grading.summative</code> and <code>DiagramConfig.summative</code> do is keep Submit available on an</p>\n<p>incomplete attempt and unlock the next part regardless — they do not cap attempts.</p>\n<p>Parts 3 and 4 therefore stay open, which is what the next section wants anyway.</p>\n\n<h2>`summative` hides the key on screen; two other pieces keep it off the wire</h2>\n\n<p>**This is the half of 1.7 a reader of its module doc would never learn, and it is the</p>\n<p>half that nearly lost the assessment.** <code>summative</code> governs what QuizView and</p>\n<p>WrittenGrader <em>draw</em>. It does not govern what the page <em>ships</em>, and the difference is</p>\n<p>not academic: <code>/lesson/[lessonId]/</code> is a statically exported page whose body is a</p>\n<p>client component, so the whole <code>Lesson</code> object crosses the boundary and lands in the</p>\n<p>RSC payload. Measured 2026-09-02 on <code>1-7-1</code>: View Source returned **all three forms,</p>\n<p>all 18 <code>answer</code> indices and every explanation**, before a question had been answered.</p>\n<p>Measured again 2026-09-03 on <code>1-7-2</code>: the <code>aiGrader.prompt</code> rubric — naming every</p>\n<p>accepted answer — was still in the built page after WrittenGrader had stopped</p>\n<p>rendering it.</p>\n\n<p>Three pieces close that, all of them inherited, none of them automatic:</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Piece</th><th>What it does</th><th>Where 2.7 depends on it</th></tr>\n<tr><td>`lib/quiz-redact.ts` → `redactLessonForClient()`</td><td>Strips `answer`, `explanation` and `source` from a summative quiz; strips `prompt` and `contextDocs` from a summative `aiGrader`</td><td>**Called in the SERVER page** (`app/lesson/[lessonId]/page.tsx:8`, `app/assignment/[id]/page.tsx:8`) — after the `'use client'` boundary it is already serialised and too late</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`scripts/score-quiz.mjs <lessonId>`</td><td>Turns picks into marks on the machine that still has the key</td><td>2.7.1 — with the key gone the browser **cannot** mark, so the submission records picks and a NULL score</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`fetchSubmissions` (server) in `WrittenGrader.tsx:144`</td><td>Decides \"already submitted\" from the server, not localStorage</td><td>2.7.2 and 2.7.5 — 30 of the 100 points</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h3>The same leak is open on Part C today, and no check can see it</h3>\n\n<p>**<code>redactLessonForClient()</code> covers <code>quiz</code> and <code>aiGrader</code>. It does not touch</p>\n<p><code>requirements</code>** (<code>lib/quiz-redact.ts:84-93</code> — two branches, and that is the whole</p>\n<p>function). <code>LessonWorkspace</code> is a client component that reads <code>lesson.requirements</code>,</p>\n<p>and <code>app/assignment/[id]/page.tsx:35-38</code> hands it the \"redacted\" lesson, so every</p>\n<p><code>Requirement</code> — <strong>including <code>pattern</code></strong> — crosses the boundary and is serialised.</p>\n<p>That is the identical mechanism <code>lib/quiz-redact.ts:4-18</code> documents for the quiz.</p>\n\n<p><strong>On a Find-and-Fix part the patterns are the answer key.</strong> 1.7.3 is</p>\n<p><code>type: \"assignment\"</code> with <code>grading.summative: true</code>, and its five regexes hand over:</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Req</th><th>What View Source gives away</th></tr>\n<tr><td>r1</td><td>the closed-string form the unterminated literal has to become</td></tr>\n<tr><td>r2</td><td>both `beforTax` (the typo) and `beforeTax` (the fix), plus the required `total = beforeTax + tax`</td></tr>\n<tr><td>r3</td><td>`let shipping = 5;` — the corrected **value**, where the bug is the string `\"5\"`</td></tr>\n<tr><td>r4</td><td>`count = 2` as the thing that must not survive, and the `wrapFee` shape that must replace it</td></tr>\n<tr><td>r5</td><td>the three words being marked: `syntax`, `runtime`, `logic`</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>The <code>title</code> and <code>hint</code> fields are the checklist the student is <em>meant</em> to see — the</p>\n<p>symptom-only wording the 2026-09-02 narrowing settled. The patterns are not, and they</p>\n<p>name the repair the narrowing deliberately stopped naming.</p>\n\n<p>**<code>scripts/check-quiz-key-leak.mjs:52-53</code> collects only <code>lesson.quiz?.summative</code> and</p>\n<p><code>lesson.aiGrader?.summative</code>.** A <code>grading.summative</code> lesson is never collected and</p>\n<p>the check has no notion of <code>requirements</code>, so the guard written for the first leak is</p>\n<p>structurally blind to this one. <code>check-summative-parts.mjs</code> only asserts the flag is</p>\n<p><em>set</em>.</p>\n\n<p><strong>This is live on 1.7.3 now; 2.7.3 inherits it by being authored the same way.</strong> The</p>\n<p>fix is the same shape as the quiz fix — strip <code>pattern</code> from a <code>grading.summative</code></p>\n<p>lesson's requirements before the client boundary, keep <code>title</code>/<code>description</code>/<code>hint</code>,</p>\n<p>and teach <code>check-quiz-key-leak.mjs</code> to collect <code>grading.summative</code> and scan the built</p>\n<p>page for each pattern. Part C's checker then cannot self-grade, which 2.7 already</p>\n<p>assumes: shCode records, the teacher marks.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Secondary, same class:</strong> <code>redactLessonForClient</code> also ignores <code>diagram.aiGrader</code>.</p>\n<p>1.7.4 has none, so nothing leaks today — but <code>2-2-12-a5-2-flowchart-decision</code> does</p>\n<p>carry one, and if 2.7.4 is given a rubric its <code>prompt</code> ships unredacted.</p>\n\n<p>Two consequences to plan for rather than discover:</p>\n\n<p>- <strong>Part A's marks do not exist until you run <code>score-quiz.mjs</code>.</strong> Budget that; it is a</p>\n<p>  step between the period ending and a number going in Aeries, and it is not optional.</p>\n<p>  <code>lib/quiz-answer-key.mjs</code> prints the key for the parts you mark by hand.</p>\n<p>- <strong><code>stripKey()</code> is a rebuild, not a delete.</strong> It names the fields it keeps — <code>id</code>,</p>\n<p>  <code>question</code>, <code>options</code>, <code>code</code>, <code>variant</code> — so **a question that grows a new</p>\n<p>  key-bearing field must be added there on purpose**. If 2.7.1's traces want a field</p>\n<p>  1.7's did not have, that is the file to check before the day, not after.</p>\n\n<p>The <code>summative</code>-drops-the-lock argument is also where the second lock came from:</p>\n<p><code>WrittenGrader</code> read localStorage alone, so clearing site data, switching browser or</p>\n<p>opening a second device handed back an unlocked Submit on an assessment that has no</p>\n<p>retake. 2.7 has no retake either.</p>\n\n<h2>What each part reveals while it is being sat</h2>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Part</th><th>Attempts</th><th>What the app tells the student</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1 Concepts and Traces</td><td>one</td><td>nothing — no marking, no explanations, no score</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2 In Your Own Words</td><td>one</td><td>nothing</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5 Write It</td><td>one</td><td>nothing</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4 Chart It</td><td>unlimited</td><td>whether it is a *legal* flowchart, never whether it answers the problem</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3 Find and Fix</td><td>unlimited</td><td>which symptom is still showing</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>Parts 3 and 4 stay open for the reason 1.7 sets out: repairing and redrawing ARE the</p>\n<p>skills, and a student who cannot check their work has been set a guessing game.</p>\n<p>Part 3's checklist names the <strong>symptom</strong> (\"the loop prints seven lines, not six\"),</p>\n<p>never the repair, and the hints say where to look rather than what to type — the</p>\n<p>narrowing decided on 2026-09-02 after the Ch 1 checklist walked a student who had</p>\n<p>found nothing to full marks.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Part 5 is one attempt and it now contains runnable code.</strong> That is new and it is</p>\n<p>worth being deliberate about: the student cannot run it before submitting, which is</p>\n<p>the point — Part C is where running is allowed, Part D is where you have to be right</p>\n<p>on paper. Say so in the lesson text, because a student who has spent seven weeks in a</p>\n<p>green-to-advance course will reasonably expect a Run button.</p>\n\n<h2>Grading and the gradebook</h2>\n\n<p>shCode records the work; it does not compute the 100. <code>points</code> is 0 on all five</p>\n<p>lessons and <code>passingScore</code> is 0, as everywhere else in the course. The teacher reads</p>\n<p>the submissions from the teacher view and enters one score per student in Aeries.</p>\n\n<h2>Grading and the gradebook</h2>\n\n<p>shCode records the work; it does not compute the 100. <code>points</code> is 0 on all five</p>\n<p>lessons and <code>passingScore</code> is 0, as everywhere else in the course. The teacher reads</p>\n<p>the submissions from the teacher view and enters one score per student in Aeries.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The syllabus's three-category scheme is the one students and parents see:</strong></p>\n<p>Daily Homework/Classwork 35%, Group Assessments 15%, Individual Assessments 50%.</p>\n<p>Chapter tests are entered under <strong>Individual Assessments (50%)</strong>. <code>curriculum-plan.md</code>'s</p>\n<p>GRADING STRUCTURE table has a second column (added 2026-08-14, explicitly marked</p>\n<p><strong>not adopted</strong>) that gives \"Individual Chapter Tests (8 total)\" 15% — **1.7 tells the</p>\n<p>teacher to enter the score \"against the 15% Individual Chapter Tests category\"**, which</p>\n<p>is the unadopted column, and 15% is also the syllabus's <em>Group</em> weight, making the</p>\n<p>confusion easy to miss. **Fix 1.7's line to match the syllabus; annotate the plan's</p>\n<p>unadopted column as \"not adopted — see syllabus.\"** One of the two documents should</p>\n<p>give, and 1.7's line needs the fix.</p>\n\n<p>The AI grader marks 2.7.2 and 2.7.5 against their rubrics and stores the result;</p>\n<p>under <code>summative</code> the student never sees it. Treat the model's marks as a first pass</p>\n<p>to review, not as the grade. **2.7.5 is the first AI-graded item on a chapter test</p>\n<p>that is code rather than prose** — read those by hand this time and check whether the</p>\n<p>rubric held before trusting it on Ch 3.</p>\n\n<h2>No retake</h2>\n\n<p>Inherited from the 2026-09-02 decision, unchanged: the score a student earns on the</p>\n<p>day is the score. No alternate paper, no second sitting.</p>\n\n<p>That puts the weight of fairness on the three same-room forms and on the open-date</p>\n<p>lock. Keep the module locked until the period starts. An absence remains a teacher</p>\n<p>judgement each time rather than a policy the app enforces.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Setting the locks</strong> — <code>node scripts/set-ch2-open-dates.mjs</code> writes module-scoped</p>\n<p><code>class_open_dates</code> rows: 2.6 opens Mon Sep 28 2026 00:00, 2.7 opens Wed Sep 30 2026</p>\n<p>00:00 (America/Los_Angeles). It reads the class list from D1 and upserts both rows</p>\n<p>per class; <code>--local</code> targets dev. Requires an authed wrangler (<code>CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN</code>)</p>\n<p>— run it where the teacher's Cloudflare session lives, then verify with the SELECT in</p>\n<p>its output.</p>\n\n<h2>Teacher notes</h2>\n\n<p>- <strong>Hand the PA rubric back as the study guide on Sep 28.</strong> Every item type here has</p>\n<p>  an analogue in the program the pair built two days earlier. Same policy as Ch 1,</p>\n<p>  and it is not a secret.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The graded PA comes back on Fri Oct 2.</strong> The Sep 28 PA is the study guide; the</p>\n<p>graded artifact is returned at the next class meeting (Fri Oct 2), two days after the</p>\n<p>individual test. The syllabus schedules §3.1 on Fri Oct 2, so the PA is handed back</p>\n<p>at the start of that lesson. This is the same policy as Ch 1 (PA on Sep 1, graded</p>\n<p>PA returned Sep 3 before the test — 1.7 says \"Sep 1\" but the calendar has the test</p>\n<p>on Sep 3, so the PA was returned at the start of that lesson).</p>\n<p>- <strong>Proctor Part B harder than you did on Ch 1.</strong> In-app means a console is a tab</p>\n<p>  away, and this is the change: Chapter 1's traces were four-line straight sequences</p>\n<p>  that a student had to retype. Chapter 2's are loops — <em>paste, run, read the output</em></p>\n<p>  is faster than tracing, and it produces a perfect answer. Shuffling and three forms</p>\n<p>  defeat the over-the-shoulder glance; they do not defeat a second tab, and the</p>\n<p>  incentive to open one just went up sharply.</p>\n<p>- <strong>The open-date lock is client-side only</strong> (<code>components/LessonAccessGate.tsx</code>). It</p>\n<p>  stops the lesson rendering early; it does not stop a determined student reaching</p>\n<p>  the lesson's data through the API.</p>\n<p>- <strong>Read D1 and D2 together.</strong> A correct chart with botched code is a different</p>\n<p>  failure from no chart at all, and the 10/20 split is what makes that visible. D2 is</p>\n<p>  graded against the problem, not against D1 — a student who charts one approach and</p>\n<p>  codes a better one loses nothing. Only a blank D1 costs the 10.</p>\n<p>- <strong>An off-by-one in D2 is not the same failure as a missing loop.</strong> Say what partial</p>\n<p>  credit looks like in the rubric before the day, or it will be decided per-paper at</p>\n<p>  10pm.</p>\n\n<h2>Fixed by the student-tester loop (2026-09-18)</h2>\n\n<p>Two answer keys in 2.7.1 were wrong and are corrected: <code>a-t3-fallthrough</code> keyed one</p>\n<p>line where the snippet prints two, and <code>b-t1-equality</code> keyed \"has crates\" where the</p>\n<p>code prints \"empty\". Both were caught by executing every snippet against its key —</p>\n<p><code>scripts/score-quiz.mjs</code> would have marked correct students wrong. 2.7.5's three</p>\n<p>point splits (rubric 8/9/3, prompt 8/12, answer key 8/8/4) are unified at 8/9/3, and</p>\n<p>the reference solution no longer uses <code>switch (true)</code>. 2.7.3's starter ships on disk</p>\n<p>(<code>lib/lessons.ts</code> reads files from disk, never from <code>lesson.json</code>), its Bug-3/4</p>\n<p>requirement patterns now fail on the buggy starter and pass on the fix, and its</p>\n<p>content.md describes the actual file. 2.7.4's text now describes the in-app checker</p>\n<p>that actually runs (11 checks), not the paper workflow the plan imagined.</p>\n\n<h2>Still open</h2>\n\n<p>- **The plan's common format says closed-laptop and hand-drawn D1; both tests are sat</p>\n<p>  in-app.** Appendix-adjacent §\"Common format\" specifies \"Individual, closed book,</p>\n<p>  closed laptop\" with \"D1 is hand-drawn on paper… the in-app checker isn't there.\"</p>\n<p>  1.7 shipped as five in-app lessons with a live diagram checker on D1, and 2.7</p>\n<p>  follows it. The in-app version is better — it is what the proctoring note is about</p>\n<p>  — but the plan still describes the paper one, so a reader of the plan and a reader</p>\n<p>  of these modules are looking at two different assessments. Reconcile before Ch 3.</p>\n<p>- **The per-module points table reconciles, but only under a split the doc never</p>\n<p>  states.** With the eight 2.7.1 questions at 5 each, the four bugs at 5, D1 at 10 and</p>\n<p>  D2 at 20, the rows 21/24/15/22/18 come out exactly if — and only if — the ten</p>\n<p>  short-answer points go <strong>3 / 4 / 3</strong> across §2.1 / §2.2 / §2.5 and D2's twenty split</p>\n<p>  <strong>8 to §2.1</strong> (the branch) and <strong>12 to §2.4</strong> (the loop and the nesting). Both are</p>\n<p>  forced by the arithmetic and neither is written down, so a reader cannot check the</p>\n<p>  table and an author cannot reproduce it. State them or drop the points column.</p>\n<p>- <strong>1.7's item count is off by one and this module does not copy it.</strong> 1.7's parts</p>\n<p>  table lists 3 + 5 + 3 + 4 + 1 + 1 and totals it to <strong>16</strong>; its own per-module</p>\n<p>  coverage row enumerates the same 17 (2 + 6 + 2 + 2 + 5). The points are right —</p>\n<p>  9 + 30 + 9 + 8 + 44 = 100 — so nothing about that test is mis-weighted, but the</p>\n<p>  headline \"16 items\" in its opening paragraph is wrong in both places it appears.</p>\n<p>  2.7 says 17. Fix 1.7 rather than letting the two disagree.</p>\n<p>- <strong>1.7's \"Part C cannot be locked\" note is now stale.</strong> It said console lessons had</p>\n<p>  no <code>summative</code> concept and that narrowing the checklist was chosen over building</p>\n<p>  one \"on the eve of the assessment\". The console/requirements-graded</p>\n<p>  <code>Grading.summative</code> exists now, as does <code>DiagramConfig.summative</code>, so 2.7.3 and</p>\n<p>  2.7.4 keep unlimited attempts **as a pedagogical choice rather than a platform</p>\n<p>  limitation**. Worth correcting in 1.7 so the next author does not re-derive a</p>\n<p>  constraint that is gone.</p>\n<p>- <strong><code>AiGraderConfig</code> is still declared twice</strong> — <code>lib/types.ts</code> and</p>\n<p>  <code>components/WrittenGrader.tsx</code>. Flagged in 1.7, still true, and 2.7.5 is the first</p>\n<p>  place a <em>code</em> rubric goes through it.</p>\n<p>- <strong>The hexagon cannot be auto-required on D1</strong>, for the same reason 2.6 flags: the</p>\n<p>  checker's addable floors are <code>min-decisions</code>, <code>min-process</code>, <code>min-nodes</code> and</p>\n<p>  <code>no-self-loop</code> only (Appendix D §D.3). D1 <em>should</em> require a loop and no rule can</p>\n<p>  enforce it. Grade by eye, or add <code>min-preparation</code>.</p>\n"},"3.10":{"title":"Chapter 3 Individual Performance Assessment","html":"\n<h1>Module 3.10: Chapter 3 Individual Performance Assessment</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 1 · Week 13 · Wed Nov 04, 2026 · One meeting · Individual · ~53 minutes</strong></p>\n\n<p>The individual half of the chapter's assessment, and the sibling of the Group PA two</p>\n<p>days before it: same chapter, same week, same in-app shape, sat alone. Five lessons,</p>\n<p>17 items, 100 points — the identical skeleton to 1.7 and 2.7.</p>\n\n<p><code>curriculum-plan.md</code> calls this the heaviest test of Q1 and the last gate before the</p>\n<p>Print Shop (§4.1, Fri Nov 6). Both are true, and they are the same fact: every line of</p>\n<p>the Print Shop is functions over an array of objects.</p>\n\n<h2>Why this module is numbered 3.10, and the check that did not know</h2>\n\n<p>Chapter 3 has eight book sections rather than five, so its Group PA is 3.9 and its test</p>\n<p>is 3.10. That is the only structural difference from 1.7 and 2.7, and it broke one gate</p>\n<p>silently:</p>\n\n<p>**<code>scripts/check-summative-parts.mjs</code> keyed test units on `/^(\\d+)\\.7 Chapter \\1</p>\n<p>Individual Performance Assessment$/<code>.** </code>3.10 Chapter 3 Individual Performance</p>\n<p>Assessment` does not match, so the check would have counted zero test units here,</p>\n<p>printed <code>ok</code>, and examined none of these five parts. That is precisely the failure the</p>\n<p>file exists to prevent — measured on 1.7 on 2026-09-03, where Parts 3 and 4 were</p>\n<p>unflagged and a student stuck on Part 3's syntax error could not reach Parts 4 and 5 at</p>\n<p>all and lost marks they had already earned.</p>\n\n<p>Fixed: the section number is now <code>\\d+</code>. The chapter backreference and the two literal</p>\n<p>words still exclude teaching modules whose number ends in <code>.7</code> (3.7 Array Methods, 6.7</p>\n<p>Advanced Input) and still exclude the Group PA units, which are deliberately not test</p>\n<p>units. <strong>Run <code>node scripts/check-summative-parts.mjs</code> before the day</strong> and confirm it</p>\n<p>reports three test units rather than two.</p>\n\n<h2>What 2.7 established and this one changes</h2>\n\n<p>2.7's one genuinely new thing was that Part D2 became real JavaScript. That holds. What</p>\n<p>changes here is what the paper can <em>ask about</em>, and it is a larger jump than 2.7's:</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th></th><th>2.7 (Sep 30)</th><th>3.10 (Nov 4)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Part B traces</td><td>loops, `switch`, `try`/`catch` — all top-level</td><td>**functions**: what a call evaluates to, what a callee can change</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The data</td><td>counters and `word[i]`</td><td>an **array of objects**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D1</td><td>chart, hexagon legal</td><td>chart, **double-rail legal** (§3.1)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D2</td><td>one script with a nested loop</td><td>**named functions** with parameters and returns</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ceiling</td><td>no functions, arrays or objects</td><td>no classes, no `this`, no moSHion</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p><strong>Tracing is worth more here than it has been</strong>, for a reason specific to this chapter.</p>\n<p>A student who cannot say what <code>total(cart)</code> evaluates to cannot debug anything built out</p>\n<p>of functions, and from §4.1 onward everything is. Part B is where that shows.</p>\n\n<h2>Why this is not the Group PA again, alone</h2>\n\n<p>Unchanged from 1.7 and 2.7: 3.9 already proves a pair can produce; what a paired build</p>\n<p>cannot tell you is which of the two produced it.</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th></th><th>3.9 Group PA (Nov 2)</th><th>3.10 Test (Nov 4)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Who</td><td>pairs</td><td>individual</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Length</td><td>~87 min over three lessons</td><td>~53 min over five</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The verb</td><td>**build** one thing end to end</td><td>**read · diagnose · design · write**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Code written</td><td>five functions and a main section</td><td>two or three functions, in Part D2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Flowchart</td><td>25 min, drawn together, gates the build</td><td>7 min, drawn alone</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Coverage</td><td>all eight sections through one artifact</td><td>all eight as independent items</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The failure it catches</td><td>cannot decompose a problem into functions</td><td>cannot trace a call, cannot name a bug, **was carried**</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Coverage and weights</h2>\n\n<p>17 items. Every section of the chapter appears in at least two places.</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Part</th><th>Lesson</th><th>Items</th><th>Points</th><th>Minutes</th></tr>\n<tr><td>**A** Concepts (multiple choice)</td><td>3.10.1</td><td>3</td><td>15</td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**B** Trace the code</td><td>3.10.1</td><td>5</td><td>25</td><td>16</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**A** Concepts (short answer)</td><td>3.10.2</td><td>3</td><td>10</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**C** Find and fix, name the type</td><td>3.10.3</td><td>4</td><td>20</td><td>11</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**D1** Chart it</td><td>3.10.4</td><td>1</td><td>10</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**D2** Write it</td><td>3.10.5</td><td>1</td><td>20</td><td>12</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td></td><td>**17**</td><td>**100**</td><td>**53**</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>The plan's fixed A/B/C/D weighting — 25 / 25 / 20 / 30 — lands exactly: Part A is the</p>\n<p>three MCQs (15) plus the three short answers (10), Part B the five traces, Part D the</p>\n<p>chart (10) plus the write (20).</p>\n\n<p>Minutes are carried unchanged from 2.7. 53 is still the plan's \"~50 minutes\", and the</p>\n<p>105-minute block absorbs it.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The ~52 minutes after the test are not idle</strong>, and the same arrangement 2.7 used</p>\n<p>applies: §4.1 Print Shop is Fri Nov 6, two days later, so Nov 4 has unscheduled time.</p>\n<p>Use it for an exam wrapper (5–10 min), errata review (10–15 min), and a §4.1 preview</p>\n<p>(10–15 min) — the Print Shop brief read aloud, no code. The calendar still places §4.1</p>\n<p>on Nov 6; this is a bridge, not an early start.</p>\n\n<h3>Where each section is assessed</h3>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Section</th><th>Points</th><th>Where</th></tr>\n<tr><td>3.1 Functions: Definition and Calls</td><td>14</td><td>one concept MCQ, half of D1 (the double-rail), part of D2's function criterion</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.2 Parameters and Return Values</td><td>13</td><td>one trace (a function with no `return`), one short answer, part of D2's function criterion</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.3 Arrays</td><td>18</td><td>one trace (index and `length`), one bug (index past the end), half of D1 (the loop), part of D2's list criterion</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.4 Function Expressions and Arrow Functions</td><td>10</td><td>one trace (block body vs implicit return), one bug (the same, as a defect)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5 Objects and Properties</td><td>12</td><td>one concept MCQ (dot vs bracket), one bug (malformed object literal), part of D2's list criterion</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.6 Functions: Pass by Value/Reference</td><td>13</td><td>one trace (a callee mutates a passed object), one short answer, one bug (mutates where it should copy)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7 Array Methods</td><td>11</td><td>one concept MCQ (mutating vs non-mutating), one short answer, part of D2's list criterion</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8 Saving and Loading Data</td><td>9</td><td>one trace (what does not survive a round trip), D2's save/load criterion</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p><strong>The split behind that table, stated.</strong> 2.7's own \"Still open\" complains that its</p>\n<p>equivalent reconciles only under a split nobody wrote down. Here it is: the eight</p>\n<p>3.10.1 questions are 5 each; the four bugs are 5 each; the ten short-answer points go</p>\n<p><strong>4 / 3 / 3</strong> across §3.2 / §3.6 / §3.7; <strong>D1's 10 splits 5 to §3.1</strong> (the double-rail)</p>\n<p>and <strong>5 to §3.3</strong> (the loop); <strong>D2's 20 splits 8 to the function criterion</strong> (4 to §3.1,</p>\n<p>4 to §3.2), <strong>8 to the list criterion</strong> (3 to §3.3, 2 to §3.5, 3 to §3.7), and **4 to</p>\n<p>the save/load criterion** (§3.8). The rows come out 14/13/18/10/12/13/11/9 = 100.</p>\n\n<p>The spread is 9 to 18. §3.3 is the heaviest because arrays are what the other sections</p>\n<p>operate on; §3.8 is the lightest because a round trip is one idea, well covered by one</p>\n<p>trace and one criterion.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The blueprint's named requirements all land</strong>, with one exception recorded below:</p>\n<p>function definition vs call (MCQ, D1); parameters vs arguments vs return value and what</p>\n<p>a function with no <code>return</code> evaluates to (trace, short answer); array indexing,</p>\n<p><code>.length</code>, iteration (trace, bug, D1, D2); arrow syntax against a <code>function</code> declaration</p>\n<p>(trace, bug); object literal access with both dot and bracket notation (MCQ);</p>\n<p>**pass-by-value vs pass-by-reference with at least one Part B trace where a function</p>\n<p>mutates a passed object** (t2 — the blueprint asks for this by name and it is authored</p>\n<p>as a trace); <code>.map</code>/<code>.slice</code> on a supplied array (MCQ, D2); <code>JSON.stringify</code>/<code>parse</code></p>\n<p>round trip (trace, D2).</p>\n\n<p><strong>The exception is <code>.filter</code>.</strong> The blueprint says \"<code>.map</code>/<code>.filter</code>/<code>.slice</code> on a</p>\n<p>supplied array\". §3.7 as shipped teaches <code>.map()</code>, <code>.slice()</code>, <code>.concat()</code> and spread;</p>\n<p><code>.filter</code> is named-not-taught in <code>3-7-19</code> and appears nowhere else in the chapter, and</p>\n<p><code>curriculum/modules/3.7_array-methods.md</code> says so outright. **A <code>.filter</code> item on this</p>\n<p>paper would assess something the chapter did not teach.** Selecting a subset is assessed</p>\n<p>as the chapter teaches it — a loop and <code>push</code> (3.3.13, 3.3.14). Same class of</p>\n<p>plan-versus-shipped divergence 2.6 records for A2.2.0; see <strong>Still open</strong>.</p>\n\n<h2>The ceiling this test may not exceed</h2>\n\n<p><strong>No classes, no <code>this</code>, no constructors.</strong> Those are §5.3 and §5.4. Every snippet is</p>\n<p>plain functions over plain objects and arrays.</p>\n\n<p><strong>No array method outside <code>.map()</code>, <code>.slice()</code>, <code>.concat()</code> and spread.</strong> A Part B trace</p>\n<p>that hands the student a <code>.reduce()</code> is asking them to read something 3.7.19 only named.</p>\n<p>This cuts both ways and the generous direction matters: a student who <em>writes</em></p>\n<p><code>.filter()</code> in D2 has done something 3.7.19 told them exists. Credit the behaviour, not</p>\n<p>the technique — the rubric says how.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Six shapes on D1, and the sixth is new.</strong> Oval, rectangle, diamond, parallelogram, the</p>\n<p>loop-setup hexagon (§2.2), and the <strong>double-rail subroutine</strong>, released at §3.1</p>\n<p>(Appendix D §D.2). <strong>Not released, must not appear:</strong> the connector circle and the</p>\n<p>comment bracket (§4.1).</p>\n\n<p><strong>3.10.4 takes 3.9.1's rule block</strong>, unchanged: <code>min-nodes: 10</code>, <code>min-process: 2</code>,</p>\n<p><code>min-decisions: 1</code>, and the other eight. 3.9's module doc sets out the survey and the</p>\n<p>reasoning — the short version is that <code>min-process</code> counts rectangles only, and a</p>\n<p>double-rail is no more a rectangle than a hexagon is, so the floor stays at 2 while</p>\n<p><code>min-nodes</code> carries the weight. Identical rules on both charts is also the fairer</p>\n<p>arrangement when the PA is two days earlier and is handed back as the study guide.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The printed syntax reference needs rebuilding for this chapter.</strong> Ch 1's sheet was</p>\n<p>declarations and the primitive types; Ch 2's was the loop forms, <code>switch</code> and</p>\n<p><code>try</code>/<code>catch</code>. Ch 3's is: the three function forms (declaration, expression, arrow) with</p>\n<p>the shorthand rules, <code>return</code>, array literals with index and <code>.length</code>, <code>push</code>, object</p>\n<p>literals with dot and bracket access, the four taught array methods, spread in an array</p>\n<p>and in an object literal, <code>JSON.stringify</code>/<code>JSON.parse</code>, <code>localStorage.setItem</code>/</p>\n<p><code>getItem</code>, and the Appendix D shape table <strong>including the double-rail</strong>. Beginners are</p>\n<p>assessed on whether they can reason, not on whether they memorised where the arrow's</p>\n<p>braces change the meaning.</p>\n\n<h2>Three forms, assigned automatically</h2>\n\n<p>Unchanged mechanism, restated so the module stands alone.</p>\n\n<pre><code>\n3.10.1 quiz ── variants: [\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"] ── hash(lessonId + student email)\n                                              │\n        ┌─────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────┐\n        ▼                                     ▼                     ▼\n   3 common concepts               3 common concepts       3 common concepts\n   + 5 traces (form a)             + 5 traces (form b)     + 5 traces (form c)\n        └──────── questions and options shuffled per student ───────┘\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Eighteen questions authored; each student is asked eight. The five traces are the same</p>\n<p>five <em>shapes</em> on every form — a function with no <code>return</code>, a callee mutating a passed</p>\n<p>object, an index read against <code>.length</code>, an arrow's block body against its implicit</p>\n<p>return, and a JSON round trip that drops something — with different names and values, so</p>\n<p>the forms are genuinely equivalent. <code>lib/quiz-variant.ts</code> does the assignment;</p>\n<p><code>scripts/test-quiz.mjs</code> refuses to build a quiz whose forms come out different lengths.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Vary the names and the values, never the trip.</strong> 2.7 found the limit of that rule on</p>\n<p>its nested-loop trace, where the bounds <em>are</em> the workload. Chapter 3 has a different</p>\n<p>version of the same hazard: <strong>the array length is the workload on t3 and t5.</strong> Three</p>\n<p>records to read is not four. So hold the record count constant across forms and vary the</p>\n<p>field names and the numbers instead.</p>\n\n<p><code>scripts/test-quiz.mjs</code> refuses forms of different <strong>length</strong>. It cannot see any of</p>\n<p>this; it is an authoring discipline and nothing more.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The other four lessons are single-form, deliberately</strong>, for the reason 1.7 gives:</p>\n<p>Part C's answer is typed into a file rather than picked from a list, and D1/D2 offer a</p>\n<p>menu of three problems from which the student picks one.</p>\n\n<h2>Test mode</h2>\n\n<p>Built for 1.7, inherited unchanged. **The flag lives in whichever config block the</p>\n<p>part's renderer reads, and the wrong one is silently inert.** All five parts set theirs:</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Part</th><th>Lesson</th><th>Block</th><th>Renderer</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1 Concepts and Traces</td><td>3.10.1</td><td>`quiz.summative`</td><td>`components/QuizView.tsx`</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2 In Your Own Words</td><td>3.10.2</td><td>`aiGrader.summative`</td><td>`components/WrittenGrader.tsx`</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3 Find and Fix</td><td>3.10.3</td><td>`grading.summative`</td><td>`components/LessonWorkspace.tsx`</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4 Chart It</td><td>3.10.4</td><td>`diagram.summative`</td><td>`components/DiagramAssignmentView.tsx`</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5 Write It</td><td>3.10.5</td><td>`aiGrader.summative`</td><td>`components/WrittenGrader.tsx`</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th></th><th>A module quiz</th><th>A chapter test (`summative`)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Attempts</td><td>unlimited</td><td>one; the paper locks on submit</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Marking shown</td><td>per-question, right or wrong</td><td>none</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Explanations shown</td><td>yes, on every question</td><td>none</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Score shown</td><td>yes</td><td>no — the teacher hands it back</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Completion</td><td>on passing</td><td>**on submitting**</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>Completing on submission rather than on score remains load-bearing: the course is</p>\n<p>green-to-advance, and a score gate on Part A would lock a student out of Parts B through</p>\n<p>D of their own exam over an answer nobody has marked yet.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"Attempts: one\" describes the <code>quiz</code> and <code>aiGrader</code> halves only.</strong></p>\n<p><code>Grading.summative</code> and <code>DiagramConfig.summative</code> keep Submit available on an incomplete</p>\n<p>attempt and unlock the next part regardless; they do not cap attempts. Parts 3 and 4</p>\n<p>therefore stay open, which is what the next section wants anyway.</p>\n\n<h2>`summative` hides the key on screen; two other pieces keep it off the wire</h2>\n\n<p>Inherited from 1.7 and 2.7 and still not automatic. <code>summative</code> governs what QuizView</p>\n<p>and WrittenGrader <em>draw</em>; it does not govern what the page <em>ships</em>. <code>/lesson/[lessonId]/</code></p>\n<p>is statically exported with a client component body, so the whole <code>Lesson</code> object crosses</p>\n<p>the boundary into the RSC payload.</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Piece</th><th>What it does</th><th>Where 3.10 depends on it</th></tr>\n<tr><td>`lib/quiz-redact.ts` → `redactLessonForClient()`</td><td>Strips `answer`, `explanation` and `source` from a summative quiz; strips `prompt` and `contextDocs` from a summative `aiGrader`</td><td>**Called in the SERVER page** (`app/lesson/[lessonId]/page.tsx`, `app/assignment/[id]/page.tsx`) — after the `'use client'` boundary it is already serialised and too late</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`scripts/score-quiz.mjs <lessonId>`</td><td>Turns picks into marks on the machine that still has the key</td><td>3.10.1 — with the key gone the browser **cannot** mark, so the submission records picks and a NULL score</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`fetchSubmissions` (server) in `WrittenGrader.tsx`</td><td>Decides \"already submitted\" from the server, not localStorage</td><td>3.10.2 and 3.10.5 — 30 of the 100 points</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h3>The Part C leak is still open, and 3.10.3 inherits it</h3>\n\n<p>**<code>redactLessonForClient()</code> covers <code>quiz</code> and <code>aiGrader</code>. It does not touch</p>\n<p><code>requirements</code>.** <code>LessonWorkspace</code> is a client component that reads</p>\n<p><code>lesson.requirements</code>, so every <code>Requirement</code> — <strong>including <code>pattern</code></strong> — is serialised</p>\n<p>into the page. On a Find-and-Fix part the patterns are the answer key.</p>\n\n<p>This is live on 1.7.3 and 2.7.3 today and 3.10.3 is authored the same way, so it is not</p>\n<p>a new defect — but it is worth knowing exactly what leaks here, because Chapter 3's</p>\n<p>patterns are more explicit than Chapter 2's:</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Req</th><th>What View Source gives away</th></tr>\n<tr><td>r1</td><td>that the object literal needs a comma, and between which two properties</td></tr>\n<tr><td>r2</td><td>that the index has to come down by one, and the `length` form that does it</td></tr>\n<tr><td>r3</td><td>that the arrow's block body needs a `return`</td></tr>\n<tr><td>r4</td><td>that the copy has to be made with spread before the change</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p><code>scripts/check-quiz-key-leak.mjs</code> collects only <code>quiz.summative</code> and</p>\n<p><code>aiGrader.summative</code>, so it is structurally blind to this;</p>\n<p><code>check-summative-parts.mjs</code> only asserts the flag is set. The fix is the same shape as</p>\n<p>the quiz fix — strip <code>pattern</code> from a <code>grading.summative</code> lesson's requirements before</p>\n<p>the client boundary, keep <code>title</code>/<code>description</code>/<code>hint</code>, and teach</p>\n<p><code>check-quiz-key-leak.mjs</code> to scan the built page for each pattern. Until then, the</p>\n<p>practical mitigation is the one Part C already relies on: **the <code>hint</code> names the symptom,</p>\n<p>never the repair**, and the teacher marks from the submission rather than the checklist.</p>\n\n<p>Two consequences to plan for rather than discover:</p>\n\n<p>- <strong>Part A's marks do not exist until you run <code>score-quiz.mjs</code>.</strong> Budget that; it sits</p>\n<p>  between the period ending and a number going into Aeries.</p>\n<p>- <strong><code>stripKey()</code> is a rebuild, not a delete.</strong> It names the fields it keeps — <code>id</code>,</p>\n<p>  <code>question</code>, <code>options</code>, <code>code</code>, <code>variant</code> — so a question that grows a new key-bearing</p>\n<p>  field must be added there on purpose. 3.10.1's traces use <code>code</code>, which is kept.</p>\n\n<h2>What each part reveals while it is being sat</h2>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Part</th><th>Attempts</th><th>What the app tells the student</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1 Concepts and Traces</td><td>one</td><td>nothing — no marking, no explanations, no score</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2 In Your Own Words</td><td>one</td><td>nothing</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5 Write It</td><td>one</td><td>nothing</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4 Chart It</td><td>unlimited</td><td>whether it is a *legal* flowchart, never whether it answers the problem</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3 Find and Fix</td><td>unlimited</td><td>which symptom is still showing</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>Parts 3 and 4 stay open because repairing and redrawing ARE the skills. Part 3's</p>\n<p>checklist names the <strong>symptom</strong>, never the repair, and the hints say where to look</p>\n<p>rather than what to type — the narrowing decided on 2026-09-02.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Part 5 is one attempt with no Run button</strong>, as on 2.7. Say so in the lesson text: a</p>\n<p>student eleven weeks into a green-to-advance course will reasonably expect one.</p>\n\n<h2>Grading and the gradebook</h2>\n\n<p>shCode records the work; it does not compute the 100. <code>points</code> is 0 on all five lessons</p>\n<p>and <code>passingScore</code> is 0, as everywhere else in the course. The teacher reads the</p>\n<p>submissions from the teacher view and enters one score per student in Aeries.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The syllabus's three-category scheme is the one students and parents see:</strong> Daily</p>\n<p>Homework/Classwork 35%, Group Assessments 15%, Individual Assessments 50%. Chapter tests</p>\n<p>go under <strong>Individual Assessments (50%)</strong>. (<code>curriculum-plan.md</code>'s GRADING STRUCTURE</p>\n<p>table has a second column marked <em>not adopted</em> that gives chapter tests 15%; 1.7's text</p>\n<p>still points at it. 2.7 flagged that for correction and it is still outstanding — do not</p>\n<p>copy 1.7's line.)</p>\n\n<p>The AI grader marks 3.10.2 and 3.10.5 against their rubrics and stores the result; under</p>\n<p><code>summative</code> the student never sees it. Treat the model's marks as a first pass to review.</p>\n<p><strong>2.7 asked for 2.7.5's code rubric to be read by hand before trusting it on Ch 3</strong> —</p>\n<p>that review is due now, and 3.10.5's rubric is written on the assumption it has not</p>\n<p>happened yet, so read these by hand too.</p>\n\n<h2>No retake</h2>\n\n<p>Inherited from the 2026-09-02 decision, unchanged. The score a student earns on the day</p>\n<p>is the score. No alternate paper, no second sitting.</p>\n\n<p>That puts the weight of fairness on the three same-room forms and on the open-date lock.</p>\n<p><strong>Keep the module locked until the period starts.</strong> <code>scripts/set-ch2-open-dates.mjs</code> is</p>\n<p>the model for it; the Chapter 3 equivalent writes module-scoped <code>class_open_dates</code> rows</p>\n<p>for 3.9 (Mon Nov 2 2026 00:00) and 3.10 (Wed Nov 4 2026 00:00, America/Los_Angeles) and</p>\n<p>needs an authed wrangler session. **Note that the open-date lock is enforced</p>\n<p>client-side only** (<code>components/LessonAccessGate.tsx</code>); it stops the lesson rendering</p>\n<p>early, not a determined student reaching the data through the API.</p>\n\n<h2>Teacher notes</h2>\n\n<p>- <strong>Hand the PA rubric back as the study guide on Nov 2</strong>, and the graded PA at the</p>\n<p>  start of §4.1 on Fri Nov 6. Same policy as Ch 1 and Ch 2. The test's D1/D2 menus use</p>\n<p>  different problems from 3.9's, so the study guide is safe.</p>\n<p>- <strong>Proctor Part B at least as hard as Ch 2.</strong> The incentive is unchanged — paste, run,</p>\n<p>  read — and one thing makes it sharper here: t2 and t5 are <em>about</em> what the console</p>\n<p>  prints, so a second tab answers them outright. Shuffling and three forms defeat the</p>\n<p>  over-the-shoulder glance, not a second tab.</p>\n<p>- <strong>Read D1 and D2 together.</strong> A correct chart with botched code is a different failure</p>\n<p>  from no chart at all, and the 10/20 split makes that visible. D2 is graded against the</p>\n<p>  problem, not against D1. Only a blank D1 costs the 10.</p>\n<p>- <strong>The one discriminator to protect if the paper runs long is §3.6.</strong> t2, Q2 and bug 4</p>\n<p>  are the three items that separate a student who can <em>use</em> functions from one who</p>\n<p>  understands what a call does to the caller's data. Everything in Chapter 4 through</p>\n<p>  Chapter 7 stands on it.</p>\n<p>- <strong>Decide partial credit on D2 before the day</strong>, or it gets decided per-paper at 10pm.</p>\n<p>  The specific case to settle: a student who writes one correct function and calls it</p>\n<p>  twice instead of writing two. That is not the same failure as writing none.</p>\n\n<h2>Still open</h2>\n\n<p>- <strong><code>check-quiz-key-leak.mjs</code> cannot see Part C's patterns.</strong> Detailed above. Live on</p>\n<p>  1.7.3 and 2.7.3, inherited here. The fix is scoped and small and nobody has done it.</p>\n<p>- <strong>The double-rail cannot be auto-required on D1</strong>, for the same reason 2.6 flagged</p>\n<p>  about the hexagon: the checker's addable floors are <code>min-decisions</code>, <code>min-process</code>,</p>\n<p>  <code>min-nodes</code> and <code>no-self-loop</code> only (Appendix D §D.3). D1 <em>should</em> require a function</p>\n<p>  call and no rule can enforce it. Grade by eye, or add <code>min-subroutine</code>. §4.1 releases</p>\n<p>  two more shapes on top of this.</p>\n<p>- <strong>The plan's blueprint names <code>.filter</code>.</strong> Detailed above under coverage. Either the</p>\n<p>  plan is corrected or §3.7 grows a <code>.filter</code> lesson.</p>\n<p>- <strong>The plan's common format still says closed-laptop with a hand-drawn D1</strong>, while</p>\n<p>  1.7, 2.7 and now 3.10 are all sat in-app with a live checker on D1. Three tests in, the</p>\n<p>  in-app version is plainly the one being built. 2.7 asked for this to be reconciled</p>\n<p>  before Ch 3; it was not. Reconcile before Ch 5.</p>\n<p>- <strong><code>AiGraderConfig</code> is still declared twice</strong> — <code>lib/types.ts</code> and</p>\n<p>  <code>components/WrittenGrader.tsx</code>. Flagged in 1.7 and 2.7, still true.</p>\n<p>- <strong>1.7's item count is off by one and its Aeries category line is wrong.</strong> Both flagged</p>\n<p>  in 2.7, both still outstanding, and this module does not copy either.</p>\n"},"3.1":{"title":"Functions: Definition and Calls","html":"\n<h1>Module 3.1: Functions: Definition and Calls</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 1 · Week 7 · Environment: browser DevTools console</strong></p>\n\n<h2>Module Goal</h2>\n<p>Students identify function calls in a program, define a <strong>parameterless</strong> function</p>\n<p>that outputs strings, and describe the benefits of using functions (modularity,</p>\n<p>reusability, maintainability). Parameters, return values, scope, and pass by</p>\n<p>value/reference are explicitly <strong>not</strong> this module's job — see the Note below.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Unit 2 (Control Flow) complete.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Students can read a program and point to every function call in it, write and</p>\n<p>call a function of their own that takes no input and returns nothing, and</p>\n<p>explain in their own words why breaking code into named functions is worth</p>\n<p>doing. Module 3.2 (Parameters and Return Values) picks up from here.</p>\n\n<h2>Numbered Lesson List</h2>\n\n<p>Re-audited 2026-09-15. §3.1 is the chapter's narrowest book section (3 subsections,</p>\n<p>no <code>&lt;h3&gt;</code> sub-concepts, and — unlike every other §3.x — its own learning objectives</p>\n<p>explicitly exclude parameters and return values: \"define a <strong>parameterless</strong> function</p>\n<p>that outputs strings\"), so this list targets 18: near 3.6's low end (the chapter's other</p>\n<p>3-subsection module) rather than 3.2/3.5's 22–26, because 3.1 does not carry the extra</p>\n<p>\"real distinctions\" (array vs. object vs. nested mutation) that pushed 3.6 above a flat</p>\n<p>per-subsection count. The 2026-09-13 chapter-wide density re-audit already reviewed §3.1</p>\n<p>and found no restructuring needed (\"the sections already line up with downstream use\");</p>\n<p>what never happened afterward, unlike 3.2–3.8, was the lesson-building pass itself.</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Slot</th><th>Type</th><th>Title</th><th>Slug</th><th>Notes</th></tr>\n<tr><td>3.1.1</td><td>slides</td><td>3.1.1 Slides: Functions</td><td>`3-1-1-slides`</td><td>**existing**, unchanged</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.1.2</td><td>reading</td><td>3.1.2 Reading: Defining & Calling a Function</td><td>`3-1-2-reading-function-basics`</td><td>**existing**, unchanged. Book §3.1.1–3.1.2 overview</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.1.3</td><td>quiz</td><td>3.1.3 Quiz: Identify the Function Calls</td><td>`3-1-11-quiz-identify-calls`</td><td>net new. Book §3.1.1 Concepts in Practice + Problem Set 3.1.1–3.1.3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.1.4</td><td>example</td><td>3.1.4 Worked Example: Defining, Calling, and Hoisting</td><td>`3-1-12-example-defining-hoisting`</td><td>net new. Book Examples 3.1.2–3.1.3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.1.5</td><td>lab</td><td>3.1.5 Lab: Fix the Syntax and the Name</td><td>`3-1-13-lab-syntax-naming`</td><td>net new. Book §3.1.2 Concepts in Practice + Try It Now 3.1.2–3.1.3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.1.6</td><td>reading</td><td>3.1.6 Reading: Why Bother?</td><td>`3-1-14-reading-benefits`</td><td>net new. Book §3.1.3 intro (modularity, reusability, maintainability)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.1.7</td><td>example</td><td>3.1.7 Worked Example: Refactoring calcDistance</td><td>`3-1-15-example-refactor-calcdistance`</td><td>net new. Book Example 3.1.4 — ends on the same parameters-preview beat the book itself uses</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.1.8</td><td>lab</td><td>3.1.8 Lab: Concessions Stand</td><td>`3-1-16-lab-concessions-stand`</td><td>net new. Book Try It Now 3.1.4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.1.9</td><td>lab</td><td>3.1.9 Lab: Terms and Conditions Loop</td><td>`3-1-17-lab-terms-loop`</td><td>net new. Book Try It Now 3.1.5 — a function called from a loop, no parameters</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.1.10</td><td>lab</td><td>3.1.10 Lab: Refactor the Receipt Header</td><td>`3-1-18-lab-refactor-receipt-header`</td><td>net new. Book Try It Now 3.1.6 — direct rehearsal for 3.1.12's flowchart gate</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.1.11</td><td>reading</td><td>3.1.11 Reading: The Function-Call Shape</td><td>`3-1-2a-reading-the-function-call-shape`</td><td>**existing**, title renumber only (was 3.1.3). Moved here from early in the sequence — its own text (\"you have just written your first function\") assumes the reader has already practiced defining/calling</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.1.12</td><td>assignment</td><td>3.1.12 Chart Before You Refactor</td><td>`3-1-7a-a3-1-0-flowchart-gate`</td><td>**existing**, title renumber only (was 3.1.4). Gate for 3.1.13</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.1.13</td><td>lab</td><td>3.1.13 Lab: Refactor the Grade Advisor</td><td>`3-1-19-lab-refactor-grade-advisor`</td><td>net new — **this lab did not exist**; 3.1.12's own content names `decideGrade`/`printAdvice` and calls itself \"the gate for the refactor lab,\" but no refactor lab was ever built. See Note.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.1.14</td><td>diagram</td><td>3.1.14 Chart What You Built</td><td>`3-1-21-chart-built-grade-advisor`</td><td>net new, added 2026-09-15. The after-bookend to 3.1.12's before-chart: draw the refactored program using the double-rail shape for `decideGrade()`/`printAdvice()`, and notice the chart has no decision diamond left at this level</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.1.15</td><td>assignment</td><td>3.1.15 Preview: findMax & isEven</td><td>`3-1-8-lab-findmax-iseven`</td><td>**existing**, title renumber + retitle only (was 3.1.5, \"findMax & isEven\"). See Note on why this stays a preview rather than moving to 3.2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.1.16</td><td>assignment</td><td>3.1.16 Preview: Sum 1 to N</td><td>`3-1-9-lab-sum-to-n`</td><td>**existing**, title renumber + retitle only (was 3.1.6, \"Sum 1 to N\")</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.1.17</td><td>assignment</td><td>3.1.17 Preview: Functions with Parameters and Arrow Syntax</td><td>`3-1-10-functions`</td><td>**existing**, title renumber + retitle only (was 3.1.7, \"Functions\")</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.1.18</td><td>quiz</td><td>3.1.18 Functions: Definition and Calls</td><td>`3-1-20-quiz`</td><td>net new, closing capstone. Problem Set 3.1</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Note</h2>\n\n<p>Book §3.1 Functions: Definition and Calls. Currently <strong>7 lessons</strong>; target <strong>18</strong> per</p>\n<p>the 2026-09-15 build pass (17 in the first pass, plus <code>3.1.14 Chart What You Built</code></p>\n<p>added the same day after a user follow-up asked for flowchart coverage on the new</p>\n<p>refactor lab).</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Module Goal above was wrong until this pass.</strong> It used to claim §3.1 covers</p>\n<p>\"parameters and return values... scope... pass by value vs reference\" — that was</p>\n<p>leftover prose from the pre-2026-08-16 combined \"1.3 Functions and Data\" unit spec,</p>\n<p>never corrected when 3.2 (Parameters and Return Values) and 3.6 (Pass by</p>\n<p>Value/Reference) were split out. The book's own §3.1 learning objectives are narrower:</p>\n<p>identify calls, define a <em>parameterless</em> function, describe benefits. Fixed above.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Three existing lessons preview 3.2/3.4 on purpose, not by mistake.</strong> <code>3-1-8-lab-findmax-iseven</code>,</p>\n<p><code>3-1-9-lab-sum-to-n</code>, and <code>3-1-10-functions</code> all require parameters and return values —</p>\n<p>outside §3.1's own scope, and they carry <code>1.3.1g</code>/<code>1.3.1h</code> comments dating them to the</p>\n<p>old combined unit. They were not reassigned to 3.2 when the split happened, unlike</p>\n<p><code>3-1-3</code> through <code>3-1-7</code>, which <em>were</em> renumbered into 3.2 and 3.6 in the 2026-09-13</p>\n<p>build. Rather than move these three now (which would touch 3.2's and 3.4's already-built</p>\n<p>numbering — out of scope for a §3.1 density pass), this build keeps them in place and</p>\n<p>reframes them honestly: retitled \"Preview: …\" and resequenced to the end, after 3.1's</p>\n<p>own parameterless content is complete. This mirrors what the book itself does — Example</p>\n<p>3.1.4 hands the reader a 4-parameter <code>calcDistance()</code> and says outright \"those are</p>\n<p>parameters, and they are the whole subject of Section 3.2. For now, notice only what</p>\n<p>they bought you.\" Moving these three into 3.2/3.4's numbering is a legitimate follow-up,</p>\n<p>just a separate one.</p>\n\n<p><strong>3.1.12's missing partner lab, now built.</strong> <code>3-1-7a-a3-1-0-flowchart-gate</code>'s own</p>\n<p>content describes itself as \"the gate for the refactor lab\" and names the two functions</p>\n<p>a correct solution will contain — <code>decideGrade</code>, <code>printAdvice</code> — but no lesson calling</p>\n<p>itself that refactor lab existed anywhere in the lesson tree. <code>3-1-19-lab-refactor-grade-advisor</code></p>\n<p>(3.1.13) is it: it takes <code>2-1-38-a2-1-1-grade-advisor</code>'s program (Module 2.1) and has</p>\n<p>the student decompose it into exactly those two named, parameterless functions, reading</p>\n<p><code>score</code>/<code>attendance</code>/<code>lateAssignments</code> from outer scope rather than taking them as</p>\n<p>arguments — keeping the lab inside §3.1's no-parameters boundary the same way the gate's</p>\n<p>own content does.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Folder numbers do not match title numbers for the carried-over lessons.</strong> <code>3-1-2a-reading-the-function-call-shape</code></p>\n<p>and <code>3-1-7a-a3-1-0-flowchart-gate</code> keep their historical slugs because <code>lesson_state.lesson_id</code></p>\n<p>is the folder name and renaming one orphans student progress; only their <code>title</code> numbers</p>\n<p>moved (to 3.1.11 and 3.1.12). The ten net-new lessons above are minted at slugs</p>\n<p><code>3-1-11</code> through <code>3-1-20</code> rather than filling the \"missing\" <code>3-1-3</code>–<code>3-1-7</code> numbers,</p>\n<p>because those folder names are already on disk — owned by 3.2 and 3.6 — and reusing</p>\n<p>them would collide.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Still missing before this is buildable at full spec fidelity</strong>: Videos table with</p>\n<p>per-video description hooks, Day Plan, and Teacher Notes</p>\n<p>(<code>sub-module-spec-conventions.md</code> §2, items 7, 12, 15) — none block building the</p>\n<p>lessons themselves, which is what this pass does.</p>\n"},"3.2":{"title":"Parameters and Return Values","html":"\n<h1>Module 3.2: Parameters and Return Values</h1>\n\n<h2>Module Goal</h2>\n<p>Students pass values into a function and hand a result back with <code>return</code>, and can say why a function that prints is not the same as a function that returns.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Module 3.1 (Functions: Definition and Calls) complete.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Students write functions that take parameters and return values: the shape every later chapter is built from.</p>\n\n<h2>Numbered Lesson List</h2>\n\n<p>Re-audited 2026-09-13 against <code>outline.md</code>'s density bar (built modules run 20–30</p>\n<p>lessons per book section, not 1 lesson per subsection). §3.2 has 6 numbered book</p>\n<p>subsections (3.2.1–3.2.6); this list allocates 3–4 lesson slots per subsection per</p>\n<p>the one-concept-per-lesson rule (<code>sub-module-spec-conventions.md</code> §2a).</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Slot</th><th>Type</th><th>Title</th><th>Slug</th><th>Notes</th></tr>\n<tr><td>3.2.1</td><td>slides</td><td>3.2.1 Slides: Parameters and Return Values</td><td>`3-2-1-slides-parameters-return`</td><td>net new. NOTE: do not use bare `3-2-1-slides` — that folder already exists (Module 3.3's slides lesson, pre-renumber folder name)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.2.2</td><td>reading</td><td>3.2.2 Reading: Parameters & Return Values</td><td>`3-1-3-reading-parameters-return`</td><td>**existing**, title renumber only (was titled 3.2.1)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.2.3</td><td>reading</td><td>3.2.3 Reading: Passing Information In</td><td>`3-2-3-reading-passing-information-in`</td><td>book §3.2.1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.2.4</td><td>example</td><td>3.2.4 Worked Example: a function with one parameter</td><td>`3-2-4-example-one-parameter`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.2.5</td><td>reading</td><td>3.2.5 Reading: Parameters and Arguments</td><td>`3-2-5-reading-parameters-arguments`</td><td>book §3.2.2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.2.6</td><td>reading</td><td>3.2.6 Reading: Order Matters</td><td>`3-2-6-reading-order-matters`</td><td>positional arguments</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.2.7</td><td>lab</td><td>3.2.7 Lab: findRectangleArea (multi-parameter)</td><td>`3-2-7-lab-rectangle-area`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.2.8</td><td>lab</td><td>3.2.8 Chart the Code: Tracing Parameter Values</td><td>`3-2-8-chart-parameter-trace`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.2.9</td><td>reading</td><td>3.2.9 Reading: Getting a Value Back with `return`</td><td>`3-2-9-reading-return`</td><td>book §3.2.3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.2.10</td><td>example</td><td>3.2.10 Worked Example: return instead of print</td><td>`3-2-10-example-return-vs-print`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.2.11</td><td>reading</td><td>3.2.11 Reading: `return` Ends the Function</td><td>`3-2-11-reading-return-ends-function`</td><td>book §3.2.4, early exit</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.2.12</td><td>lab</td><td>3.2.12 Lab: an Early-Return Guard Clause</td><td>`3-2-12-lab-guard-clause`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.2.13</td><td>reading</td><td>3.2.13 Reading: Printing Is Not Returning</td><td>`3-2-13-reading-printing-not-returning`</td><td>book §3.2.5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.2.14</td><td>example</td><td>3.2.14 Worked Example: Which Should a Function Do?</td><td>`3-2-14-example-print-or-return`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.2.15</td><td>lab</td><td>3.2.15 Lab: Fix a Function That Prints Instead of Returning</td><td>`3-2-15-lab-fix-print-not-return`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.2.16</td><td>reading</td><td>3.2.16 Reading: Building With Returned Values</td><td>`3-2-16-reading-building-with-returns`</td><td>book §3.2.6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.2.17</td><td>example</td><td>3.2.17 Worked Example: Chaining Two Functions</td><td>`3-2-17-example-chaining-functions`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.2.18</td><td>lab</td><td>3.2.18 Chart the Code: Data Flow Through Chained Calls</td><td>`3-2-18-chart-chained-calls`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.2.19</td><td>lab</td><td>3.2.19 Lab: Compose Two Functions</td><td>`3-2-19-lab-compose-functions`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.2.20</td><td>reading</td><td>3.2.20 Reading: Scope: Local vs Global</td><td>`3-1-4-reading-scope`</td><td>**existing**, title renumber only (was titled 3.2.2). Book-gap filler — no book §3.2 subsection covers scope; see `BOOK-TO-MODULE.md` §8. Do not delete on a resync for being absent from the book.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.2.21</td><td>lab</td><td>3.2.21 Lab: Scope Prediction Exercise</td><td>`3-2-21-lab-scope-prediction`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.2.22</td><td>assignment</td><td>3.2.22 Parameters and Return Values</td><td>`3-2-22-parameters-return-quiz`</td><td>capstone/unit quiz</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Note</h2>\n<p>Book §3.2 Parameters and Return Values. Currently <strong>2 lessons</strong>; target <strong>22</strong> per the</p>\n<p>2026-09-13 density re-audit (see <code>outline.md</code>'s chapter-3 re-audit note and the ch3</p>\n<p>shCode-modules proposal thread).</p>\n\n<p>This module was split out of module 3.1, which was carrying three book sections at once on 2026-08-16. The lessons were</p>\n<p>already written and taught this material: they were simply numbered into a</p>\n<p>neighbouring section, so the book's own section never appeared in the app.</p>\n<p>Nothing was rewritten; the lessons were renumbered and their <code>unit</code> label</p>\n<p>corrected.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Thin: 2 lessons against a 749-line book section.</strong> Needs building out before Oct 07, 2026.</p>\n<p>The Numbered Lesson List above is the target; only §3.2.2 and §3.2.20 are built today.</p>\n<p>Inserting the other 20 requires a full sequential renumber of this module's lesson</p>\n<p><em>titles</em> when built (letter-suffix inserts are retired, <code>sub-module-spec-conventions.md</code></p>\n<p>§4.1) — the two existing lessons' <strong>folders</strong> keep their historical slugs</p>\n<p>(<code>3-1-3-reading-parameters-return</code>, <code>3-1-4-reading-scope</code>) per <code>lesson_state.lesson_id</code>;</p>\n<p>only their <code>title</code> numbers move.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Still missing before this is buildable</strong>: Videos/Readings tables with per-reading</p>\n<p>content guidance, full Worked Example code, Day Plan, Assignment pass-criteria,</p>\n<p>Vocabulary table, and Teacher Notes (<code>sub-module-spec-conventions.md</code> §2, items 7–15).</p>\n<p>The Numbered Lesson List is the build target and is now current; those sections are not.</p>\n\n<h3>⚠ Folder numbers do not match title numbers here</h3>\n\n<p>These lessons keep their original folder names, because <code>lesson_state.lesson_id</code></p>\n<p>is the folder name and renaming one orphans student progress. Order comes from</p>\n<p>the number in <code>lesson.json.title</code>. See <code>BOOK-TO-MODULE.md</code> §7.3.</p>\n"},"3.3":{"title":"Arrays","html":"\n<h1>Module 3.3: Arrays</h1>\n\n<h2>Module Goal</h2>\n<p>Students store a list in an array, read it by index, mutate it by index, add and remove items from either end, walk it with a loop, pass it into and build one from a function, and work with a list of lists.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Module 3.2 (Parameters and Return Values) complete.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Students can hold many values in one variable, process them in turn, and pass whole lists through functions — the shape Module 3.7 (Array Methods) builds on next.</p>\n\n<h2>Density re-audit (2026-09-14)</h2>\n\n<p>Book §3.3 Arrays (<code>bookSHelf/.../Chapter_3_Remastered/3.3_arrays.md</code>, 893 lines) has</p>\n<p>7 numbered subsections. Diffing those against the 8 built lessons (concepts, not</p>\n<p>prose, per <code>BOOK-TO-MODULE.md</code> §6 step 2) found **4 subsections with zero lesson</p>\n<p>coverage**, not a general thinness:</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Book subsection</th><th>Concept</th><th>Covered today?</th></tr>\n<tr><td>3.3.1 When One Name Is Not Enough</td><td>motivation</td><td>slides only</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.3.2 Making a List and Reading From It</td><td>array, index, length, push, pop</td><td>yes — `3-2-2-reading-array-basics`</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**3.3.3 Changing What Is in a List**</td><td>**index assignment (`arr[i] = x`)**</td><td>**no lesson anywhere**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**3.3.4 Adding and Removing at the Ends**</td><td>push/pop (front half only)</td><td>**shift/unshift not covered**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.3.5 Walking Through a List</td><td>for / for...of</td><td>yes — `3-2-3-reading-array-iteration`, `3-2-6-example-menu-loop`, `3-3-11-chart-the-array-loop`</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**3.3.6 Arrays and Functions**</td><td>passing an array into a function; building one with a function</td><td>**no lesson anywhere**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**3.3.7 Lists Inside Lists**</td><td>nested arrays</td><td>**no lesson anywhere**</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>Target is <strong>18 lessons</strong> (8 existing + 10 net-new covering exactly the 4 gap</p>\n<p>concepts above) — not the 20–30 density bar mechanically applied. Sizing follows</p>\n<p>concept count, not a target-shape ceiling (<code>sub-module-spec-conventions.md</code> §2a,</p>\n<p>BOOK-TO-MODULE.md \"sizing is honest\"): every real concept in the book section now</p>\n<p>has a slot, and no slot is invented to pad toward a round number.</p>\n\n<h2>Numbered Lesson List</h2>\n\n<p>Letter-suffix inserts are retired (<code>sub-module-spec-conventions.md</code> §4.1) — inserting</p>\n<p>mid-sequence means every lesson after the insertion point gets its <strong>title</strong></p>\n<p>renumbered. <strong>Folders never rename</strong>: existing lessons keep their historical</p>\n<p><code>3-2-*</code> slugs (pre-existing from before this module was split out of 3.2); only</p>\n<p>new lessons get fresh <code>3-3-*</code> folder names.</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Slot</th><th>Type</th><th>Title</th><th>Slug</th><th>Notes</th></tr>\n<tr><td>3.3.1</td><td>slides</td><td>3.3.1 Slides: Arrays</td><td>`3-2-1-slides`</td><td>existing, unchanged</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.3.2</td><td>reading</td><td>3.3.2 Reading: Array Basics: Index, push, pop</td><td>`3-2-2-reading-array-basics`</td><td>existing, unchanged</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.3.3</td><td>reading</td><td>3.3.3 Reading: Changing an Item by Index</td><td>`3-3-3-reading-index-assignment`</td><td>net new — book §3.3.3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.3.4</td><td>lab</td><td>3.3.4 Lab: Update Scores by Index</td><td>`3-3-4-lab-update-by-index`</td><td>net new</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.3.5</td><td>reading</td><td>3.3.5 Reading: Looping Over Arrays (for / for…of)</td><td>`3-2-3-reading-array-iteration`</td><td>existing, **title renumber only** (was 3.3.3)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.3.6</td><td>reading</td><td>3.3.6 Reading: Adding and Removing from the Front</td><td>`3-3-6-reading-shift-unshift`</td><td>net new — book §3.3.4's other half</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.3.7</td><td>lab</td><td>3.3.7 Lab: Front-of-Line Queue</td><td>`3-3-7-lab-shift-unshift-queue`</td><td>net new</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.3.8</td><td>reading</td><td>3.3.8 Reading: Splitting Text into Lines (.split)</td><td>`3-2-4-reading-split-lines`</td><td>existing, **title renumber only** (was 3.3.4). Designed/load-bearing, no book §3.3 anchor — do not delete on a future resync</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.3.9</td><td>assignment</td><td>3.3.9 Sum an Array</td><td>`3-2-5-lab-sum-array`</td><td>existing, **title renumber only** (was 3.3.5)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.3.10</td><td>example</td><td>3.3.10 Worked Example: A tiny menu loop</td><td>`3-2-6-example-menu-loop`</td><td>existing, **title renumber only** (was 3.3.6)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.3.11</td><td>diagram</td><td>3.3.11 Chart the Code: Looping Over an Array</td><td>`3-3-11-chart-the-array-loop`</td><td>existing, **title renumber, and folder renamed 2026-09-15** (was 3.3.7, folder `3-2-6a-chart-the-array-loop`; 2 `lesson_state` rows + 1 `lesson_drafts` row repointed in production D1 at the same time)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.3.12</td><td>reading</td><td>3.3.12 Reading: Arrays and Functions</td><td>`3-3-12-reading-arrays-and-functions`</td><td>net new — book §3.3.6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.3.13</td><td>example</td><td>3.3.13 Worked Example: Keeping Only the Passing Scores</td><td>`3-3-13-example-filter-passing-scores`</td><td>net new — mirrors book Example 3.4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.3.14</td><td>assignment</td><td>3.3.14 Lab: Write a Function That Filters an Array</td><td>`3-3-14-lab-filter-function`</td><td>net new</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.3.15</td><td>reading</td><td>3.3.15 Reading: Lists Inside Lists</td><td>`3-3-15-reading-nested-arrays`</td><td>net new — book §3.3.7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.3.16</td><td>example</td><td>3.3.16 Worked Example: A Tic-Tac-Toe Board as a Nested Array</td><td>`3-3-16-example-nested-array-grid`</td><td>net new</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.3.17</td><td>assignment</td><td>3.3.17 Lab: Read and Update a Nested Array</td><td>`3-3-17-lab-nested-array-update`</td><td>net new</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.3.18</td><td>assignment</td><td>3.3.18 Arrays</td><td>`3-2-7-arrays`</td><td>existing, **title renumber only** (was 3.3.8) — stays the module capstone</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Note</h2>\n<p>Book §3.3 Arrays. <strong>8 lessons built, target 18</strong> (see re-audit above).</p>\n\n<p>This module was split out of module 3.2, which was numbered as if Arrays were the book's second section rather than its third on 2026-08-16. The lessons were</p>\n<p>already written and taught this material: they were simply numbered into a</p>\n<p>neighbouring section, so the book's own section never appeared in the app.</p>\n<p>Nothing was rewritten; the lessons were renumbered and their <code>unit</code> label</p>\n<p>corrected.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Still missing before this is <code>ready</code></strong>: Reading content guidance, full Worked</p>\n<p>Example code, and Assignment pass-criteria for the 10 net-new lessons per</p>\n<p><code>sub-module-spec-conventions.md</code> §2 items 8, 10, 13 — see <code>SPEC-3-3-arrays-build.md</code></p>\n<p>at the repo root, which supplies those for the build pass.</p>\n\n<h3>⚠ Folder numbers do not match title numbers here</h3>\n\n<p>These lessons keep their original folder names, because <code>lesson_state.lesson_id</code></p>\n<p>is the folder name and renaming one orphans student progress. Order comes from</p>\n<p>the number in <code>lesson.json.title</code>. See <code>BOOK-TO-MODULE.md</code> §7.3.</p>\n"},"3.4":{"title":"Function Expressions and Arrow Functions","html":"\n<h1>Module 3.4: Function Expressions and Arrow Functions</h1>\n\n<h2>Module Goal</h2>\n<p>Students write the same function three ways (declaration, expression, arrow), know when the shorthand rules apply, and pass a function as an argument to another function.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Module 3.3 (Arrays) complete.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Students can write and pass a short callback-shaped function — the form Module 3.7 (Array Methods), Module 8.5 (JSCAD array loops), and every later moSHion callback depend on.</p>\n\n<h2>Numbered Lesson List</h2>\n\n<p>New module, 2026-09-13. §3.4 has 6 numbered book subsections (3.4.1–3.4.6), zero</p>\n<p>built lessons today. Sized at 21 lessons per the chapter's density re-audit (built</p>\n<p>modules like 1.2 run ~3.4 lessons per book subsection; §3.4's own audit finding — every</p>\n<p>downstream <code>.map()</code> callback uses an arrow, never a named function — makes the</p>\n<p>implicit-return and callback-passing slots load-bearing, not optional practice).</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Slot</th><th>Type</th><th>Title</th><th>Slug</th><th>Notes</th></tr>\n<tr><td>3.4.1</td><td>slides</td><td>3.4.1 Slides: Function Expressions and Arrow Functions</td><td>`3-4-1-slides`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.4.2</td><td>reading</td><td>3.4.2 Reading: A Function Is a Value</td><td>`3-4-2-reading-function-is-a-value`</td><td>book §3.4.1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.4.3</td><td>example</td><td>3.4.3 Worked Example: Storing a Function in a Variable</td><td>`3-4-3-example-function-in-variable`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.4.4</td><td>reading</td><td>3.4.4 Reading: Function Expressions</td><td>`3-4-4-reading-function-expressions`</td><td>book §3.4.2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.4.5</td><td>lab</td><td>3.4.5 Lab: Rewrite a Declaration as an Expression</td><td>`3-4-5-lab-declaration-to-expression`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.4.6</td><td>reading</td><td>3.4.6 Reading: Arrow Functions</td><td>`3-4-6-reading-arrow-functions`</td><td>book §3.4.3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.4.7</td><td>example</td><td>3.4.7 Worked Example: The Same Function Three Ways</td><td>`3-4-7-example-three-forms`</td><td>declaration / expression / arrow</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.4.8</td><td>reading</td><td>3.4.8 Reading: The Shorthand Rules</td><td>`3-4-8-reading-shorthand-rules`</td><td>book §3.4.4, implicit return</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.4.9</td><td>lab</td><td>3.4.9 Chart the Code: Expanding an Arrow to Its Full Form</td><td>`3-4-9-chart-expand-arrow`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.4.10</td><td>lab</td><td>3.4.10 Lab: Implicit-Return Rewrite Drill</td><td>`3-4-10-lab-implicit-return-drill`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.4.11</td><td>reading</td><td>3.4.11 Reading: Passing a Function to a Function</td><td>`3-4-11-reading-passing-a-function`</td><td>book §3.4.5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.4.12</td><td>example</td><td>3.4.12 Worked Example: An Arrow Passed as a Callback</td><td>`3-4-12-example-arrow-callback`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.4.13</td><td>lab</td><td>3.4.13 Lab: Write a One-Line Arrow Callback</td><td>`3-4-13-lab-one-line-callback`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.4.14</td><td>reading</td><td>3.4.14 Reading: Declaration or Expression?</td><td>`3-4-14-reading-declaration-or-expression`</td><td>book §3.4.6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.4.15</td><td>lab</td><td>3.4.15 Chart the Code: Decision Tree for Form Choice</td><td>`3-4-15-chart-form-decision`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.4.16</td><td>lab</td><td>3.4.16 Lab: Rewrite 3 Named Functions as Arrows</td><td>`3-4-16-lab-rewrite-three-arrows`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.4.17</td><td>lab</td><td>3.4.17 Lab: Fix a Broken Arrow</td><td>`3-4-17-lab-fix-broken-arrow`</td><td>missing parens on 2+ params</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.4.18</td><td>example</td><td>3.4.18 Worked Example: An Arrow With No Parameters</td><td>`3-4-18-example-zero-param-arrow`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.4.19</td><td>lab</td><td>3.4.19 Lab: Callback Capstone</td><td>`3-4-19-lab-callback-capstone`</td><td>pass an arrow into a provided helper</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.4.20</td><td>lab</td><td>3.4.20 Chart the Code: Full Syntax-Forms Comparison</td><td>`3-4-20-chart-forms-comparison`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.4.21</td><td>assignment</td><td>3.4.21 Function Expressions and Arrow Functions</td><td>`3-4-21-arrow-functions-quiz`</td><td>capstone/unit quiz</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Note</h2>\n<p>Book §3.4 Function Expressions and Arrow Functions. Currently <strong>0 lessons</strong>; target</p>\n<p><strong>21</strong> per the 2026-09-13 chapter-3 density re-audit (<code>outline.md</code>'s re-audit note,</p>\n<p>this module's usage audit).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Still missing before this is buildable</strong>: Videos/Readings tables with per-reading</p>\n<p>content guidance, full Worked Example code, Day Plan, Assignment pass-criteria,</p>\n<p>Vocabulary table, and Teacher Notes (<code>sub-module-spec-conventions.md</code> §2, items 7–15).</p>\n<p>The Numbered Lesson List above is the build target; nothing else in the formal spec</p>\n<p>shape exists yet.</p>\n"},"3.5":{"title":"Objects and Properties","html":"\n<h1>Module 3.5: Objects and Properties</h1>\n\n<h2>Module Goal</h2>\n<p>Students group related values in an object, read and change properties by dot or bracket, work with nested objects, attach methods, and destructure fields out of an object.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Module 3.4 (Function Expressions and Arrow Functions) complete.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Students can model a record as an object and pull fields out of it by destructuring — the shape Module 5.3 (Classes and Objects via moSHion) builds instances on, and the pattern that closes the object-literal/property-access gap the original 2026-08-07 audit flagged (140/133 downstream uses against 6/12 taught).</p>\n\n<h2>Numbered Lesson List</h2>\n\n<p>New module, 2026-09-13. §3.5 has 7 numbered book subsections (3.5.1–3.5.7), zero built</p>\n<p>lessons today, and is this chapter's largest book section (796 lines) — sized at 26</p>\n<p>lessons, the top of the chapter's density range. Includes one lesson closing a real</p>\n<p>vocabulary gap found by the ch3 usage audit: \"record\" is used in the book's own prose</p>\n<p>(§3.5) with no definition anywhere in the course.</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Slot</th><th>Type</th><th>Title</th><th>Slug</th><th>Notes</th></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.1</td><td>slides</td><td>3.5.1 Slides: Objects and Properties</td><td>`3-5-1-slides`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.2</td><td>reading</td><td>3.5.2 Reading: Grouping Related Values</td><td>`3-5-2-reading-grouping-values`</td><td>book §3.5.1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.3</td><td>example</td><td>3.5.3 Worked Example: An Object Literal for One Record</td><td>`3-5-3-example-object-literal`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.4</td><td>reading</td><td>3.5.4 Reading: Reading and Changing Properties</td><td>`3-5-4-reading-properties`</td><td>book §3.5.2, dot access</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.5</td><td>lab</td><td>3.5.5 Lab: Read and Update Object Fields</td><td>`3-5-5-lab-read-update-fields`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.6</td><td>reading</td><td>3.5.6 Reading: Square Brackets and Computed Keys</td><td>`3-5-6-reading-computed-keys`</td><td>book §3.5.3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.7</td><td>example</td><td>3.5.7 Worked Example: Bracket Access With a Variable Key</td><td>`3-5-7-example-bracket-access`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.8</td><td>lab</td><td>3.5.8 Lab: Dot vs. Bracket — When You're Forced to Bracket</td><td>`3-5-8-lab-dot-vs-bracket`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.9</td><td>reading</td><td>3.5.9 Reading: Objects Inside Objects and Arrays</td><td>`3-5-9-reading-nested-objects`</td><td>book §3.5.4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.10</td><td>example</td><td>3.5.10 Worked Example: A Nested Object Access Chain</td><td>`3-5-10-example-nested-access`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.11</td><td>lab</td><td>3.5.11 Chart the Code: Tracing a Nested Access</td><td>`3-5-11-chart-nested-access`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.12</td><td>lab</td><td>3.5.12 Lab: Build a Small Nested-Object Structure</td><td>`3-5-12-lab-nested-structure`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.13</td><td>reading</td><td>3.5.13 Reading: Methods</td><td>`3-5-13-reading-methods`</td><td>book §3.5.5, functions as properties</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.14</td><td>example</td><td>3.5.14 Worked Example: An Object With a Method</td><td>`3-5-14-example-object-method`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.15</td><td>lab</td><td>3.5.15 Lab: Add a Method to an Object</td><td>`3-5-15-lab-add-method`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.16</td><td>reading</td><td>3.5.16 Reading: Objects as Named Arguments</td><td>`3-5-16-reading-named-arguments`</td><td>book §3.5.6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.17</td><td>example</td><td>3.5.17 Worked Example: A Function Taking One Options Object</td><td>`3-5-17-example-options-object`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.18</td><td>lab</td><td>3.5.18 Lab: Rewrite a Multi-Parameter Function to Take an Object</td><td>`3-5-18-lab-params-to-object`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.19</td><td>reading</td><td>3.5.19 Reading: Destructuring</td><td>`3-5-19-reading-destructuring`</td><td>book §3.5.7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.20</td><td>example</td><td>3.5.20 Worked Example: Destructuring in a Parameter List</td><td>`3-5-20-example-destructure-param`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.21</td><td>lab</td><td>3.5.21 Chart the Code: Destructuring vs. Plain Access</td><td>`3-5-21-chart-destructure-vs-access`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.22</td><td>lab</td><td>3.5.22 Lab: Destructure 3 Fields From an Object</td><td>`3-5-22-lab-destructure-three`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.23</td><td>example</td><td>3.5.23 Worked Example: A Record-Shaped Object</td><td>`3-5-23-example-record-shape`</td><td>closes \"record\" vocab gap, ch3 usage audit 2026-09-13</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.24</td><td>lab</td><td>3.5.24 Lab: Parallel-Arrays-vs-Object Refactor</td><td>`3-5-24-lab-parallel-arrays-refactor`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.25</td><td>lab</td><td>3.5.25 Lab: Objects Capstone — Build, Read, Destructure</td><td>`3-5-25-lab-objects-capstone`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5.26</td><td>assignment</td><td>3.5.26 Objects and Properties</td><td>`3-5-26-objects-quiz`</td><td>capstone/unit quiz</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Note</h2>\n<p>Book §3.5 Objects and Properties. Currently <strong>0 lessons</strong>; target <strong>26</strong> per the</p>\n<p>2026-09-13 chapter-3 density re-audit.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Still missing before this is buildable</strong>: Videos/Readings tables with per-reading</p>\n<p>content guidance, full Worked Example code, Day Plan, Assignment pass-criteria,</p>\n<p>Vocabulary table (should include \"record\" — see 3.5.23), and Teacher Notes</p>\n<p>(<code>sub-module-spec-conventions.md</code> §2, items 7–15).</p>\n"},"3.6":{"title":"Functions: Pass by Value/Reference","html":"\n<h1>Module 3.6: Functions: Pass by Value/Reference</h1>\n\n<h2>Module Goal</h2>\n<p>Students explain why changing a number inside a function leaves the original alone while changing an array does not, and copy defensively when it matters.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Module 3.5 (Objects and Properties) complete.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Students can predict whether a function call will alter the caller's data.</p>\n\n<h2>Numbered Lesson List</h2>\n\n<p>Re-audited 2026-09-13. §3.6 is genuinely the chapter's smallest book section (384</p>\n<p>lines, 3 subsections, <strong>zero</strong> <code>&lt;h3&gt;</code> sub-concepts — checked, nothing hidden), so this</p>\n<p>list targets 17, not the 20–30 used elsewhere in this chapter: padding a flat, narrow</p>\n<p>topic to a uniform quota would mean inventing lesson slots with no new concept behind</p>\n<p>them, which the granularity bar (<code>sub-module-spec-conventions.md</code> §2a) exists to</p>\n<p>prevent. The added reps are real distinctions the book doesn't subsection separately</p>\n<p>(array- vs. object- vs. nested-parameter mutation are different student failure modes)</p>\n<p>plus one genuine downstream tie-in found by the ch3 usage audit: 6.2's</p>\n<p><code>for (const b of [...bullets])</code> defensive-copy pattern is this module's own lesson,</p>\n<p>in the wild.</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Slot</th><th>Type</th><th>Title</th><th>Slug</th><th>Notes</th></tr>\n<tr><td>3.6.1</td><td>slides</td><td>3.6.1 Slides: Pass by Value/Reference</td><td>`3-6-1-slides`</td><td>net new</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.6.2</td><td>reading</td><td>3.6.2 Reading: Pass by Value vs Reference</td><td>`3-1-5-reading-pass-by-value-reference`</td><td>**existing**, title renumber only (was 3.6.1). Book §3.6.1 What Gets Passed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.6.3</td><td>example</td><td>3.6.3 Worked Example: Mutating an Array Parameter</td><td>`3-6-3-example-mutating-array-param`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.6.4</td><td>lab</td><td>3.6.4 Lab: Predict Output — Primitive vs. Array Argument</td><td>`3-6-4-lab-predict-primitive-vs-array`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.6.5</td><td>reading</td><td>3.6.5 Reading: Changing vs. Reassigning</td><td>`3-6-5-reading-changing-vs-reassigning`</td><td>book §3.6.2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.6.6</td><td>example</td><td>3.6.6 Worked Example: Reassigning a Parameter Doesn't Escape</td><td>`3-6-6-example-reassign-doesnt-escape`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.6.7</td><td>lab</td><td>3.6.7 Chart the Code: Reassignment vs. Mutation Trace</td><td>`3-6-7-chart-reassign-vs-mutate`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.6.8</td><td>lab</td><td>3.6.8 Lab: Fix a Function That Reassigns Instead of Mutating</td><td>`3-6-8-lab-fix-reassign-bug`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.6.9</td><td>reading</td><td>3.6.9 Reading: Side Effects and Copies</td><td>`3-6-9-reading-side-effects-copies`</td><td>book §3.6.3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.6.10</td><td>reading</td><td>3.6.10 Reading: Defensive Copying — `[...]` and `{...}`</td><td>`3-1-6-reading-defensive-copying`</td><td>**existing**, title renumber only (was 3.6.2)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.6.11</td><td>example</td><td>3.6.11 Worked Example: Defensive Copy Before Mutating</td><td>`3-6-11-example-defensive-copy`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.6.12</td><td>lab</td><td>3.6.12 Lab: Object-Parameter Mutation</td><td>`3-6-12-lab-object-param-mutation`</td><td>distinct case from 3.6.4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.6.13</td><td>lab</td><td>3.6.13 Lab: Nested-Structure Mutation</td><td>`3-6-13-lab-nested-mutation`</td><td>a copy that isn't deep enough</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.6.14</td><td>example</td><td>3.6.14 Worked Example: Why 6.2 Copies Before It Loops</td><td>`3-6-14-example-6-2-defensive-copy`</td><td>real downstream tie-in, ch3 usage audit 2026-09-13</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.6.15</td><td>assignment</td><td>3.6.15 Writeup: Pass by Value vs Reference</td><td>`3-1-7-a7-2-pass-by-reference-writeup`</td><td>**existing**, title renumber only (was 3.6.3). Artifact A7.2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.6.16</td><td>lab</td><td>3.6.16 Lab: Pass-by-Reference Capstone Practice</td><td>`3-6-16-lab-capstone`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.6.17</td><td>assignment</td><td>3.6.17 Pass by Value/Reference</td><td>`3-6-17-pass-by-reference-quiz`</td><td>capstone/unit quiz</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Note</h2>\n<p>Book §3.6 Functions: Pass by Value/Reference. Currently <strong>3 lessons</strong>; target <strong>17</strong> per</p>\n<p>the 2026-09-13 density re-audit — see the module's own note above on why this section</p>\n<p>gets fewer slots than 3.2/3.4/3.5/3.7/3.8 rather than a flat 20–30.</p>\n\n<p>This module was split out of module 3.1, which was carrying three book sections at once on 2026-08-16. The lessons were</p>\n<p>already written and taught this material: they were simply numbered into a</p>\n<p>neighbouring section, so the book's own section never appeared in the app.</p>\n<p>Nothing was rewritten; the lessons were renumbered and their <code>unit</code> label</p>\n<p>corrected.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Thin: 3 lessons against a 385-line book section.</strong> The Numbered Lesson List above is</p>\n<p>the target; only §3.6.2, §3.6.10, and §3.6.15 are built today. Inserting the other 14</p>\n<p>requires a full sequential renumber of this module's lesson <em>titles</em> when built</p>\n<p>(letter-suffix inserts are retired, <code>sub-module-spec-conventions.md</code> §4.1) — the three</p>\n<p>existing lessons' <strong>folders</strong> keep their historical slugs per <code>lesson_state.lesson_id</code>;</p>\n<p>only their <code>title</code> numbers move.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Still missing before this is buildable</strong>: Videos/Readings tables with per-reading</p>\n<p>content guidance, full Worked Example code, Day Plan, Assignment pass-criteria,</p>\n<p>Vocabulary table, and Teacher Notes (<code>sub-module-spec-conventions.md</code> §2, items 7–15).</p>\n\n<h3>⚠ Folder numbers do not match title numbers here</h3>\n\n<p>These lessons keep their original folder names, because <code>lesson_state.lesson_id</code></p>\n<p>is the folder name and renaming one orphans student progress. Order comes from</p>\n<p>the number in <code>lesson.json.title</code>. See <code>BOOK-TO-MODULE.md</code> §7.3.</p>\n"},"3.7":{"title":"Array Methods","html":"\n<h1>Module 3.7: Array Methods</h1>\n\n<h2>Module Goal</h2>\n<p>Students transform, slice, and join arrays without mutating the original, and use spread to build new arrays and objects.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Module 3.6 (Pass by Value/Reference) complete.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Students can transform a list into a new list without touching the original — the pattern Module 8.5 (JSCAD array loops) uses throughout, and the exact non-mutating shape confirmed by the ch3 usage audit's <code>.push()</code>/<code>.map()</code> downstream check.</p>\n\n<h2>Numbered Lesson List</h2>\n\n<p>New module, 2026-09-13. §3.7 has 6 numbered book subsections (3.7.1–3.7.6), zero built</p>\n<p>lessons today. The book deliberately teaches only <code>.map()</code>, <code>.slice()</code>, <code>.concat()</code>, and</p>\n<p>spread (per the original 2026-08-07 audit: of the array methods the source material</p>\n<p>covers, chapters 4+ use exactly these) — §3.7.6 \"What Else Is Out There\" <em>names</em> the</p>\n<p>rest without teaching them, including <code>.every()</code>, added there by the 2026-09-13</p>\n<p>re-audit after it turned up cold in a chapter 5 solution. This module's lesson count</p>\n<p>comes from depth of practice on the taught methods, not topic breadth.</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Slot</th><th>Type</th><th>Title</th><th>Slug</th><th>Notes</th></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7.1</td><td>slides</td><td>3.7.1 Slides: Array Methods</td><td>`3-7-1-slides`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7.2</td><td>reading</td><td>3.7.2 Reading: Transforming a List with `.map()`</td><td>`3-7-2-reading-map`</td><td>book §3.7.1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7.3</td><td>example</td><td>3.7.3 Worked Example: `.map()` Over a Numeric Array</td><td>`3-7-3-example-map-numeric`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7.4</td><td>lab</td><td>3.7.4 Lab: Transform a List Without Mutating the Input</td><td>`3-7-4-lab-map-non-mutating`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7.5</td><td>lab</td><td>3.7.5 Chart the Code: `.map()`'s Callback Trace</td><td>`3-7-5-chart-map-trace`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7.6</td><td>reading</td><td>3.7.6 Reading: Taking Part of a List with `.slice()`</td><td>`3-7-6-reading-slice`</td><td>book §3.7.2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7.7</td><td>example</td><td>3.7.7 Worked Example: `.slice()` Copies a Range</td><td>`3-7-7-example-slice-copy`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7.8</td><td>lab</td><td>3.7.8 Lab: Extract a Sub-List with `.slice()`</td><td>`3-7-8-lab-slice-sublist`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7.9</td><td>reading</td><td>3.7.9 Reading: Joining Lists with `.concat()`</td><td>`3-7-9-reading-concat`</td><td>book §3.7.3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7.10</td><td>example</td><td>3.7.10 Worked Example: `.concat()` Without Mutating Either Input</td><td>`3-7-10-example-concat-non-mutating`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7.11</td><td>lab</td><td>3.7.11 Lab: `.concat()` Two Lists</td><td>`3-7-11-lab-concat-two-lists`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7.12</td><td>reading</td><td>3.7.12 Reading: Spreading an Array into Arguments</td><td>`3-7-12-reading-spread-arguments`</td><td>book §3.7.4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7.13</td><td>example</td><td>3.7.13 Worked Example: `fn(...arr)` Spread-Call</td><td>`3-7-13-example-spread-call`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7.14</td><td>reading</td><td>3.7.14 Reading: Spreading into a New Array or Object</td><td>`3-7-14-reading-spread-into-new`</td><td>book §3.7.5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7.15</td><td>example</td><td>3.7.15 Worked Example: Copying an Object with Changes</td><td>`3-7-15-example-spread-override`</td><td>`{...obj, field}`</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7.16</td><td>lab</td><td>3.7.16 Chart the Code: Spread vs. Rest, Side by Side</td><td>`3-7-16-chart-spread-vs-rest`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7.17</td><td>lab</td><td>3.7.17 Lab: Build a New Array with Spread</td><td>`3-7-17-lab-spread-array`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7.18</td><td>lab</td><td>3.7.18 Lab: Build a New Object with Spread + Override</td><td>`3-7-18-lab-spread-object`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7.19</td><td>reading</td><td>3.7.19 Reading: What Else Is Out There</td><td>`3-7-19-reading-what-else`</td><td>book §3.7.6, named not taught, incl. `.every()`</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7.20</td><td>example</td><td>3.7.20 Worked Example: Chaining `.map()` off a `.push()`-Built Array</td><td>`3-7-20-example-push-then-map`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7.21</td><td>lab</td><td>3.7.21 Lab: Mutating vs. Non-Mutating Method Sort Drill</td><td>`3-7-21-lab-mutating-sort-drill`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7.22</td><td>lab</td><td>3.7.22 Lab: Array-Methods Capstone</td><td>`3-7-22-lab-capstone`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7.23</td><td>assignment</td><td>3.7.23 Array Methods</td><td>`3-7-23-array-methods-quiz`</td><td>capstone/unit quiz</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Note</h2>\n<p>Book §3.7 Array Methods. Currently <strong>0 lessons</strong>; target <strong>23</strong> per the 2026-09-13</p>\n<p>chapter-3 density re-audit. <code>.slice()</code>/<code>.concat()</code> show zero uses in chapters 4-9</p>\n<p>(the only chapters published) per the same audit — kept in the lesson list rather</p>\n<p>than cut, since their justification likely lives in the still-unwritten chapters</p>\n<p>10-13; re-check once those exist (<code>outline.md</code>'s re-audit note).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Still missing before this is buildable</strong>: Videos/Readings tables with per-reading</p>\n<p>content guidance, full Worked Example code, Day Plan, Assignment pass-criteria,</p>\n<p>Vocabulary table, and Teacher Notes (<code>sub-module-spec-conventions.md</code> §2, items 7–15).</p>\n"},"3.8":{"title":"Saving and Loading Data","html":"\n<h1>Module 3.8: Saving and Loading Data</h1>\n\n<h2>Module Goal</h2>\n<p>Students save a value to text with <code>JSON.stringify</code>, restore it with <code>JSON.parse</code>, handle a parse failure, and persist state by key across a session.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Module 3.7 (Array Methods) complete.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Students can persist a small piece of state across a reload — the pattern Module 6.5 (Save and Load) builds games on, and the reason §3.8 exists at all (the original 2026-08-07 audit found <code>JSON.parse</code> used 11 times downstream, <code>JSON.stringify</code> 3 times, and key-value storage 3 times, none taught anywhere before this section).</p>\n\n<h2>Numbered Lesson List</h2>\n\n<p>New module, 2026-09-13. §3.8 has <strong>6</strong> numbered book subsections (3.8.1–3.8.6) — the</p>\n<p>first pass of this proposal missed <code>&lt;h3&gt;</code>-level sub-concepts and undercounted; corrected</p>\n<p>after checking. Real additional atomic content: \"Readable output\" and \"What Does Not</p>\n<p>Survive\" under §3.8.2, \"A Round Trip\" under §3.8.3, \"The Whole Pattern\" under §3.8.5, and</p>\n<p>§3.8.6 \"Why There Is No <code>open()</code> Here\" is its own numbered subsection, not a footnote.</p>\n<p>Sized at 24 lessons on that corrected structure.</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Slot</th><th>Type</th><th>Title</th><th>Slug</th><th>Notes</th></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8.1</td><td>slides</td><td>3.8.1 Slides: Saving and Loading Data</td><td>`3-8-1-slides`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8.2</td><td>reading</td><td>3.8.2 Reading: Data Has to Outlive the Program</td><td>`3-8-2-reading-data-outlives-program`</td><td>book §3.8.1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8.3</td><td>example</td><td>3.8.3 Worked Example: Readable Output vs. What Doesn't Survive</td><td>`3-8-3-example-readable-vs-lost`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8.4</td><td>reading</td><td>3.8.4 Reading: `JSON.stringify` — Object to Text</td><td>`3-8-4-reading-json-stringify`</td><td>book §3.8.2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8.5</td><td>reading</td><td>3.8.5 Reading: Readable Output</td><td>`3-8-5-reading-readable-output`</td><td>JSON as human-readable text</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8.6</td><td>reading</td><td>3.8.6 Reading: What Does Not Survive</td><td>`3-8-6-reading-what-does-not-survive`</td><td>functions, `undefined`, lost in translation</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8.7</td><td>lab</td><td>3.8.7 Lab: Stringify an Object and Spot What Got Dropped</td><td>`3-8-7-lab-stringify-spot-drops`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8.8</td><td>reading</td><td>3.8.8 Reading: `JSON.parse` — Text Back to Object</td><td>`3-8-8-reading-json-parse`</td><td>book §3.8.3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8.9</td><td>example</td><td>3.8.9 Worked Example: A Round Trip</td><td>`3-8-9-example-round-trip`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8.10</td><td>lab</td><td>3.8.10 Lab: Round-Trip an Object Through JSON</td><td>`3-8-10-lab-round-trip-object`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8.11</td><td>lab</td><td>3.8.11 Lab: Round-Trip an Array of Objects</td><td>`3-8-11-lab-round-trip-array`</td><td>game-state shape, not just a flat object</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8.12</td><td>reading</td><td>3.8.12 Reading: Parsing Can Fail</td><td>`3-8-12-reading-parsing-can-fail`</td><td>book §3.8.4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8.13</td><td>example</td><td>3.8.13 Worked Example: `try`/`catch` Around a Bad Parse</td><td>`3-8-13-example-try-catch-parse`</td><td>ties to §2.5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8.14</td><td>lab</td><td>3.8.14 Lab: Handle a Malformed JSON String</td><td>`3-8-14-lab-malformed-json`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8.15</td><td>reading</td><td>3.8.15 Reading: Saving by Key</td><td>`3-8-15-reading-saving-by-key`</td><td>book §3.8.5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8.16</td><td>example</td><td>3.8.16 Worked Example: The Whole Pattern</td><td>`3-8-16-example-whole-pattern`</td><td>key, stringify, store</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8.17</td><td>lab</td><td>3.8.17 Chart the Code: Save/Load Round Trip End to End</td><td>`3-8-17-chart-save-load-round-trip`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8.18</td><td>lab</td><td>3.8.18 Lab: Save a State Object by Key</td><td>`3-8-18-lab-save-by-key`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8.19</td><td>lab</td><td>3.8.19 Lab: Load and Restore a Saved State</td><td>`3-8-19-lab-load-restore`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8.20</td><td>reading</td><td>3.8.20 Reading: Why There Is No `open()` Here</td><td>`3-8-20-reading-no-open`</td><td>book §3.8.6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8.21</td><td>example</td><td>3.8.21 Worked Example: What the Browser Sandbox Actually Allows</td><td>`3-8-21-example-browser-sandbox`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8.22</td><td>lab</td><td>3.8.22 Lab: Saving-and-Loading Capstone</td><td>`3-8-22-lab-capstone`</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8.23</td><td>lab</td><td>3.8.23 Lab: Adapt the Pattern to a List</td><td>`3-8-23-lab-adapt-to-list`</td><td>not just a single object</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8.24</td><td>assignment</td><td>3.8.24 Saving and Loading Data</td><td>`3-8-24-saving-loading-quiz`</td><td>capstone/unit quiz</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Note</h2>\n<p>Book §3.8 Saving and Loading Data. Currently <strong>0 lessons</strong>; target <strong>24</strong> per the</p>\n<p>2026-09-13 chapter-3 density re-audit (revised up from an initial 20 after the h3-level</p>\n<p>recount above).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Still missing before this is buildable</strong>: Videos/Readings tables with per-reading</p>\n<p>content guidance, full Worked Example code, Day Plan, Assignment pass-criteria,</p>\n<p>Vocabulary table, and Teacher Notes (<code>sub-module-spec-conventions.md</code> §2, items 7–15).</p>\n"},"3.9":{"title":"Chapter 3 Group Performance Assessment","html":"\n<h1>Module 3.9: Chapter 3 Group Performance Assessment</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 1 · Week 13 · Mon Nov 02, 2026 · One meeting · Paired · Environment: browser DevTools console</strong></p>\n\n<p>The third Group PA of the year and the last one before the Print Shop. Same shape as</p>\n<p>1.6 and 2.6 — one block, in pairs, three thirds: <strong>design, build, demo</strong>, with the</p>\n<p>design third first and neither partner opening an editor until the chart is green</p>\n<p>(Appendix D).</p>\n\n<h2>Why this module is numbered 3.9</h2>\n\n<p>1.6/1.7 and 2.6/2.7 sit after their chapters' five book sections. **Chapter 3 has</p>\n<p>eight<strong> (§3.1–3.8), so its assessments are </strong>3.9<strong> (this) and </strong>3.10** (the test).</p>\n<p>Nothing else changes: the assessment modules are still \"the two after the content\",</p>\n<p>which is what the numbering has always meant.</p>\n\n<p>Two things keyed on the literal <code>.7</code> and had to be told:</p>\n\n<p>- <code>scripts/check-summative-parts.mjs</code> matched test units with `/^(\\d+)\\.7 Chapter \\1</p>\n<p>  Individual Performance Assessment$/<code>. Against </code>3.10` that is simply false, so the</p>\n<p>  check would have reported \"ok\" while examining none of 3.10's five parts — the exact</p>\n<p>  failure it was written for. The section number is now <code>\\d+</code>; the chapter</p>\n<p>  backreference and the two literal words still keep teaching modules (3.7 Array</p>\n<p>  Methods, 6.7 Advanced Input) and the Group PAs out.</p>\n<p>- Sorting. <code>lib/curriculum.ts</code> orders modules and lessons with</p>\n<p>  <code>localeCompare(…, { numeric: true })</code> and matches a lesson to its module with</p>\n<p>  <code>numberedId.startsWith(moduleId + '.')</code>, so <code>3.10</code> sorts after <code>3.9</code> and does not</p>\n<p>  collide with <code>3.1</code>. Both were already correct; neither had been exercised past <code>.9</code>.</p>\n\n<h2>What 2.6 delivered and this one changes</h2>\n\n<p>2.6's program was one console script top to bottom with a loop in the middle, and its</p>\n<p>own text said functions, arrays and objects were Chapter 3's. They arrive here. Three</p>\n<p>things change and nothing else about the shape does:</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th></th><th>2.6 (Sep 28)</th><th>3.9 (Nov 2)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>The program's shape</td><td>one straight script with a loop</td><td>**named functions** called from a short main section</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The data</td><td>loose variables; iteration from a counter or `word[i]`</td><td>**an array of objects**, walked directly</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chart shapes in play</td><td>+ the loop-setup hexagon (§2.2)</td><td>+ the **double-rail subroutine** (§3.1)</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>The double-rail is the headline. 2.6's note said the hexagon problem \"will bite again on</p>\n<p>every chapter from here — §3.1's double-rail has the same problem.\" This is that</p>\n<p>chapter; see <strong>The checks on 3.9.1</strong> below.</p>\n\n<h2>What it assesses</h2>\n\n<p>All eight sections, once each, through one artifact:</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Section</th><th>Where it shows up</th></tr>\n<tr><td>3.1 Functions: Definition and Calls</td><td>The program is a set of named functions called from a short main section, not one long script</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.2 Parameters and Return Values</td><td>Every function takes what it needs and **returns** a value; nothing does its work by printing (3.2.13)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.3 Arrays</td><td>The data is a list; a `for`/`for…of` loop walks it (3.3.5) and `push` builds a new one (3.3.2)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.4 Function Expressions and Arrow Functions</td><td>The callback handed to `.map()` is an arrow (3.4.12)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.5 Objects and Properties</td><td>Each record is an object literal read with dot notation (3.5.3)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.6 Functions: Pass by Value/Reference</td><td>One function is **required to mutate** what it was handed, and the demo has to say why the caller sees it (3.6.2)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.7 Array Methods</td><td>One transformation with `.map()`, one copy with spread (3.7.14)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3.8 Saving and Loading Data</td><td>Save and load through `JSON.stringify`/`JSON.parse` under a key (3.8.15), with the failure case handled (3.8.12)</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Weights</h2>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Third</th><th>Weight</th><th>Lesson</th></tr>\n<tr><td>**Design** — the flowchart, drawn together, all checks green</td><td>25%</td><td>3.9.1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**Build** — the program, documented to the style guide</td><td>50%</td><td>3.9.2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>**Demo** — run it, test it, explain it</td><td>25%</td><td>3.9.3</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>Unchanged from 1.6 and 2.6 on purpose. The in-app lessons are green-to-advance like</p>\n<p>every other lesson in the course; the 25/50/25 is how the three artifacts are weighted</p>\n<p>in the gradebook, not something the checker computes.</p>\n\n<blockquote>**Do not mark these lessons `summative`.** The chapter *test* (3.10) sets the flag on</blockquote>\n<blockquote>all five of its parts; the Group PA deliberately does not, and 1.6's and 2.6's</blockquote>\n<blockquote>lessons set it nowhere. Green-to-advance is the whole mechanism here — 3.9.1's chart</blockquote>\n<blockquote>gates 3.9.2, and `summative` exists precisely to switch that gating off.</blockquote>\n\n<h2>Timing for one 1.75-hour block</h2>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Minutes</th><th>What</th></tr>\n<tr><td>0–8</td><td>Pairs form, pick one problem from the menu of four, read the brief</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8–33</td><td>**Design.** Contracts and pseudocode on paper, then the chart in 3.9.1. Editors stay closed.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>33–75</td><td>**Build.** 3.9.2. One screen, partners swap the keyboard at least once.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>75–95</td><td>**Demo — Gallery Walk.** Two waves (8 hosts / 7 visitors), ~3 min per station; teacher circulates with the rubric and samples 4–5 live.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>95–105</td><td>Slack.</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>Identical to 2.6's clock. The build is not bigger in minutes even though the program is</p>\n<p>bigger in lines, and that is deliberate: the five functions are each three to six lines,</p>\n<p>and what takes the time is deciding what goes in and what comes out — which is the</p>\n<p>design third's job, not the build's.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The design third gains a step 2.6 did not have: the contract table.</strong> Before any</p>\n<p>shape is drawn, both partners write what each function <em>takes in</em> and <em>gives back</em>. It</p>\n<p>is the §3.2 vocabulary made to do work, and it is the single best predictor of whether</p>\n<p>the build goes anywhere. A pair that cannot say what <code>totalValue</code> returns will write a</p>\n<p>function that prints — the 3.2.13 error, and the most common failure in this chapter.</p>\n\n<h2>Why the flowchart is 25% and still comes first</h2>\n\n<p>Same reason as 1.6 and 2.6, one turn harder. In Chapter 2 the disagreement a pair had</p>\n<p>to settle on paper was <em>what happens next</em> and <em>how many times</em>. Here it is **who owns</p>\n<p>which step**: a chart with a double-rail on it is a claim that some named function does</p>\n<p>that job, and two students who have not agreed on the function list cannot draw one.</p>\n\n<p>A chart drawn after the code reads exactly like one and is graded as one (Appendix D</p>\n<p>§D.7).</p>\n\n<h2>What this deliberately does not ask for</h2>\n\n<p><strong>No classes, no <code>this</code>, no constructors.</strong> Those are §5.3 and §5.4. The program is</p>\n<p>functions over plain objects.</p>\n\n<p><strong>No <code>.filter()</code>, no <code>.reduce()</code>, no <code>.forEach()</code>, no <code>.sort()</code>.</strong> This is the</p>\n<p>constraint that shapes the whole build, and it is easy to get backwards. **§3.7 teaches</p>\n<p>exactly four tools: <code>.map()</code>, <code>.slice()</code>, <code>.concat()</code> and spread.** 3.7.19 \"What Else Is</p>\n<p>Out There\" <em>names</em> the others so students recognise them in someone else's code and says</p>\n<p>in as many words that the book does not teach them. So:</p>\n\n<p>- <strong>Selecting a subset is hand-written</strong>: an empty array, a loop, and <code>push</code> (3.3.2,</p>\n<p>  3.3.5). The chapter's own worked example does exactly this — 3.3.13 \"Keeping Only the</p>\n<p>  Passing Scores\" — and 3.3.14 makes a lab of it.</p>\n<p>- <strong>Totalling is hand-written</strong>: an accumulator set before the loop and added to inside</p>\n<p>  it (3.3.9 \"Sum an Array\").</p>\n<p>- *<em><code>.map()</code> is the one that </em>is<em> taught</em>*, so the one-line-per-record transformation is</p>\n<p>  where a pair should reach for it rather than looping.</p>\n\n<p><code>curriculum-plan.md</code>'s Ch 3 Test blueprint says \"<code>.map</code>/<code>.filter</code>/<code>.slice</code> on a supplied</p>\n<p>array\" and is <strong>wrong about <code>.filter</code></strong> — the same plan-versus-shipped divergence 2.6</p>\n<p>records for A2.2.0. <code>curriculum/modules/3.7_array-methods.md</code> states the shipped</p>\n<p>position outright. Assess what shipped.</p>\n\n<p>A pair that writes <code>.reduce()</code> anyway is not cheating — 3.7.19 told them it exists. See</p>\n<p>the rubric: behaviour credit yes, technique credit no.</p>\n\n<p><strong>No connector circles, no comment brackets.</strong> Released at §4.1 (Appendix D §D.2).</p>\n\n<h2>The checks on 3.9.1, and why they are not 2.6.1's</h2>\n\n<p>2.6 worked out that <code>min-process</code> counts <code>shape === 'process'</code> and nothing else</p>\n<p>(<code>lib/diagram-check.ts</code>), so a hexagon and a parallelogram are not rectangles and a</p>\n<p>Chapter 2 floor of 3 punished the better chart. **The double-rail is the same kind of</p>\n<p>shape and lands the same way** — <code>subroutine</code> is not <code>process</code> either — so a Chapter 3</p>\n<p>chart that names its functions properly spends <em>more</em> of its shapes on non-rectangles</p>\n<p>than a Chapter 2 one did.</p>\n\n<p>Surveying this chapter's own charts:</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Where</th><th>`min-process`</th><th>`min-decisions`</th><th>`min-nodes`</th></tr>\n<tr><td>`3-1-14` Chart What You Built</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`3-3-11` chart a loop over an array</td><td>—</td><td>1</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`3-4-20` full syntax-forms comparison</td><td>3</td><td>2</td><td>8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`3-7-5` `.map()`'s callback trace</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`3-8-17` save/load round trip end to end</td><td>—</td><td>2</td><td>**10**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`2-6-1` / `2-7-4` the Chapter 2 pair</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>8</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p><strong>What 3.9.1 carries</strong>, sitting a notch above 3.8.17 because it is a whole program</p>\n<p>rather than one round trip:</p>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Rule</th><th>Value</th><th>Why</th></tr>\n<tr><td>`min-nodes`</td><td>**10**</td><td>3.8.17, the chapter's biggest chart, is 10. A PA chart is two terminals + load + loop setup + a decision + the body + two or three double-rails + a report</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`min-process`</td><td>**2**</td><td>2.6's value, unchanged. Keeps the rectangle from vanishing entirely without punishing the double-rail or the hexagon</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`min-decisions`</td><td>1</td><td>Unchanged from 2.6.1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>the other eight</td><td>keep</td><td>Unchanged</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>Eleven rules, the same set as 2.6.1, with <code>min-nodes</code> raised 8 → 10.</p>\n\n<p><strong>And say so in the lesson text.</strong> 2.6.1 had to be made to say \"the checker counts</p>\n<p>rectangles — a parallelogram is not one\"; the Chapter 3 version adds the double-rail to</p>\n<p>that sentence. A rule a student cannot see is a trap whether or not it was meant as one.</p>\n\n<h2>The menu</h2>\n\n<p>Four problems, matched in difficulty, all the same shape: **an array of record objects;</p>\n<p>five functions over it — build one record, total the list, select a subset, transform it</p>\n<p>to one line per record, and change one record in place — then save and load the list.**</p>\n<p>Full statements are in 3.9.1.</p>\n\n<p>1. <strong>Snack Shack</strong> — <code>{ name, price, qty }</code>. Total the inventory's value; list what is</p>\n<p>   below a reorder level; one receipt line per item; sell one unit of a named item</p>\n<p>   (mutates); save and reload the stock.</p>\n<p>2. <strong>Lap Times</strong> — <code>{ name, laps, bestLap }</code>. Total every lap logged; list who is above</p>\n<p>   a target time; one result line per runner; log one more lap for a named runner</p>\n<p>   (mutates); save and reload the log.</p>\n<p>3. <strong>Seed Tray</strong> — <code>{ variety, planted, sprouted }</code>. Total seeds planted; list the</p>\n<p>   varieties under a sprout rate; one label line per variety; record one more sprout for</p>\n<p>   a named variety (mutates); save and reload the tray.</p>\n<p>4. <strong>Library Cart</strong> — <code>{ title, copies, dueInDays }</code>. Total copies on the cart; list</p>\n<p>   what is due within a limit; one spine line per title; check out one copy of a named</p>\n<p>   title (mutates); save and reload the cart.</p>\n\n<p>Pairs at adjacent tables should not pick the same one.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Every one of the four has one function that must mutate and one that must not</strong>, and</p>\n<p>that pairing is the point rather than a detail. <code>sellItem</code> (or its equivalent) is handed</p>\n<p>the list and has to change it where the caller can see — §3.6.2. The record-building</p>\n<p>function has to hand back something new without touching its input — §3.7.14. A pair</p>\n<p>that copies in the first and mutates in the second has written a program that runs and</p>\n<p>is wrong in the way this chapter is about.</p>\n\n<h2>The checklist's honest limits</h2>\n\n<p>Eleven token checks verify that Chapter 3's tools are <strong>present</strong>, never that the</p>\n<p>program is right. The 2.6 loop measured this the hard way: an advanced student built a</p>\n<p>15-line program that satisfied every check and solved nothing. The same is available</p>\n<p>here — a <code>.map()</code> over a one-element array and a <code>push</code> into a list nobody reads would</p>\n<p>go green.</p>\n\n<p>The demo writeup is the defence. 3.9.3's Part 2 (three test cases, one landing on the</p>\n<p>other side of the comparison) and Part 5 (name where each of the eight sections appears)</p>\n<p>are where a paper program shows what it actually does. **Read those before you read the</p>\n<p>code.**</p>\n\n<p>One check is worth more than the others and cannot be gamed by accident: the §3.8 round</p>\n<p>trip needs <code>JSON.stringify</code>, <code>JSON.parse</code> and <code>localStorage</code> all present. A pair that</p>\n<p>saves without parsing back, or parses a key that was never written, fails the demo's</p>\n<p>test-case part even when the checklist is green.</p>\n\n<h2>Teacher notes</h2>\n\n<p>- <strong>Circulate during the design third, not the build third.</strong> Unchanged from 1.6 and</p>\n<p>  2.6, and still the note that matters most. What to listen for here is different: the</p>\n<p>  question is no longer \"where does your loop end\" but **\"what does that function give</p>\n<p>  back?\"** A pair who answer \"it prints it\" have the 3.2.13 error and thirty minutes to</p>\n<p>  discover it the hard way.</p>\n<p>- <strong>A chart with no double-rail is the failure to look for, and no check will catch it.</strong></p>\n<p>  Appendix D §D.3 lets an assignment add only <code>min-decisions</code>, <code>min-process</code>,</p>\n<p>  <code>min-nodes</code> and <code>no-self-loop</code>; there is no floor that can require a subroutine shape,</p>\n<p>  exactly as there is none for the hexagon. A pair can go eleven-for-eleven green on a</p>\n<p>  chart that never names a function. Read the shapes yourself.</p>\n<p>- <strong>The one failure mode is still a silent partner.</strong> The demo writeup asks who drove</p>\n<p>  which third by name. Read that answer before you read the program.</p>\n<p>- <strong>A pair that finishes early has not finished.</strong> Two questions, both cheap:</p>\n<p>  - *\"Call your save, then reload the page, then call your load. Does the list come</p>\n<p>    back?\"* Half the pairs will have written <code>setItem</code> and never read it (3.8.15).</p>\n<p>  - <em>\"What does your mutating function return, and what happens if I ignore it?\"</em> The</p>\n<p>    §3.6 answer is that the change already happened; a pair that has to think about it</p>\n<p>    has found the chapter's real question on their own.</p>\n<p>- <strong>Hand this back as the study guide on Nov 2.</strong> Every item type on the Ch 3 Test two</p>\n<p>  days later has an analogue in the program the pair just built. Same policy as 1.6 → 1.7</p>\n<p>  and 2.6 → 2.7, and it is not a secret. The test's menus use different problems.</p>\n<p>- <strong>Absent-partner policy:</strong> the present member proceeds through all three thirds; the</p>\n<p>  pair artifact and observed contribution are graded. The absent member completes an</p>\n<p>  adjusted-scope solo make-up at a scheduled time; the present partner's grade is never</p>\n<p>  affected by the make-up.</p>\n<p>- <strong>Odd-class trio policy:</strong> one trio is permitted; roles (driver/navigator/recorder)</p>\n<p>  rotate every 20 minutes; they produce one chart, one program, and the demo names all</p>\n<p>  three and who drove which third. The 25/50/25 weights apply to the trio's single</p>\n<p>  artifact.</p>\n\n<h2>Still open</h2>\n\n<p>- **The double-rail cannot be auto-required, and this is the first assessment where that</p>\n<p>  bites.** Flagged in 2.6 as the thing that would come next, and it has. Two ways out:</p>\n<p>  add a <code>min-subroutine</code> floor to the checker alongside the four in Appendix D §D.3, or</p>\n<p>  accept that the function decomposition is graded by eye and say so in the rubric. The</p>\n<p>  rubric currently says so. §4.1 releases two more shapes on top of this.</p>\n<p>- <strong>No graded rehearsal stands behind the double-rail.</strong> §3.1's two charts —</p>\n<p>  <code>3-1-12</code> Chart Before You Refactor and <code>3-1-14</code> Chart What You Built — are where a</p>\n<p>  student would have met it, and neither carries a rule that requires it, so whether a</p>\n<p>  student has ever <em>drawn</em> one before 3.9.1 is unverified. Same structural gap 2.6 found</p>\n<p>  for the hexagon behind A2.2.0, one chapter on. Check one of those two charts before</p>\n<p>  the day, or treat 3.9.1 as first contact and say so in the briefing.</p>\n<p>- <strong><code>min-nodes: 10</code> is a judgement, not a measurement.</strong> It comes from 3.8.17 plus one</p>\n<p>  step for the extra function. If the first run shows pairs padding the chart to clear</p>\n<p>  it, drop it to 9 rather than letting the floor dictate the drawing.</p>\n<p>- <strong>The plan's blueprint names <code>.filter</code>.</strong> Detailed above. Either the plan is corrected</p>\n<p>  or §3.7 grows a <code>.filter</code> lesson; until one of those happens, 3.9 and 3.10 assess the</p>\n<p>  shipped four and this note is the record of the divergence.</p>\n"},"4.1":{"title":"Print Shop: Q1 Synthesis","html":"\n<h1>Module 4.1: Synthesis</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 1 · Week 9 · Environment: browser DevTools console</strong></p>\n\n<h2>SLO Focus</h2>\n<p>- <strong>SLO 3 backup artifact (A9.1 Print Job Manager)</strong>: the first complete design / implement / test program.</p>\n<p>- Reinforces SLO 1, 2, and 4 in a single cohesive project.</p>\n\n<h2>Unit Goal</h2>\n<p>Students pull together everything from Q1: variables, conditionals, loops, functions, and arrays, into one documented, tested program, and reflect on how they built it.</p>\n\n<h2>Lessons in this unit</h2>\n<p>- <strong>Plan before you code</strong>: turn a task into steps before writing code.</p>\n<p>- <strong>Manual testing</strong>: call a function with a known input and check it against an expected result (PASS / FAIL).</p>\n<p>- <strong>Print Job Manager (mini-project)</strong>: the Q1 capstone: store jobs as objects in an array, write functions to process them, and test the key logic.</p>\n<p>- <strong>Q1 Reflection</strong>: reflect on the quarter and connect the build to the software-development life-cycle (SLO 1).</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Units 1.1–1.3 complete: variables, conditionals, loops, functions, and arrays.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Students have shipped one complete, documented, manually-tested program and can describe the design → implement → test process they followed.</p>\n"},"5.1":{"title":"Hello Sprite and Movement","html":"\n<h1>Module 5.1: moSHion Foundations</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 2 · Weeks 10–11 · ~7 contact hours</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Environment:</strong> moSHion in-app editor (built on q5.js + Box2D physics).</p>\n\n<h2>SLO Focus</h2>\n<p>- Reinforces <strong>SLO 3</strong> (design/implement/test in a visual context)</p>\n<p>- Applies Q1 control structures (conditionals, loops) to interactive programs: first time students use their language skills for something that moves on screen</p>\n\n<h2>Unit Goal</h2>\n<p>Students can build a minimum-viable moSHion sketch from scratch: a canvas, a controllable sprite, keyboard input, and basic physics. They understand the frame loop (<code>setup()</code> runs once, <code>draw()</code> runs every frame) and can predict what a <code>vel.x = 4</code> assignment does. By end of W11, the \"wow, I made a thing that moves!\" moment has landed.</p>\n\n<h2>Modules in this unit</h2>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>ID</th><th>Week</th><th>Title</th><th>Primary SLO</th><th>Key Artifact</th></tr>\n<tr><td>[2.1.1](modules/2.1.1_hello-sprite-movement.md)</td><td>10</td><td>Hello Sprite and Movement</td><td>SLO 3</td><td>A10.1 sprite playground</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[2.1.2](modules/2.1.2_physics-feel.md)</td><td>11</td><td>Physics Feel</td><td>SLO 3</td><td>A11.1 pinball scene</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Inter-module bridges</h2>\n<p>- <strong>2.1.1 → 2.1.2:</strong> Keyboard input + velocity from W10 stays; W11 layers physics (gravity, bounciness) on top.</p>\n<p>- <strong>Module 5.1 → Unit 2.2:</strong> By end of W11 students have called <code>new Sprite(...)</code> dozens of times without any formal explanation of classes: deliberate setup for the OOP reveal in W12.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot (entering this unit)</h2>\n<p>- Completed Q1 (variables, types, conditionals, loops, functions, arrays).</p>\n<p>- Comfortable reading and writing ~30-line console.log programs.</p>\n<p>- Can follow camelCase + class style guide.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot (exiting this unit)</h2>\n<p>- Can write a moSHion sketch with canvas, sprite, keyboard input, and basic physics from memory.</p>\n<p>- Uses <code>kb.pressing(...)</code> correctly including the else-to-zero pattern.</p>\n<p>- Understands that <code>vel</code> is pixels-per-frame at ~60 fps.</p>\n<p>- Has instantiated at least 3 objects without being told they're using OOP.</p>\n\n<h2>Unit-level assessments</h2>\n<p>None at unit close: Midterm 3 falls at end of W14 (Unit 2.3).</p>\n"},"5.2":{"title":"Physics Feel","html":"\n<h1>Module 5.2: Physics Feel</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 2 · Environment: moSHion canvas</strong></p>\n\n<h2>SLO Focus</h2>\n<p>- Reinforces <strong>SLO-3</strong> (program design tools and environments) through</p>\n<p>  tuning: change one property, run it, judge the result.</p>\n\n<h2>Module Goal</h2>\n<p>Students give a sprite a physical character: gravity, bounce, friction :</p>\n<p>and learn that these are dials to be tuned by running the thing, not values</p>\n<p>to be calculated.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Module 5.1 (Hello Sprite and Movement) complete. Students can create a</p>\n<p>canvas, make a sprite and set its velocity.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Students can build a closed physical space, drop a body into it, and adjust</p>\n<p>gravity, bounciness and friction to get a feel they chose deliberately.</p>\n\n<h2>Note</h2>\n<p>Book §5.2 Physics Feel. <strong>This module is barely built</strong>: it carries a</p>\n<p>single lesson, <code>moshion-bounce</code>, against a book section that has not been</p>\n<p>spec'd out. <code>BOOK-TO-MODULE.md</code> §4 lists §5.2 as mapped but never built.</p>\n\n<p>This file exists because that one lesson was <strong>orphaned</strong>: its title is</p>\n<p>numbered <code>5.2.1</code>, and until 2026-08-16 there was no module file with</p>\n<p><code>id: \"5.2\"</code>, so <code>listModules()</code> never emitted the module and the lesson</p>\n<p>rendered on no page at all. Anyone could reach it by direct URL and nobody</p>\n<p>could find it.</p>\n\n<p>The right fix is to build §5.2 out properly at module 5.1's density (24</p>\n<p>lessons over its book section). Until someone does, this file at least</p>\n<p>makes the one existing lesson reachable.</p>\n\n<h3>Lesson list</h3>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>#</th><th>Lesson</th><th>Covers</th></tr>\n<tr><td>5.2.1</td><td>`moshion-bounce`</td><td>gravity, four static walls, a dynamic ball, bounciness and friction</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p><strong>No slide deck.</strong> Book §5.2 has no published deck: see</p>\n<p><code>BOOK-TO-MODULE.md</code> §8 for the deck inventory and how to check.</p>\n\n<h3>Authoring note</h3>\n\n<p><code>moshion-bounce</code>'s starter was a commented-out solution (\"Uncomment each</p>\n<p>STEP and fill in the code\"), which meant every graded requirement passed on</p>\n<p>an untouched file. Rewritten 2026-08-16 as a real scaffold: STEP comments</p>\n<p>describing the work, no code to uncomment. See</p>\n<p><code>BOOK-TO-MODULE.md</code> §6, which forbids commented-out solution code.</p>\n"},"5.3":{"title":"Classes and Instances","html":"\n<h1>Module 5.3: Object-Oriented Programming</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 2 · Week 12 · ~3.5 contact hours</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Environment:</strong> moSHion in-app editor.</p>\n\n<h2>SLO Focus</h2>\n<p>- <strong>SLO 2 primary artifact</strong>: A12.2 written OOP-vs-procedural comparison</p>\n<p>- This unit is the dual-enrollment anchor for structured-programming documentation</p>\n\n<h2>Unit Goal</h2>\n<p>Students formally understand what a class is, after two weeks of quietly using <code>new Sprite(...)</code>. They can define their own class, instantiate it, and articulate why OOP is more than \"just another way to organize code.\" A12.2 written comparison becomes the teacher's SLO 2 evidence.</p>\n\n<h2>Modules in this unit</h2>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>ID</th><th>Week</th><th>Title</th><th>Primary SLO</th><th>Key Artifact</th></tr>\n<tr><td>[2.2.1](modules/2.2.1_classes-via-moshion.md)</td><td>12</td><td>Classes and Objects via moSHion</td><td>**SLO 2 primary**</td><td>**A12.2 written (SLO 2 evidence)** + A12.1 Collectible class lab</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Inter-module bridges</h2>\n<p>- <strong>Unit 2.1 → 2.2.1:</strong> Two weeks of using <code>new Sprite(x, y, w, h)</code> without explanation pays off: the reveal lands because students have muscle memory.</p>\n<p>- <strong>2.2.1 → Unit 2.3:</strong> Groups (W13) are a class: students can now inspect <code>Group</code>'s methods knowing what a method means.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>- Comfortable with <code>new Sprite()</code>, <code>sprite.color = ...</code>, and dot access in general.</p>\n<p>- Has seen functions (Q1 W6) and understands parameter passing.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>- Can write a class with constructor + methods from scratch.</p>\n<p>- Can explain what <code>this</code> refers to in a method.</p>\n<p>- Has written a 1-page comparison of procedural vs OOP (A12.2: retained for SLO 2 evidence).</p>\n\n<h2>Unit-level assessments</h2>\n<p>No unit-closing quiz: Midterm 3 at W14 covers Units 2.1–2.3.</p>\n"},"5.4":{"title":"Writing Your Own Classes","html":"\n<h1>Module 5.4: Writing Your Own Classes</h1>\n\n<h2>Module Goal</h2>\n<p>Students write their own class with methods, hold sprites inside it by composition, and judge when a class earns its place over parallel variables.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Module 5.3 (Classes and Instances) complete.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Students design and build a class of their own, and can argue for or against using one.</p>\n\n<h2>Note</h2>\n<p>Book §5.4 Writing Your Own Classes. Currently <strong>21 lessons</strong>.</p>\n\n<p>This module was split out of module 5.3, which covered both book sections under one number on 2026-08-16. The lessons were</p>\n<p>already written and taught this material: they were simply numbered into a</p>\n<p>neighbouring section, so the book's own section never appeared in the app.</p>\n<p>Nothing was rewritten; the lessons were renumbered and their <code>unit</code> label</p>\n<p>corrected.</p>\n\n\n<h3>⚠ Folder numbers do not match title numbers here</h3>\n\n<p>These lessons keep their original folder names, because <code>lesson_state.lesson_id</code></p>\n<p>is the folder name and renaming one orphans student progress. Order comes from</p>\n<p>the number in <code>lesson.json.title</code>. See <code>BOOK-TO-MODULE.md</code> §7.3.</p>\n"},"6.1":{"title":"Groups","html":"\n<h1>Module 6.1: Groups and Overlaps</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 2 · Week 13 · ~3.5 contact hours</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Environment:</strong> moSHion in-app editor.</p>\n\n<h2>SLO Focus</h2>\n<p>- Reinforces <strong>SLO 3</strong> (design/implement/test)</p>\n<p>- Reinforces <strong>SLO 4</strong> (collision detection + spawn logic are algorithms)</p>\n<p>- Reinforces Q1 Arrays topic in a motivating new context</p>\n\n<h2>Module Goal</h2>\n<p>Students level up from \"one sprite\" to \"many sprites managed as a group.\" They understand collision detection as an algorithm and handle spawn/despawn logic safely.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>- Comfortable with classes and <code>new Sprite()</code> from Module 5.3.</p>\n<p>- Remembers Q1 arrays (push, forEach, iteration).</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>- Can create a Group, spawn sprites into it, detect overlaps, and despawn safely.</p>\n<p>- Has shipped the Asteroid Field challenge.</p>\n\n<h2>Note</h2>\n<p>Split out of the former combined \"2.3 Collections and Physics Applications\" spec</p>\n<p>(§6.1 Groups + §6.2 Overlaps and Collisions, per BOOK-TO-MODULE.md's documented</p>\n<p>1:1 book-section rule) during the 2026-08-12 book-native renumber. Content carried</p>\n<p>over unchanged; this file's prose has not yet been independently re-verified</p>\n<p>against the book sections it now represents 1:1: flagged for a follow-up pass.</p>\n"},"6.2":{"title":"Overlaps and Collisions","html":"\n<h1>Module 6.2: Overlaps and Collisions</h1>\n\n<h2>Module Goal</h2>\n<p>Students detect contact between sprites and groups, and remove sprites safely while iterating.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Module 6.1 (Groups) complete.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Students can catch, collect and despawn without hitting the remove-during-iteration bug.</p>\n\n<h2>Note</h2>\n<p>Book §6.2 Overlaps and Collisions. Currently <strong>7 lessons</strong>.</p>\n\n<p>This module was split out of module 6.1, which combined both sections under \"Groups and Overlaps\" on 2026-08-16. The lessons were</p>\n<p>already written and taught this material: they were simply numbered into a</p>\n<p>neighbouring section, so the book's own section never appeared in the app.</p>\n<p>Nothing was rewritten; the lessons were renumbered and their <code>unit</code> label</p>\n<p>corrected.</p>\n\n\n<h3>⚠ Folder numbers do not match title numbers here</h3>\n\n<p>These lessons keep their original folder names, because <code>lesson_state.lesson_id</code></p>\n<p>is the folder name and renaming one orphans student progress. Order comes from</p>\n<p>the number in <code>lesson.json.title</code>. See <code>BOOK-TO-MODULE.md</code> §7.3.</p>\n"},"6.3":{"title":"Physics Applications","html":"\n<h1>Module 6.3: Physics Applications</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 2 · Week 14 · ~3.5 contact hours</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Environment:</strong> moSHion in-app editor.</p>\n\n<h2>SLO Focus</h2>\n<p>- Reinforces <strong>SLO 3</strong> (design/implement/test)</p>\n\n<h2>Module Goal</h2>\n<p>Students apply groups and edge-triggered input to real physics scenarios: jump feel, ground detection, and pendulums: consolidating W13 skills through challenges that preview what their capstone could look like.</p>\n\n<h2>Inter-module bridges</h2>\n<p>- <strong>6.1 → 6.3:</strong> Groups (W13) are used to build level geometry in W14 challenges (platforms, obstacles).</p>\n<p>- <strong>6.3 → 6.4:</strong> Animation &amp; Camera (W15) assumes students can already handle groups of sprites: polish adds on top.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>- Can create a Group, spawn sprites into it, detect overlaps, and despawn safely (Module 6.1).</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>- Understands edge-triggered vs level-triggered input (<code>kb.presses</code> vs <code>kb.pressing</code>).</p>\n<p>- Has shipped at least one complete playable challenge (Space Jumper or Car on a Ramp).</p>\n\n<h2>Note</h2>\n<p>Split out of the former combined \"2.3 Collections and Physics Applications\" spec</p>\n<p>(§6.3 Physics Applications, per BOOK-TO-MODULE.md's documented 1:1 book-section</p>\n<p>rule) during the 2026-08-12 book-native renumber. Content carried over unchanged;</p>\n<p>this file's prose has not yet been independently re-verified against the book</p>\n<p>section it now represents 1:1: flagged for a follow-up pass.</p>\n"},"6.4":{"title":"Animated Sprites and Camera","html":"\n<h1>Module 6.4: Animation &amp; Camera</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 2 · Week 15 · ~3.5 contact hours</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Environment:</strong> moSHion in-app editor.</p>\n\n<h2>SLO Focus</h2>\n<p>- Reinforces <strong>SLO 3</strong> (implement + test polished systems needed for the capstone)</p>\n\n<h2>Unit Goal</h2>\n<p>Students add the two things that visually separate \"my first q5 sketch\" from \"this looks like a real game\": animated sprites and a camera that follows the player. By end of W15 their games feel scaled: the world no longer ends at canvas edge.</p>\n\n<h2>Modules in this unit</h2>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>ID</th><th>Week</th><th>Title</th><th>Primary SLO</th><th>Key Artifact</th></tr>\n<tr><td>[2.4.1](modules/2.4.1_animation-camera.md)</td><td>15</td><td>Animated Sprites and Camera</td><td>SLO 3</td><td>A15.1 Side-Scrolling Platformer challenge</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Inter-module bridges</h2>\n<p>- <strong>Unit 2.3 → 2.4.1:</strong> Groups are used for platforms; platformer challenge needs groups + physics + animation together.</p>\n<p>- <strong>Module 6.4 → Unit 2.5:</strong> The \"game loop has a scene larger than the canvas\" framing sets up the need for game states (menu vs play vs win/lose) in W16.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>- Can create and iterate over groups (2.3.1).</p>\n<p>- Comfortable with physics and input (2.1–2.3).</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>- Can attach multiple animations to a sprite and switch between them.</p>\n<p>- Can implement a camera-follow pattern using <code>camera.x = player.x - canvas.w/2</code>.</p>\n<p>- Has shipped a scrolling platformer with a visible end goal.</p>\n\n<h2>Unit-level assessments</h2>\n<p>None at unit close.</p>\n"},"6.5":{"title":"Save and Load","html":"\n<h1>Module 6.5: Game Saves</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 2 · ~3.5 contact hours</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Environment:</strong> moSHion in-app editor.</p>\n\n<h2>SLO Focus</h2>\n<p>- <strong>SLO 3</strong> primary (File I/O: A16.1 Save System is the SLO-3 artifact)</p>\n<p>- Covers the Butte outline topic <strong>File I/O (secondary location after W8 FileReader)</strong></p>\n\n<h2>Unit Goal</h2>\n<p>Students give their moSHion games the one thing they're missing to feel like real games: data that survives a refresh. The unit walks <code>storeItem</code> / <code>getItem</code>, the type-coercion trap, JSON serialization, save slots, and auto-save / continue patterns. A16.1 ships a complete save system on top of the Unit 2.4 game.</p>\n\n<h2>Modules in this unit</h2>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>ID</th><th>Title</th><th>Primary SLO</th><th>Key Artifact</th></tr>\n<tr><td>[2.5.1](lessons/2.5.1_game-saves.md)</td><td>Game Saves</td><td>SLO 3</td><td>A16.1 Save System (slots + continue + autosave)</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Inter-module bridges</h2>\n<p>- <strong>Unit 2.4 → 2.5.1:</strong> The platformer / side-scroller from 2.4 gets a persistent save layer bolted on.</p>\n<p>- <strong>Module 6.5 → Unit 2.6:</strong> Saves are the data layer; Unit 2.6 (Game States) is the flow layer that decides <em>when</em> to save. A16.2 in 2.6 explicitly integrates the save system from A16.1.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>- Has a Unit 2.4 game (platformer / side-scroller) to bolt persistence onto.</p>\n<p>- Arrays + JSON object-literal familiarity (Q1 fundamentals).</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>- Can write and read single values with <code>storeItem</code> / <code>getItem</code>, including the <code>Number(getItem(...))</code> coercion.</p>\n<p>- Can round-trip a structured object through storage via <code>JSON.stringify</code> / <code>JSON.parse</code>.</p>\n<p>- Can manage multiple save slots (read, write, delete, overwrite).</p>\n<p>- Has a Title screen that detects existing saves and offers New Game / Continue.</p>\n<p>- Has a working auto-save (checkpoint or timer-driven) on the Unit 2.4 game.</p>\n<p>- Knows <code>loadJSON</code> is async and lives in <code>preload()</code>.</p>\n\n<h2>Unit-level assessments</h2>\n<p>None at unit close: A16.1 is the unit's graded artifact.</p>\n"},"6.6":{"title":"Game State Machines","html":"\n<h1>Module 6.6: Game States</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 2 · ~3.5 contact hours</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Environment:</strong> moSHion in-app editor.</p>\n\n<h2>SLO Focus</h2>\n<p>- <strong>SLO 3</strong> primary (structured programming via state machines)</p>\n<p>- Reinforces <strong>SLO 2</strong> (one variable owns flow: composition over scattered booleans)</p>\n\n<h2>Unit Goal</h2>\n<p>Students give their games a <em>shape</em>: title → play → game over → back. The unit introduces the state-machine pattern (<code>let gameState</code>, <code>switch (gameState)</code> in <code>draw</code>, input- and condition-driven transitions) and then integrates it with the save system from Unit 2.5 so a Continue actually restores the right screen. A16.2 ships a 4+ state machine with full save integration.</p>\n\n<h2>Modules in this unit</h2>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>ID</th><th>Title</th><th>Primary SLO</th><th>Key Artifact</th></tr>\n<tr><td>[2.6.1](lessons/2.6.1_game-states.md)</td><td>Game States</td><td>SLO 3</td><td>A16.2 Game States (state machine + save integration)</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Inter-module bridges</h2>\n<p>- <strong>Unit 2.5 → 2.6.1:</strong> A16.2 reuses the save system shipped in A16.1: saves trigger on state transitions; Continue restores the right state.</p>\n<p>- <strong>Module 6.6 → next:</strong> State machines are a structural prerequisite for any later capstone-style work; once a game has both a save layer and a state flow, it's a complete shippable artifact.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>- Module 2.5.1 complete: A16.1 save system is in hand and ready to be wired into state transitions.</p>\n<p>- <code>if</code> / <code>else</code> familiarity from Q1 fundamentals (used as the contrast for <code>switch</code>).</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>- Can declare a state variable (<code>let gameState = 'title'</code>) and drive <code>draw()</code> with <code>switch (gameState)</code>.</p>\n<p>- Can write input-driven transitions (<code>kb.presses(...) → gameState = '...'</code>) inside the right case.</p>\n<p>- Can write condition-driven transitions (score / lives threshold → state change).</p>\n<p>- Has implemented a 4+ state machine (title / play / pause / gameover / win).</p>\n<p>- Can save on state transitions and load into the correct state on Continue.</p>\n\n<h2>Unit-level assessments</h2>\n<p>None at unit close: A16.2 is the unit's graded artifact.</p>\n"},"6.7":{"title":"Advanced Input","html":"\n<h1>Module 6.7: Advanced Input</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 2 · ~2 contact hours</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Environment:</strong> moSHion in-app editor.</p>\n\n<blockquote>**Renumber note:** Was Unit 2.6 before Unit 2.5 was split into 2.5 (Game Saves)</blockquote>\n<blockquote>and 2.6 (Game States); cascaded to 2.7, then to 6.7 with the chapter renumber.</blockquote>\n<blockquote>On 2026-09-01 the joints half was split out into Module 6.8 so the app matches</blockquote>\n<blockquote>book §6.7/§6.8. See §7.3 of `BOOK-TO-MODULE.md`.</blockquote>\n\n<h2>SLO Focus</h2>\n<p>- <strong>SLO 3</strong> primary (implement + debug a complex interactive system)</p>\n\n<h2>Unit Goal</h2>\n<p>Students read the cursor, tell a held button from a one-shot click, drag a sprite</p>\n<p>without fighting the physics engine, and drive two players from one keyboard with</p>\n<p>independent key schemes and independent state. <code>bounciness</code> is taught here because</p>\n<p>the two-paddle example needs a ball that pushes back. Joints, <code>applyForce</code> and the</p>\n<p>slingshot composition are Module 6.8.</p>\n\n<h2>Modules in this unit</h2>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>ID</th><th>Title</th><th>Primary SLO</th><th>Key Artifact</th></tr>\n<tr><td>6.7</td><td>Advanced Input</td><td>SLO 3</td><td>6.7.11 Fix the Broken Chart: Dragging a Sprite</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Inter-module bridges</h2>\n<p>- <strong>Unit 6.6 → 6.7:</strong> state machines are assumed, not retaught; the two-paddle</p>\n<p>  example leaves flow control to the student.</p>\n<p>- <strong>Unit 6.7 → 6.8:</strong> hit-testing (6.7.6) and drag (6.7.9, 6.7.10) are the two</p>\n<p>  halves the slingshot pattern composes with a joint. 6.8 cites them directly.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>- Classes (5.3, 5.4), groups (6.1), animation/camera (6.4), saves (6.5), state</p>\n<p>  machines (6.6).</p>\n<p>- Mouse input and <code>bounciness</code> have NOT been formally taught before this unit.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>- Can read the cursor (<code>mouse.x</code> / <code>mouse.y</code>) and tell held from one-shot</p>\n<p>  (<code>mouse.pressing</code> vs <code>mouse.presses</code>).</p>\n<p>- Can hit-test a sprite against the cursor.</p>\n<p>- Can drag a sprite (snap pos to cursor + zero vel).</p>\n<p>- Can drive two players from one keyboard with two key schemes and two sets of</p>\n<p>  state variables.</p>\n<p>- Can tune push-collision feel with <code>sprite.bounciness</code>.</p>\n\n<h2>Unit-level assessments</h2>\n<p>None at unit close. The graded lab for this material is 6.8.14 Pong-Sumo, which</p>\n<p>also needs a joint and therefore sits at the end of Module 6.8.</p>\n"},"6.8":{"title":"Joints","html":"\n<h1>Module 6.8: Joints</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 2 · ~1.5 contact hours</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Environment:</strong> moSHion in-app editor.</p>\n\n<h2>Module Goal</h2>\n<p>Students connect two sprites with a joint, choose between a distance and a hinge</p>\n<p>joint, remove a joint cleanly, and then compose all three with a directional</p>\n<p>impulse into the slingshot pattern — which is what book §6.8.6 (Impulses and the</p>\n<p>Slingshot Pattern) closes the section on. Most students won't fully master joints;</p>\n<p>aim for \"understand enough to pick one for the capstone.\" <code>SliderJoint</code> is</p>\n<p>challenge-only.</p>\n\n<h2>Modules in this unit</h2>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>ID</th><th>Title</th><th>Primary SLO</th><th>Key Artifact</th></tr>\n<tr><td>6.8</td><td>Joints</td><td>SLO 3</td><td>6.8.14 Two-Player Pong-Sumo (mastery / all-green Submit)</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Inter-module bridges</h2>\n<p>- <strong>Unit 6.7 → 6.8:</strong> the slingshot is drag (6.7.9, 6.7.10) plus hit-testing</p>\n<p>  (6.7.6) plus a joint plus a release impulse. It cannot be taught before 6.7.</p>\n<p>- <strong>Unit 6.6 → 6.8:</strong> Pong-Sumo is structured with the state machine from 6.6</p>\n<p>  (title → play → win → reset).</p>\n<p>- <strong>Unit 6.8 → 7.1 (Capstone):</strong> this is the last content unit; students draft a</p>\n<p>  capstone design doc as evening homework, due Day 1 of Unit 7.1.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>Module 6.7 (Advanced Input) complete. <code>applyForce</code> was introduced in 5.2 and 6.3;</p>\n<p>this unit is where it is taught as an aimed impulse.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>- Can create a <code>DistanceJoint</code> and a <code>HingeJoint</code>, and remove a joint at runtime.</p>\n<p>- Can build a pendulum and a rotating arm.</p>\n<p>- Can apply a directional impulse with <code>applyForce</code> using a vector from sprite A</p>\n<p>  to sprite B.</p>\n<p>- Has implemented the slingshot pattern (joint + drag + release + force).</p>\n<p>- Has shipped a two-player game with separate input schemes, tuned push-collision</p>\n<p>  (<code>bounciness</code>), state-machine flow, and at least one joint.</p>\n<p>- Has a 1–2 page capstone design doc in hand for Day 1 of Unit 7.1.</p>\n\n<h2>Unit-level assessments</h2>\n<p>A17.1 Pong-Sumo (6.8.14). No unit quiz; the capstone (A18.1) begins in Unit 7.1.</p>\n\n<h2>Note</h2>\n<p>Book §6.8 Joints. Currently <strong>15 lessons</strong>.</p>\n\n<p>This module was split out of module 6.7, which combined both book sections under</p>\n<p>\"Advanced Input and Joints\". The split ran in two passes:</p>\n\n<p>- <strong>2026-08-16</strong> — the six joint readings/examples were renumbered 6.8.1–6.8.6.</p>\n<p>- <strong>2026-09-01</strong> — the rest of §6.8 followed: <code>applyForce</code>, the vector-to-target</p>\n<p>  reading, the launch example and the whole slingshot arc were still numbered</p>\n<p>  into 6.7, which sorted them <em>before</em> the joint readings they depend on. The</p>\n<p>  joints challenges lesson and Pong-Sumo (which requires a joint) moved with</p>\n<p>  them. Nothing was rewritten; lessons were renumbered and <code>unit</code> labels</p>\n<p>  corrected.</p>\n\n<h3>⚠ Folder numbers do not match title numbers here</h3>\n\n<p>These lessons keep their original folder names, because <code>lesson_state.lesson_id</code></p>\n<p>is the folder name and renaming one orphans student progress. Order comes from</p>\n<p>the number in <code>lesson.json.title</code>. Every lesson in this module still lives in a</p>\n<p><code>6-7-*</code> folder. See <code>BOOK-TO-MODULE.md</code> §7.3.</p>\n"},"7.1":{"title":"Arcade Cabinet: Q2 Synthesis","html":"\n<h1>Module 7.1: Synthesis (Game Capstone)</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 2 · ~3.5 contact hours in class + homework</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Environment:</strong> moSHion in-app editor.</p>\n\n<blockquote>**Renumber note:** Was Unit 2.7 before Unit 2.5 was split into 2.5 (Game Saves) and 2.6 (Game States). Cascaded to 2.8.</blockquote>\n\n<h2>SLO Focus</h2>\n<p>- <strong>SLO 3 PRIMARY Sem 1 evidence (A18.1 Game Capstone)</strong>: design + implement + test cycle</p>\n<p>- Contributes to <strong>SLO 1</strong> (lived SDLC)</p>\n<p>- Contributes to <strong>SLO 2</strong> (classes + state machines in a shipped product)</p>\n\n<h2>Unit Goal</h2>\n<p>Students ship a complete game that they designed themselves. The unit is entirely build + test + iterate + showcase. This is the Semester 1 closing artifact: the teacher retains design docs, testing logs, and reflections for dual-enrollment documentation.</p>\n\n<h2>Shape of this module</h2>\n\n<p>A performance task, not a guided build. There is no reading, no worked example</p>\n<p>and no starter game: the module is a checklist of twelve things the game must</p>\n<p>contain, drawn from every unit behind it, and nothing at all about what the game</p>\n<p>should be. Two students can tick every box and hand in games with nothing in</p>\n<p>common. That is the point: the checklist measures what they can reach for</p>\n<p>unprompted, which is the one thing a scaffolded project cannot.</p>\n\n<p>The checker is capability-level on purpose. It looks for <em>a</em> class, <em>a</em> Group,</p>\n<p><em>a</em> function that takes a parameter and returns a value, never a particular</p>\n<p>name, because a checker that pins names would quietly turn a design task back</p>\n<p>into a fill-in-the-blanks one.</p>\n\n<h2>Lessons in this module</h2>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>ID</th><th>Title</th><th>Primary SLO</th><th>Key Artifact</th></tr>\n<tr><td>7.1.1</td><td>Arcade Cabinet (Q2 Synthesis): the twelve-item checklist</td><td>**SLO 1, 2, 3 primary**</td><td>**A18.1 Game Capstone (Sem 1 SLO 3 primary evidence)**</td></tr>\n<tr><td>7.1.2</td><td>Q2 Reflection</td><td>SLO 1, 3</td><td>1-page SDLC reflection</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Inter-module bridges</h2>\n<p>- <strong>Modules 5.1–6.8 → 7.1.1:</strong> Every prior moSHion module contributes directly. The checklist <em>requires</em> a custom class (5.4), a Group (6.1), an overlap or collision (6.2), persistent storage (6.5), and three game states (6.6), plus functions, arrays and manual testing carried over from Q1. Animated sprites (6.4) and joints (6.8) sit in the choose-any-two item rather than the required list, since 6.7's own notes say most students will not master joints.</p>\n<p>- <strong>Module 7.1 → Sem 2 (Q3):</strong> None: clean quarter break. Semester 2 starts with a fresh environment (JSCAD).</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>- All of Q2 content (Units 2.1–2.7).</p>\n<p>- A capstone design doc drafted as the prior unit's evening homework.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>- Student has shipped a working game.</p>\n<p>- Student has written design doc, testing log, and 1-page SDLC reflection.</p>\n<p>- Student has presented a 3-minute showcase to the class.</p>\n<p>- Teacher's SLO 3 evidence folder contains the A18.1 artifact.</p>\n\n<h2>Unit-level assessments</h2>\n<p>- <strong>A18.1 Capstone Game (major grade: 15% of course total).</strong></p>\n<p>- <strong>Semester 1 Final exam (scheduled alongside the capstone unit, separate day):</strong> 1.5 hour cumulative coverage of JS fundamentals + moSHion + OOP across all Semester 1 units.</p>\n"},"8.1":{"title":"Solid Shapes","html":"\n<h1>Module 8.1: Solid Shapes</h1>\n\n<p><strong>Quarter 3 · first reSHape module</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Environment:</strong> reSHape in-app modeller (B-rep kernel), Build side first, then reSHape Script.</p>\n\n<h2>Unit Goal</h2>\n<p>Students make their first solid part by hand, discover that the part is a list</p>\n<p>of steps they can go back and change, then read and finally write those steps</p>\n<p>as a program. One new idea per lesson.</p>\n\n<h2>Lessons in this module</h2>\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>ID</th><th>Title</th><th>Mode</th><th>One idea</th></tr>\n<tr><td>8.1.1</td><td>Reading: A Part Is a List of Steps</td><td>reading</td><td>a model is a recipe of steps; mm; Build vs Code</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8.1.2</td><td>Lab: Your First Box</td><td>visual</td><td>Box tool, typing sizes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8.1.3</td><td>Lab: Change Your Mind</td><td>visual</td><td>re-select an earlier step and change a number</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8.1.4</td><td>Lab: Drill a Hole</td><td>visual</td><td>Hole on a face, Across</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8.1.5</td><td>Lab: Round an Edge</td><td>visual</td><td>pick an edge, Round</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8.1.6</td><td>Lab: Hollow It Out</td><td>visual</td><td>Hollow, wall, open face</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8.1.7</td><td>Reading: Build Wrote a Program</td><td>reading</td><td>the script is the timeline as text</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8.1.8</td><td>Lab: Read the Script</td><td>both</td><td>change one number in the text</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8.1.9</td><td>Lab: Write It Yourself</td><td>code</td><td>write box + hole from breadcrumbs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8.1.10</td><td>Lab: Sketch and Pull</td><td>visual</td><td>Rectangle sketch, then Pull</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8.1.11</td><td>Project: Desk Tray</td><td>both</td><td>box + hollow + round, together</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Inter-module bridges</h2>\n<p>- <strong>Modules 1.x → 8.1.8/8.1.9:</strong> variables and function calls carry straight</p>\n<p>  over; <code>const b = box(40, 40, 20)</code> is a call the student has read a hundred of.</p>\n<p>- <strong>Module 8.1 → later reSHape modules:</strong> sketches, patterns, parameters.</p>\n\n<h2>Pre-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>- Units 1-4 (JavaScript fundamentals). No prior CAD.</p>\n\n<h2>Post-requisite snapshot</h2>\n<p>- Student has built, edited, drilled, rounded and hollowed a part by hand.</p>\n<p>- Student can read a reSHape Script and change it, and write a two-line one.</p>\n"}};
